window.FULL_QUIZ = {"lessons":[{"file":"java_foundations_interactive_tutorial.html","code":"00","name":"Java Foundations","sections":[{"title":"1. Your first Java program","teach":"A program is a set of instructions. In these first examples, Java starts an ordinary program at main, and the class is the container that holds that code.","questions":[{"q":"In these lessons, what is a Java program?","options":["A set of instructions written so Java tools can run them","A picture stored on the desktop","Only the words that appear in the console","A note Java ignores"],"answer":0,"explain":"A program is a set of instructions for a computer. Java code is the form those instructions take so the Java tools can run them.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the recipe analogy say a beginner program has in common with a recipe?","options":["It stores a photo of the result","It groups instructions and has a place where the work begins","It is only a title with no steps","The computer invents the steps while it runs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson compares a program to a recipe: a title, instructions, and a starting point. For these examples, Java begins at main.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In the sample programs, what is the job of the class?","options":["It prints a blank line by itself","It can store only numbers","It groups related code","It is the console window"],"answer":2,"explain":"A class is a container for related Java code. In the first example, the class named Main holds main and the statements inside it.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where does a simple Java application normally begin running?","options":["The first // comment","The word System.out","The class's final closing brace","public static void main(String[] args)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Java looks for main as the starting point of an ordinary application. Comments are ignored, and a closing brace only ends a block.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why can a program print text even though the class name is Main?","options":["Printing happens only when an instruction such as println runs","The class name is always sent to the console","Java prints every word it finds in the file","A program displays its file name instead of instructions"],"answer":0,"explain":"A program runs the instructions you wrote. The class name is a container name, not something println prints unless you put that name in a statement.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does this program display?","options":["Hello world without the comma","Hello, world!","Main","Nothing, because the class name is printed instead"],"answer":1,"explain":"println displays the text inside the quotes. The class name Main is not printed by that statement.","kind":"practice","code":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, world!\");\n    }\n}"},{"q":"A program contains only System.out.println(\"Welcome\"); and no other print. What appears in the console?","options":["The name of the class only","Nothing, because quotes hide the text","Welcome","Welcome Main"],"answer":2,"explain":"The one println instruction displays Welcome. Quotes mark the text to print; they are not hidden from the output.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is the instruction that produces console output?","options":["class Main {","public static void main(String[] args) {","// start here","System.out.println(\"Hi\");"],"answer":3,"explain":"println is the instruction that displays text. The class line, the main header, and a comment do not print anything.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How many lines of output does this program produce?","options":["Two","One, because both statements share a line","Three, including the class name","None, until someone types the text"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each println finishes its own line, so Hello and then Java are two lines.","kind":"practice","code":"System.out.println(\"Hello\");\nSystem.out.println(\"Java\");"},{"q":"Which snippet matches the goal \"print a greeting, then print the number 5 on the next line\"?","options":["println(Hello) and println(\"5\")","println(\"Hello\"); then println(5);","println(\"Hello 5\"); as the only statement","print(\"Hello\") with no second statement"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two println calls make two lines. The second value is the number 5, so it is not inside the greeting's quotes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the last line of console output?","options":["Java is fun 5","Main","5","2 + 3"],"answer":2,"explain":"print stays on the same line, so the first line is Java is fun. The next println calculates 2 + 3 and prints 5.","kind":"practice","code":"System.out.print(\"Java \");\nSystem.out.println(\"is fun\");\nSystem.out.println(2 + 3);"},{"q":"What stops this program from compiling?","options":["Hello must not have quotes","main is not allowed to print","The class name has to be lowercase","The println statement is missing its semicolon"],"answer":3,"explain":"A call used as a statement needs a semicolon. The quotes around Hello are correct, and System starts with a capital S.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(\"Hello\")","fix":"System.out.println(\"Hello\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. What does System.out.println mean?","teach":"System is the class, out is the usual place text is sent, and println prints a value and then starts a new line. print stays on the same line.","questions":[{"q":"In System.out.println(\"Hello\"), which piece is the standard output stream?","options":["out","System","println","Hello"],"answer":0,"explain":"System is the class, out is the usual place text is sent, often the console, and println is the method that prints and then starts a new line.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does println do that print does not?","options":["It ignores the value in the parentheses","It starts a new line after writing the value","It can print only numbers","It removes the previous line"],"answer":1,"explain":"print writes and stays on the same line. println writes and then moves to the next line.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the announcement-system analogy in this section?","options":["System is the text inside the quotes","The semicolon chooses the font","System.out is the speaker, and println makes the announcement","println is the computer's power button"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson compares System.out to a speaker system. println is the action of making the announcement, and the text in parentheses is the message.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the semicolon after println mark?","options":["The start of a comment","A new class","The value that will be printed","The end of that Java statement"],"answer":3,"explain":"The semicolon ends the statement. It is not printed, and it is not part of the message.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which part of System.out.println(\"Hi\") is the method that does the printing?","options":["println","System","out","Hi"],"answer":0,"explain":"println is the method. System is the class, out is the stream, and Hi is the argument.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does this pair of statements print?","options":["Nothing, because print must be written as println","RedBlue on the same line","Red on one line and Blue on the next","Blue only"],"answer":1,"explain":"print writes text and stays on the same line, so Blue continues immediately after Red.","kind":"practice","code":"System.out.print(\"Red\");\nSystem.out.print(\"Blue\");"},{"q":"What appears in the console?","options":["Arun only","Age: 15 Name: Arun","Name: Arun, then Age: 15 on the next line","Name: Arun Age: 15 on one line"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first print stays on the line, println(\"Arun\") finishes that line, and the next println starts Age: 15.","kind":"practice","code":"System.out.print(\"Name: \");\nSystem.out.println(\"Arun\");\nSystem.out.println(\"Age: \" + 15);"},{"q":"Which call prints Hello and then moves to a new line?","options":["System.out.print(\"Hello\");","System.println.out(\"Hello\");","out.System.print(\"Hello\");","System.out.println(\"Hello\");"],"answer":3,"explain":"println is the method that prints and starts a new line. The names have to stay in the order System.out.println.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the console output of println(\"Java\") followed by println(2)?","options":["Java on one line, then 2 on the next line","Java 2 on the same line","2 only","Java2 with no line break"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each println finishes its own line. The second call prints the number 2, not more text glued to Java.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which statement prints the words Good morning on one line?","options":["System.out.println(\"Good\" + morning);","System.out.println(\"Good morning\");","System.out.println(Good morning);","System.out.print(\"Good\"); System.out.print(\"morning\");"],"answer":1,"explain":"The words belong inside one pair of quotes if they are a single text message. Without quotes, Java looks for names instead of text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does System.out.println(\"Total: \" + 3) display?","options":["6","Total only","Total: 3","Total:  + 3"],"answer":2,"explain":"When one side of + is text, Java joins the text and the number into one message. It does not add 3 to the word Total.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this statement?","options":["out cannot receive text","Hello must be a number","println has to be written as print","There is no semicolon at the end of the statement"],"answer":3,"explain":"The method call is a statement, so it needs a semicolon. The quotes and the name println are already right.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(\"Hello\")","fix":"System.out.println(\"Hello\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Text, numbers and quotation marks","teach":"Double quotes make a String. A bare number can be used in arithmetic. true and false without quotes are boolean values, not text.","questions":[{"q":"Why can println(\"42\") and println(42) look the same and still be different?","options":["The quoted 42 is text, while the bare 42 is a number that can be used in arithmetic","Java treats them as the same kind of value","Only the quoted form is legal Java","The bare 42 prints the word String"],"answer":0,"explain":"Double quotes make a String. A number written without quotes can be added or compared as a number, even when the printed characters look the same.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do double quotes tell Java?","options":["The console must stay blank","The characters inside are text, not a name or a bare number","The program should skip that line","The value is always a boolean"],"answer":1,"explain":"Quotation marks mark a String. Without them, Java treats a word as a name and a bare 42 as a number.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which of these is a boolean value, not text?","options":["'true'","True","true","\"true\""],"answer":2,"explain":"The boolean literals are the lowercase words true and false. \"true\" is a String, and a capital True is not the boolean literal.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which value is a decimal number in the lesson's simulator?","options":["\"3.14\"","'3.14'","true","3.14 written without quotes"],"answer":3,"explain":"A decimal written without quotes is a number. Quotes would make the same characters text.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the type of a value matter if the console looks the same?","options":["Later calculations, comparisons, and method calls use the type, not just the printed characters","The console font changes the type","Java forgets the type after printing","Only text can be stored in a program"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says the printed output can look identical while the values are different kinds of data. That difference matters as soon as you calculate or compare.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the difference between println(\"20\") and println(21)?","options":["The quoted form prints an extra pair of quote marks","The quotes make 20 text, while 21 is a number","Both are text","Both are numbers, including the quoted 20"],"answer":1,"explain":"Quotes are syntax. They are not printed around the value. They do change 20 from a number into text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does println(\"42\" + 1) display?","options":["42 + 1","An error, because text cannot use +","421","43"],"answer":2,"explain":"The quoted 42 is text, so + joins the character 1 onto that text and produces 421.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does println(42 + 1) display?","options":["421","42 + 1","42","43"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both values are numbers, so + adds them. The console shows 43, not the digits glued together.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which argument makes println display the boolean value rather than the word as text?","options":["true","\"true\"","'true'","True"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lowercase word true, with no quotes, is the boolean value. Quoted true is a String that happens to contain those letters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call prints a whole number that Java could also use in arithmetic?","options":["System.out.println(true);","System.out.println(25);","System.out.println(\"25\");","System.out.println(\"twenty five\");"],"answer":1,"explain":"25 without quotes is a whole number. \"25\" is text, even though it looks numeric, and true is a boolean.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A learner wants the output 3.14 and also wants a numeric decimal. Which call fits?","options":["System.out.println('3.14');","System.out.println(true);","System.out.println(3.14);","System.out.println(\"3.14\");"],"answer":2,"explain":"A decimal without quotes is a number. Quotes would make 3.14 text, and single quotes are not how this lesson writes a decimal.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this attempt to print the text Hello?","options":["println cannot print words","The semicolon is illegal","System.out only accepts numbers","Hello is not inside double quotes, so Java does not treat it as text"],"answer":3,"explain":"Text needs double quotes. Without them, Java looks for a name called Hello instead of printing those letters.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(Hello);","fix":"System.out.println(\"Hello\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Symbols and syntax","teach":"Semicolons end many statements, braces mark a block, parentheses hold inputs or conditions, double quotes mark text, a dot accesses a member, and // starts a comment.","questions":[{"q":"What do curly braces { } mark in the beginner examples?","options":["The start and end of a block of code","A piece of text, like quotation marks","A comment for a person reading the code","The end of one statement, the way a semicolon does"],"answer":0,"explain":"Braces group a block, such as the body of a class, main, or if. A semicolon ends many individual statements.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does // do?","options":["It compares two numbers","It starts a comment. Java ignores the rest of that line","It ends a statement","It prints the words that follow"],"answer":1,"explain":"Comments are notes for people. The compiler does not treat the rest of a // line as an instruction.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do parentheses hold in these early examples?","options":["A comment","The file name","Method inputs or a condition","The name of the class only"],"answer":2,"explain":"println(\"Hello\") puts the input inside parentheses. An if condition is also written in parentheses.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the dot in System.out do?","options":["It ends the statement","It starts a comment","It marks a String","It accesses a member, such as out from System"],"answer":3,"explain":"The dot selects a member. System.out means the out member of System, and another dot reaches println.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which symbol surrounds String text?","options":["Double quotes","Curly braces","A semicolon","//"],"answer":0,"explain":"Double quotes mark text. Braces mark a block, a semicolon ends a statement, and // starts a comment.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which example is a comment rather than an instruction?","options":["{ }","// Print a greeting","System.out.println(\"Hi\");","int age = 14;"],"answer":1,"explain":"The two slashes make the rest of the line a note. Java does not run it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is a complete statement that stores a whole number?","options":["// int age = 14;","age = \"14\"","int age = 14;","int age = 14"],"answer":2,"explain":"The statement has a type, a name, a value, and a semicolon. A comment is ignored, and quotes would make 14 text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Where do the braces belong in this idea: if the age is greater than 10, print Hi?","options":["Around the word if","Inside the quotes with Hi","After the semicolon only","Around the println, as the body of the if"],"answer":3,"explain":"The braces mark the block that runs when the condition is true. The text Hi still needs its own quotes inside println.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which snippet prints Hi and is a finished statement?","options":["System.out.println(\"Hi\");","System.out.println(\"Hi\")","System.out.println(Hi);","// System.out.println(\"Hi\");"],"answer":0,"explain":"The finished statement has quotes around Hi and a semicolon. The comment version is not executed.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does Java do with the words after // on this line?","options":["It treats them as a number","It ignores them","It prints them","It uses them as a class name"],"answer":1,"explain":"A comment is for the reader. It does not become output.","kind":"practice","code":"// Print a greeting\nSystem.out.println(\"Hi\");"},{"q":"Which symbol should end int books = 12?","options":["//","A dot before int","A semicolon","A pair of double quotes around the whole line"],"answer":2,"explain":"The declaration is a statement, so it ends with a semicolon. The number 12 does not take quotes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What stops this statement from compiling?","options":["Hi must not have quotes","println is not a Java method","System has to be written in lowercase","The statement is missing a semicolon"],"answer":3,"explain":"A call used as a statement needs a semicolon at the end. The quotes around Hi are correct.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(\"Hi\")","fix":"System.out.println(\"Hi\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. What are class and main?","teach":"The class groups the code. main is the entry point of a simple application. Each opening brace needs a matching closing brace.","questions":[{"q":"What does class Main declare in the sample?","options":["A class named Main that contains the program's code","A variable named class","The console title","A comment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The class is a named container. Main is the name of that container in the sample, not a value that gets printed.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the sample show two pairs of braces?","options":["The inner pair stores a number","One pair wraps the class and the inner pair wraps main","One pair is a comment and the other is text","Both pairs print blank lines"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first opening brace begins the class. The next begins main. Each needs its own closing brace.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What should a beginner recognise about public static void main(String[] args) for now?","options":["It prints its own keywords","It is optional in every example on this page","It is the standard entry point of a simple application","It is a comment"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson asks you to recognise main as where execution begins, before memorising every keyword.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which description matches the mental model in this section?","options":["main contains the class","The braces are the entry point and main is a comment","The class runs only the final brace","The class is the container, and main is where running starts"],"answer":3,"explain":"The class holds the code. Java starts a simple application at main, which sits inside the class.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which line is the entry point, not the container?","options":["public static void main(String[] args) {","class Main {","}","// class ends"],"answer":0,"explain":"main is where execution begins. class Main begins the container, and a closing brace only ends a block.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How are the braces nested in the sample?","options":["Only main has braces","The main block sits inside the class block","The class block sits inside main","The braces are side by side and neither contains the other"],"answer":1,"explain":"The class opens first, main opens inside it, main closes, and then the class closes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does this program print?","options":["class Main","Nothing, because braces are comments","Hi","Main"],"answer":2,"explain":"The only printing instruction is println(\"Hi\"). The class and main structure organises that instruction.","kind":"practice","code":"class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hi\");\n    }\n}"},{"q":"Which piece of the sample is a comment?","options":["class Main {","System.out.println(\"Hi\");","}","// class begins"],"answer":3,"explain":"Words after // are a note for a person. The class line, the print, and the brace are program structure.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A learner puts println outside main but inside the class. What does the lesson's model say they missed?","options":["The ordinary starting point is main, so that is where the sample instructions belong","println must be written above the class name","A class cannot contain braces","Comments have to be printed first"],"answer":0,"explain":"The samples place the instructions inside main because that is where a simple application starts.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which order matches the sample structure?","options":["close both braces, then declare the class","class, then main, then the print, then close main, then close the class","print, then class, then main","main, then class, then print"],"answer":1,"explain":"The container opens, the entry point opens inside it, the instruction runs there, and the blocks close from the inside outward.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the name of the class in the sample, as distinct from the entry-point method?","options":["println","args","Main","main"],"answer":2,"explain":"Main is the class name. main is the method where running starts. They are different words, and Java is case sensitive.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is missing from this class body?","options":["println should not have quotes","The class keyword must be written as Class","Hi is too short to print","main never closes, and the class never closes, because both closing braces are absent"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each opening brace needs a match. The print itself is a valid statement; the blocks around it are unfinished.","kind":"debug","code":"class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hi\");","fix":"class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hi\");\n    }\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Running code and understanding errors","teach":"The page simulates output. Real Java needs a JDK: write the source, compile it, then run the bytecode. Syntax mistakes are compile errors and appear before normal output.","questions":[{"q":"What does this page's interactive output actually do?","options":["It simulates the idea in the browser. It does not compile Java on your computer","It installs a JDK for you","It edits your .java files","It can only run if a file is already on the desktop"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson uses simulation so you can learn the ideas. Running real Java needs a JDK and a place to write the program.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which tool set compiles Java source and runs the program?","options":["The browser's address bar","The JDK","A pair of double quotes","The // comment"],"answer":1,"explain":"The JDK includes the compiler and the runtime. Quotes and comments are parts of source code, not the tools that run it.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When is a missing quote or a missing brace usually reported?","options":["Only when the computer is offline","Never, because Java ignores punctuation","While the compiler checks the source, before the program runs","Only after the program has printed its output"],"answer":2,"explain":"Syntax mistakes are compile errors. The program does not get as far as normal console output.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the beginner workflow in this section?","options":["Run the source file by renaming it to .class","Compile only after the console has printed","Skip the compiler and edit the bytecode by hand","Write the source, compile it, then run the compiled program"],"answer":3,"explain":"You save source such as Main.java, the compiler checks it and produces bytecode, and the runtime executes that program.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which file is the source you write, in the lesson's example?","options":["Main.java","Main.class as the file you type","A .txt file that the console edits","The JDK installer"],"answer":0,"explain":"The source is the Java code you write, saved in a file such as Main.java. The compiler produces the form that the runtime executes.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A source line is missing a quote. What should you expect?","options":["The error appears only after every println has run","A compile error, before normal console output","The program prints the line with a blank word","The runtime fixes the quote"],"answer":1,"explain":"The compiler reports the syntax problem. This is not a successful run that happens to look odd.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The program compiled, and then a calculation divides by zero. Which description fits the lesson's contrast?","options":["The compiler always catches division by zero as a syntax error","Java ignores the division","The failure happens when the program runs, after compilation succeeded","It is the same kind of problem as a missing brace"],"answer":2,"explain":"A missing brace is a compile error. Division by zero is a problem that shows up when that instruction runs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which order matches a successful first run?","options":["Run, then write the source, then compile","Compile the console output into a .java file","Rename the JDK to Main.java","Save Main.java, compile, then run and read the console"],"answer":3,"explain":"The source is written first. Compiling checks it. Running produces the console output.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does this page still show console-style results without a JDK installed?","options":["The examples are simulations for learning the ideas","The browser includes a full Java compiler","HTML comments are Java bytecode","println works in every website automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says the interactive examples simulate output. The real compile-and-run steps need a JDK.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You fix a missing semicolon and compile again. What changes about the earlier error?","options":["Java keeps reporting the old line forever","That syntax error is gone once the source is valid, and you can go on to run","The semicolon is added to the console output","The JDK deletes the class"],"answer":1,"explain":"A compile error describes the source you just submitted. After the statement is complete, that particular syntax problem is no longer there.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which problem is a compile error rather than a result the program prints?","options":["The number 5 from println(2 + 3)","A blank line after println","A missing closing brace","The text Hello from a successful println"],"answer":2,"explain":"A missing brace is grammar the compiler rejects. Hello and 5 are normal output from a program that compiled.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"This source is supposed to print Hi. Why does it fail before running?","options":["Hi is a boolean","println ran and then deleted the output","The class name must be Hi","The string is missing its closing quote, which is a compile error"],"answer":3,"explain":"The compiler needs the closing quote to know where the text ends. Until the source is valid, there is no normal console output.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(\"Hi);","fix":"System.out.println(\"Hi\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Practice and quick quiz","teach":"Use print and println, quotes, and arithmetic together. print stays on the line; println finishes the line; a bare addition is calculated.","questions":[{"q":"What should you be able to recognise before the next tutorial?","options":["The class and main structure, println, quotes, semicolons, and simple console output","How to build a graphical window","Every library in the JDK","How to sort an array"],"answer":0,"explain":"This practice section checks the building blocks from the page: structure, printing, quotes, and reading simple output.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which statement stays on the same line?","options":["A closing brace","System.out.print","System.out.println","A // comment"],"answer":1,"explain":"print writes without moving to the next line. println writes and then starts a new line.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When does + calculate instead of joining text?","options":["Only inside a comment","When one side is text","When both sides are numbers","Whenever quotes appear anywhere in the file"],"answer":2,"explain":"2 + 3 is arithmetic because both values are numbers. \"Java \" + \"is fun\" joins text.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the point of predicting the output before revealing it?","options":["The page cannot print until you guess","Java changes the answer after a reveal","Comments are executed only if you predict them","To check that you can read the instructions, not only recognise the words"],"answer":3,"explain":"The practice asks you to read what each statement does and then compare that with the shown result.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the console output?","options":["Java is fun on one line, then 5 on the next line","Java is fun, then 23","Java on one line, is fun on the next, then 5","5 only"],"answer":0,"explain":"print writes Java and stays on that line, so is fun continues beside it. println then ends the line. 2 + 3 is arithmetic, so the next line is 5.","kind":"practice","code":"System.out.print(\"Java \");\nSystem.out.println(\"is fun\");\nSystem.out.println(2 + 3);"},{"q":"What does print(\"Red\") followed by print(\"Blue\") display?","options":["Red Blue with a space that Java inserts","RedBlue","Red on one line and Blue on the next","BlueRed"],"answer":1,"explain":"print does not add a line break or an extra space. The second word starts right after the first.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair prints two separate lines?","options":["one println(\"Good morning\")","a comment containing both words","println(\"Good\"); then println(\"morning\");","print(\"Good\"); then print(\"morning\");"],"answer":2,"explain":"Two println calls make two lines. Two print calls stay together. One quoted message is a single line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is printed by println(\"Score: \" + 4 + 1)?","options":["Score: 5","5","Score: 4 + 1","Score: 41"],"answer":3,"explain":"The text is first, so + joins 4 and then joins 1. It does not add 4 and 1 together.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is printed by println(4 + 1)?","options":["5","41","4 + 1","Score: 5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both values are numbers, so + adds them and println shows 5.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which program prints the words on one line and the number 5 on the next line?","options":["println(\"Java is fun 2 + 3\");","print(\"Java \"); println(\"is fun\"); println(2 + 3);","println(\"Java \"); println(\"is fun\"); println(2 + 3);","print(\"Java \"); print(\"is fun\"); print(2 + 3);"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first print stays on the line and println(\"is fun\") finishes it. A second println shows the calculated 5 underneath. Three print calls would stay on one line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which program prints Hello on the first line and 7 on the second?","options":["println(\"Hello 3 + 4\");","println(Hello); then println(\"7\");","println(\"Hello\"); then println(3 + 4);","print(\"Hello\"); then print(3 + 4);"],"answer":2,"explain":"println(\"Hello\") finishes the first line. 3 + 4 is arithmetic, so the next line is 7. print would keep 7 on Hello's line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does this fail to compile?","options":["2 + 3 should be inside quotes","print is not allowed before println","Java cannot print the word Java","The second statement never closes its quote or its parenthesis, and it has no semicolon"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first statement is valid. The second statement is unfinished text, so the compiler stops before any output.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.print(\"Java \");\nSystem.out.println(\"is fun);","fix":"System.out.print(\"Java \");\nSystem.out.println(\"is fun\");","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_variables_data_types_interactive.html","code":"01","name":"Variables and data types","sections":[{"title":"Imagine a labelled storage box","teach":"A variable is a named place that holds one current value. The name is the label, = stores a value, and a later assignment replaces what was there. Java treats score and Score as different names.","questions":[{"q":"What is a variable most like in this lesson?","options":["A labelled box that stores a value","The computer screen","A type of keyboard","A comment beside the code"],"answer":0,"explain":"The label is the name you use in code, and the item inside is the current value. age might hold 18, and name might hold \"Maya\".","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In int age = 18; which word is the label?","options":["the semicolon","age","int","18"],"answer":1,"explain":"int says what kind of value the box holds, age is the name, and 18 is the value placed in the box.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a second assignment do to the value already in a variable?","options":["It creates a second variable with the same name","It turns the value into a comment","It replaces the old value. The variable still holds only one current value","It keeps both values at the same time"],"answer":2,"explain":"A variable stores the latest value. points = 15 after points = 10 leaves 15, not both numbers.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why are score and Score not the same variable?","options":["Java always ignores capital letters","A capital letter makes the variable a boolean","Names may differ only in spaces, not in case","Java is case sensitive, so the capital letter makes a different name"],"answer":3,"explain":"score and Score are two labels. Changing one does not change the other.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the semicolon do in int age = 18;?","options":["It marks the end of the instruction","It prints 18","It creates a second box","It changes 18 into text"],"answer":0,"explain":"The semicolon ends the statement. It is not part of the stored value.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After int age = 18; what does the name age refer to?","options":["Only the semicolon","The labelled storage that currently holds 18","The Java compiler","A comment next to the number"],"answer":1,"explain":"age is the label, and 18 is the value placed in that box.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"int age = 10; then age = 3; What does age hold?","options":["both 10 and 3","10","3","13"],"answer":2,"explain":"The second assignment replaces 10. age does not add the numbers and does not keep the old value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"int score = 4; int Score = 9; What does score hold?","options":["9","13","nothing, because the names conflict","4"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lowercase name and the capital name are different variables. score still holds 4.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line changes what city stores?","options":["city = \"Delhi\";","// city = \"Delhi\";","String city;","The quotes around Delhi by themselves"],"answer":0,"explain":"An assignment writes a new value into the existing variable. A comment does not run, and the type line alone does not change a later value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What do the boxes contain at the end?","options":["name is 21","age is 21 and name is Maya","age is 18 and name is Maya","age is both 18 and 21"],"answer":1,"explain":"The second assignment replaces 18 with 21. name is a different variable and still holds the text Maya.","kind":"practice","code":"int age = 18;\nage = 21;\nString name = \"Maya\";"},{"q":"Which reading of int books = 12; matches the lesson?","options":["12 is the name of the variable","The semicolon is the stored value","int is the kind of value, books is the label, and 12 is placed in the box","books is the type and int is the value"],"answer":2,"explain":"Read it from left to right: type, name, then the value stored by =.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What goes wrong when the code uses score after declaring only Score?","options":["Java combines the two names into one box","A capital letter deletes the value","Assignment always copies into every similar name","score and Score are different names, so score has not been given the value stored in Score"],"answer":3,"explain":"The assignment stores 9 in Score. The later use of score is a different label, so it is not the box that holds 9.","kind":"debug","code":"int Score = 9;\nSystem.out.println(score);","fix":"int score = 9;\nSystem.out.println(score);","debug":true}]},{"title":"Not every value belongs in every box","teach":"A type chooses the container. int is the usual whole number, double is a decimal such as a price, char is one character in single quotes, boolean is true or false, and String is text in double quotes.","questions":[{"q":"Which type is the usual choice for a whole number such as a person's age?","options":["int","String","boolean","char"],"answer":0,"explain":"int stores whole numbers and is the normal choice for an age or a count. String is text, boolean is true or false, and char is one character.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which type fits a price such as 99.50 in this lesson?","options":["char","double","int","boolean"],"answer":1,"explain":"double is the lesson's decimal type. int cannot store the fractional part 0.50.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What can a boolean hold?","options":["Only whole numbers","One character in single quotes","true or false","Any sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"boolean is the container for a truth value. Text belongs in a String, and a single character belongs in a char.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does the lesson tell char from String?","options":["They are the same type","char uses double quotes and String uses none","boolean is written with single quotes","char is one character in single quotes, and String is text in double quotes"],"answer":3,"explain":"A letter such as 'A' fits a char. A name such as \"Maya\" fits a String.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does Java have several numeric types instead of one box for every number?","options":["Different numbers have different sizes and some need a fractional part","So that every variable can hold text and a number together","Because a type is only a comment","So that true and false can be stored in int"],"answer":0,"explain":"byte and short are small whole numbers, int is the usual whole number, long is for very large whole numbers, and float and double store decimals.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which declaration stores a whole-number age?","options":["char age = 18;","int age = 18;","String age = 18;","boolean age = 18;"],"answer":1,"explain":"18 is a whole number, so the usual type is int. String would need quotes, and boolean and char are different kinds of value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration stores the name Maya?","options":["char name = \"Maya\";","int name = \"Maya\";","String name = \"Maya\";","String name = Maya;"],"answer":2,"explain":"Text uses a String and double quotes. Without quotes, Maya is a name, not text. char holds one character, not a whole name.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration stores a single initial?","options":["char initial = \"M\";","String initial = 'M';","boolean initial = 'M';","char initial = 'M';"],"answer":3,"explain":"One character uses char and single quotes. Double quotes make a String even when only one character is inside.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A sensor reading is 19.5. Which type from this lesson fits?","options":["double","int","boolean","char"],"answer":0,"explain":"19.5 has a fractional part, so it needs a decimal type. int would have to drop the fraction.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which value matches boolean finished?","options":["1","true","\"true\"","'T'"],"answer":1,"explain":"The boolean literals are true and false, without quotes. \"true\" is text, and 'T' is a char.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A town's population is a very large whole number, larger than an ordinary int is meant for in this lesson. Which type is the lesson's choice?","options":["boolean","String","long","char"],"answer":2,"explain":"long is the lesson's type for very large whole numbers. char, boolean, and String are not whole-number containers.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this declaration of a score?","options":["int cannot store a score","The name score is too short","The semicolon must come before int","An int is being given text. A whole number should not be inside quotes"],"answer":3,"explain":"Quotes make \"100\" a String. An int needs a numeric value such as 100, without quotes.","kind":"debug","code":"int score = \"100\";","fix":"int score = 100;","debug":true}]},{"title":"Declare it, then give it a value","teach":"The usual pattern is type, name, =, value, and a semicolon. String starts with a capital S, text uses double quotes, and a name should describe the value.","questions":[{"q":"Which order matches the lesson's declaration pattern?","options":["Type, then name, then =, then value, then a semicolon","Value, then type, then name","Name, then semicolon, then type","Quotes, then type, then a comment"],"answer":0,"explain":"String city = \"Chennai\"; is type, name, assignment, value, and the statement ends with a semicolon.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is the type written first?","options":["It creates a second copy of the value","It tells Java what kind of value to expect in that variable","It prints the value","It is a comment for the next line"],"answer":1,"explain":"The type chooses the container before the name labels it and = stores the value.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which type name is spelled with a capital S?","options":["Int","Boolean","String","string"],"answer":2,"explain":"The text type in this lesson is String. The other basic types here, such as int and boolean, start with a lowercase letter.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the job of the name in String city = \"Chennai\";?","options":["It is the value inside the box","It tells Java to ignore the line","It is the semicolon","It is the label you use when you want that stored text"],"answer":3,"explain":"city is the label. \"Chennai\" is the value. The type String says the value is text.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which statement about changing a variable later is true?","options":["You can assign a new value of the same kind without declaring the type again","Every change needs a new type word","A variable can never receive a second value","The second assignment must use a different name"],"answer":0,"explain":"The declaration introduces the name. A later line such as score = 90; replaces the value already stored there.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which statement follows the declaration pattern?","options":["String \"city\" = Chennai;","String city = \"Chennai\";","String city = Chennai;","string city = 'Chennai';"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pattern uses String, a name, =, double-quoted text, and a semicolon.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which statement stores the whole number 12?","options":["String books = 12;","boolean books = 12;","int books = 12;","int books = \"12\";"],"answer":2,"explain":"12 without quotes matches int. Quotes would make text, and boolean does not store a count.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which statement stores the price 99.50?","options":["int price = 99.50;","double price = \"99.50\";","char price = 99.50;","double price = 99.50;"],"answer":3,"explain":"A decimal value belongs in double here, written without quotes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After int score = 100; which later line replaces the stored number with 80?","options":["score = 80;","int score = 80;","String score = 80;","score = \"80\";"],"answer":0,"explain":"The name already exists, so the later assignment does not repeat the type. The new value must still be a whole number.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration stores one character?","options":["String grade = 'A';","char grade = 'A';","char grade = \"A\";","char grade = A;"],"answer":1,"explain":"char uses single quotes around one character. Double quotes belong to String.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration stores a yes-or-no flag?","options":["boolean ready = yes;","int ready = true;","boolean ready = true;","boolean ready = \"true\";"],"answer":2,"explain":"true without quotes is a boolean value. A quoted word would be text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this statement?","options":["A city cannot be stored","The semicolon is not allowed","Chennai has to be a number","The type name string must be written as String, and the text needs double quotes"],"answer":3,"explain":"Java's text type is String with a capital S. Text uses double quotes, not single quotes, in this lesson's pattern.","kind":"debug","code":"string city = 'Chennai';","fix":"String city = \"Chennai\";","debug":true}]},{"title":"Your mini Java code lab","teach":"Read the lines in order. A new assignment replaces the old value. Text stays text, and true or false stays a boolean. The latest value of each name is the one that remains.","questions":[{"q":"What does the lab want you to do before revealing the result?","options":["Read each line in order and predict what each variable holds","Ignore the assignments and print the class name","Add every number in the file together","Treat a comment as a stored value"],"answer":0,"explain":"The program gives each name a value. Later lines can replace an earlier value, so the order matters.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does points = 15 create a second variable named points?","options":["No, because assignment deletes the variable","No. It replaces the value in the variable declared earlier","Yes. Java keeps points for 10 and another points for 15","Yes, but only if the types differ"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lab says assigning a new value changes what that variable stores. It does not create a second box with the same name.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What kind of value is winner in the sample program?","options":["A whole number","A comment","A boolean, true or false","Text, because it is a word in the output"],"answer":2,"explain":"true is a boolean literal. It is not the text \"true\" and it is not a number.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is player able to store Maya while points stores a number?","options":["A variable changes type whenever it is printed","Java stores every value as text","boolean can hold a name and a number together","They are different variables with different types"],"answer":3,"explain":"points is an int, player is a String, and winner is a boolean. Each name has its own container.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do the variables hold after these lines run?","options":["points is 15, player is Maya, and winner is true","points is 10 and winner is a String","points stores both 10 and 15","The program deletes points when it is assigned again"],"answer":0,"explain":"The second assignment replaces 10 with 15. player stores the text Maya, and winner stores the boolean true.","kind":"practice","code":"int points = 10;\npoints = 15;\nString player = \"Maya\";\nboolean winner = true;"},{"q":"points starts at 20 and is then set to 5. What does points hold?","options":["both 20 and 5","5","25","20"],"answer":1,"explain":"The later assignment replaces 20. The variable does not add the two numbers.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does player hold?","options":["15","points","Maya","true"],"answer":2,"explain":"player is assigned the text Maya. The other lines store into different names.","kind":"practice","code":"int points = 10;\npoints = 15;\nString player = \"Maya\";\nboolean winner = true;"},{"q":"After the sample lines, is winner the text \"true\" or the boolean true?","options":["The text \"true\"","The number 1","It is empty because true has no quotes","The boolean true"],"answer":3,"explain":"The literal true has no quotes, so it is a boolean value, which matches the variable's type.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line makes the final value of points differ from its first value?","options":["points = 15;","int points = 10;","String player = \"Maya\";","boolean winner = true;"],"answer":0,"explain":"The declaration stores 10. The later assignment to the same name stores 15. The other lines do not mention points.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What would be a correct boolean declaration for a player who did not win?","options":["int winner = false;","boolean winner = false;","boolean winner = \"false\";","boolean winner = no;"],"answer":1,"explain":"false is the other boolean literal. Quotes would make text, and int is a number type.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"int books = 2; books = books + 3; What does books hold?","options":["23","books + 3 as text","5","2"],"answer":2,"explain":"The right side uses the current value, 2, adds 3, and stores 5 back into books.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with the winner line?","options":["true cannot be stored","The name winner is illegal","A boolean must be a number","winner is a boolean, but \"true\" is text"],"answer":3,"explain":"The type boolean accepts true or false without quotes. Quotes make a String, which does not match this container.","kind":"debug","code":"boolean winner = \"true\";","fix":"boolean winner = true;","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_operators_expressions_interactive.html","code":"02","name":"Operators and expressions","sections":[{"title":"Operators are the action buttons of Java","teach":"An operator is the action in an expression. Arithmetic calculates, comparison asks a true-or-false question, and logic combines those answers. The expression produces one result.","questions":[{"q":"What does an operator do?","options":["It tells Java what action to perform on one or more values","It stores a comment","It names a class","It prints only the first value and skips the action"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the lesson, values are like ingredients and operators are the actions. 8 + 3 uses + as the action.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is an expression?","options":["A class with no statements","Values and operators combined so they produce one result","A comment that Java prints","Only a variable name"],"answer":1,"explain":"int total = 8 + 3; stores the result of the expression 8 + 3. That result is 11.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which kind of operator calculates a number, such as 8 * 3?","options":["A comment","A brace","An arithmetic operator","A comparison operator"],"answer":2,"explain":"Arithmetic operators calculate. Comparison operators ask a question and produce true or false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What kind of result does a comparison such as age \u003e= 18 produce?","options":["The sum of the two numbers","A new class","The text of the operator","A boolean, true or false"],"answer":3,"explain":"A comparison answers a question. It does not itself change the stored age.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In the kitchen analogy, what are 8 and 3 in 8 + 3?","options":["The values the action uses","The operators","Comments","The console"],"answer":0,"explain":"The numbers are the values. + is the operator, and the expression evaluates to 11.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does 8 + 3 evaluate to?","options":["5","11","83","8"],"answer":1,"explain":"The + operator adds the two values. The digits are not glued together.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What kind of action is 8 * 3?","options":["A comment","Text joining","Multiplication","Comparison"],"answer":2,"explain":"The asterisk means multiply. 8 * 3 evaluates to 24.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does 5 == 5 evaluate to?","options":["5","10","yes as text","true"],"answer":3,"explain":"== asks whether the values are equal. The answer is the boolean true, not a number.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does true \u0026\u0026 false evaluate to?","options":["false","true","1","The word and"],"answer":0,"explain":"\u0026\u0026 means both sides must be true. One false side makes the whole expression false.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which expression stores 11 in total?","options":["int total = \"8 + 3\";","int total = 8 + 3;","int total = 8 + 3","// int total = 8 + 3;"],"answer":1,"explain":"The expression calculates 11, and the assignment stores that result. A comment does not run, and quotes would store text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which operator compares two values rather than adding them?","options":["*","The semicolon","\u003c","+"],"answer":2,"explain":"\u003c asks which value is smaller. + and * calculate numbers.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this comparison?","options":["5 cannot be compared","boolean cannot hold a result","Parentheses are comments","A single = assigns a value. A comparison for equality uses =="],"answer":3,"explain":"5 = 5 tries to assign 5 to 5, which is not a comparison. Equality is written ==.","kind":"debug","code":"boolean same = (5 = 5);","fix":"boolean same = (5 == 5);","debug":true}]},{"title":"Arithmetic: Java can do the maths","teach":"Addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, and remainder work on numbers. For two positive ints, / keeps the whole-number quotient and % keeps the leftover.","questions":[{"q":"What does 8 % 3 mean in this lesson?","options":["The leftover after sharing 8 into equal groups of 3, which is 2","8 divided by 3 as a decimal 2.66","83","The larger of 8 and 3"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each group gets 2 and 2 slices remain. The remainder operator reports that leftover.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is special about int division in this lesson?","options":["It adds the numbers instead","The fractional part is dropped. 7 / 2 is 3, not 3.5","It always returns a decimal","It joins the digits"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both values are whole numbers, so / produces a whole-number result. The leftover can be found separately with %.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which operator means multiply?","options":["%","==","*","x"],"answer":2,"explain":"Java uses an asterisk for multiplication. % is remainder, and == compares for equality.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do ++ and -- do to a variable?","options":["They multiply it by itself","They compare it with 1","They turn it into text","They increase or decrease that variable by one"],"answer":3,"explain":"score++ is a short way to make score one larger. score-- makes it one smaller.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does 8 % 3 evaluate to?","options":["2","1","5","2.66"],"answer":0,"explain":"3 goes into 8 twice, with a leftover of 2.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Both values are ints. What is 7 / 2?","options":["72","3","3.5","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integer division drops the fraction. The leftover 1 would come from 7 % 2, not from /.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does 8 * 3 evaluate to?","options":["83","5","24","11"],"answer":2,"explain":"The asterisk multiplies. 8 times 3 is 24.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does 8 - 3 evaluate to?","options":["11","83","2","5"],"answer":3,"explain":"The minus operator finds the difference.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"int score = 4; score++; What does score hold?","options":["5","4","8","41"],"answer":0,"explain":"++ adds one to the current value. It does not glue a digit onto 4.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is 9 % 4?","options":["13","1","2","2.25"],"answer":1,"explain":"4 goes into 9 twice, and the leftover is 1. This is the same idea as the lesson's 9 % 4 question.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which expression finds the leftover when 14 is shared into groups of 3?","options":["14 - 3","14 == 3","14 % 3","14 / 3"],"answer":2,"explain":" / would give the whole-number quotient 4. % gives the leftover 2.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this attempt to multiply?","options":["8 and 3 cannot be multiplied","The semicolon stores the product as text","Parentheses reverse subtraction","The letter x is not the multiplication operator. Java uses *"],"answer":3,"explain":"The arithmetic operators use symbols such as *. The expression should be 8 * 3.","kind":"debug","code":"int area = 8 x 3;","fix":"int area = 8 * 3;","debug":true}]},{"title":"Comparison: ask a question","teach":"A comparison produces true or false and does not change the values it checks. == means equal, != means not equal, and \u003e= means greater than or equal.","questions":[{"q":"What kind of result does age \u003e= 18 produce?","options":["true or false","The age plus 18","A String","The larger number only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The comparison answers the question. In the lesson, age 18 makes age \u003e= 18 true.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does a comparison such as age \u003e= 18 change age?","options":["It deletes the variable","No. It only produces an answer","Yes. It sets age to 18","Yes. It adds 18 to age"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's ticket gate checks a condition. The person's age stays whatever it was.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which operator means not equal?","options":["==","%","!=","="],"answer":2,"explain":"!= asks whether two values are different. == asks whether they are equal, and a single = assigns.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is == used instead of = when asking whether two numbers are equal?","options":["They mean the same thing","= is only for text","== always gives a number","== compares. A single = assigns a value"],"answer":3,"explain":"5 == 5 is true. 5 = 5 is not a comparison.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"int age = 18; What is age \u003e= 18?","options":["true","false","18","36"],"answer":0,"explain":"18 is equal to the boundary, and \u003e= includes equality, so the answer is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does 5 == 5 evaluate to?","options":["5","true","false","10"],"answer":1,"explain":"The two numbers are equal, so the comparison is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does 5 != 3 evaluate to?","options":["2","8","true","false"],"answer":2,"explain":"The values are different, so the not-equal question is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does 3 \u003c 5 evaluate to?","options":["false","3","2","true"],"answer":3,"explain":"3 is smaller than 5, so the comparison is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does 5 \u003c= 5 evaluate to?","options":["true","false","0","10"],"answer":0,"explain":"\u003c= includes the equal case, so 5 is less than or equal to 5.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"int age = 16; The gate requires age \u003e= 18. What is the comparison's result?","options":["18","false","true","16"],"answer":1,"explain":"16 is below 18, so the answer is false. The comparison does not change age to 18.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which expression asks whether tickets is different from 0?","options":["tickets % 0","tickets + 0","tickets != 0","tickets = 0"],"answer":2,"explain":"!= is the not-equal comparison. A single = would assign 0 to tickets.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this gate?","options":["18 cannot be stored in age","Parentheses are illegal around a comparison","boolean cannot store true or false","It uses = where it needs a comparison. \u003e= already includes the equal case and does not assign"],"answer":3,"explain":"age = 18 would try to assign. The lesson's check is age \u003e= 18, which only asks a question.","kind":"debug","code":"boolean canVote = (age = 18);","fix":"boolean canVote = (age \u003e= 18);","debug":true}]},{"title":"Logic and assignment: combine and update","teach":"\u0026\u0026 is true only when both sides are true. || is true when at least one side is true. ! flips a boolean. += updates a variable from its current value.","questions":[{"q":"What does \u0026\u0026 require?","options":["Both conditions must be true","At least one condition must be true","The first condition is ignored","Both conditions become numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"AND is the stricter combination. One false side makes true \u0026\u0026 false false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does || require?","options":["It adds the two values","At least one condition must be true","Both conditions must be true","Both conditions must be false"],"answer":1,"explain":"OR matches the lesson's VIP-pass idea: either accepted reason is enough.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does ! do?","options":["It joins two strings","It assigns zero","It flips a boolean result","It adds one"],"answer":2,"explain":"!true is false, and !false is true.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does score += 5 mean?","options":["Compare score with 5","Store the text score5","Always set score to 5","Replace score with its current value plus 5"],"answer":3,"explain":"+= updates the variable using the value it already holds.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the result of true \u0026\u0026 false?","options":["false","true","0","truefalse"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both sides are not true, so AND is false.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the result of true || false?","options":["The word or","true","false","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"One side is true, so OR is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is !true?","options":["yes","2","false","true"],"answer":2,"explain":"NOT flips the boolean.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"age is 10, the limit is 12, and hasTicket is true. What is age \u003e= 12 \u0026\u0026 hasTicket?","options":["true","12","22","false"],"answer":3,"explain":"The age check is false. AND needs both sides, so the ticket alone is not enough.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Visitor age is 16 and guest is true. What is age \u003e= 18 || guest?","options":["true","false","16","18"],"answer":0,"explain":"The age check is false, but guest is true. OR succeeds when either side is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"int score = 10; score += 5; What does score hold?","options":["105","15","10","5"],"answer":1,"explain":"+= adds 5 to the current 10 and stores 15.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which expression matches \"ticket and tall enough\"?","options":["hasTicket = tallEnough","!hasTicket","hasTicket \u0026\u0026 tallEnough","hasTicket || tallEnough"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's theme-park rule uses AND when both conditions are required. OR would allow either one.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this update?","options":["score cannot be an int","5 must be inside quotes","+= is a comparison","A single = stores 5 and throws away the old score. Adding to the current score uses +="],"answer":3,"explain":"score = 5 replaces the old value with 5. score += 5 makes it 15 when it started at 10.","kind":"debug","code":"int score = 10;\nscore = 5;","fix":"int score = 10;\nscore += 5;","debug":true}]},{"title":"Expressions and the order of operations","teach":"Multiplication and division happen before addition and subtraction. Parentheses happen first. += updates a variable, and int division of positive numbers drops the fraction.","questions":[{"q":"Why is 2 + 3 * 4 equal to 14 rather than 20?","options":["Multiplication happens before addition, so 3 * 4 is 12 and then 2 is added","Java always works strictly left to right and ignores *","4 is stored as text","Parentheses are assumed around 2 + 3"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson multiplies first, then adds. Parentheses are how you force 2 + 3 to happen first.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do parentheses change?","options":["They compare the values","They make the grouped part happen first","They turn numbers into text","They end the statement"],"answer":1,"explain":"(2 + 3) * 4 adds first and gets 20. Without them, the multiplication happens first.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After the apples program adds 4, divides by 3, and takes a remainder, what does integer division mean?","options":["The remainder includes the quotient","Division happens before += ","14 / 3 is 4, and the leftover 2 is a separate remainder","14 / 3 is 4.666 and is stored in an int"],"answer":2,"explain":"The program starts at 10, += makes 14, shared is 4, and leftover is 2.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does enough mean when apples is 14 and the check is apples \u003e= 12?","options":["false, because division already used the apples","12","The text enough","true, because 14 is at least 12"],"answer":3,"explain":"The comparison only answers a question about the current number of apples.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the value of 2 + 3 * 4?","options":["14","20","24","9"],"answer":0,"explain":"3 * 4 is 12, then 2 + 12 is 14.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the value of (2 + 3) * 4?","options":["24","20","14","9"],"answer":1,"explain":"The parentheses add 2 and 3 first. 5 * 4 is 20.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After int apples = 10; apples += 4; what is apples?","options":["40","104","14","6"],"answer":2,"explain":"+= adds 4 to the current value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Using the lesson's apples steps, what is apples / 3 after apples becomes 14?","options":["4.666","2","14","4"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both values are ints, so 14 / 3 is the whole number 4.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is 14 % 3?","options":["2","4","1","12"],"answer":0,"explain":"The remainder of 14 shared into groups of 3 is 2.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is 14 \u003e= 12?","options":["26","true","false","2"],"answer":1,"explain":"14 is greater than 12, so the comparison is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which expression forces addition to happen before multiplication?","options":["2 + 3 + 4","2 * 3 * 4","(2 + 3) * 4","2 + 3 * 4"],"answer":2,"explain":"Parentheses group 2 + 3. The version without them multiplies 3 * 4 first.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The programmer wanted addition first and wrote 2 + 3 * 4 expecting 20. What should change?","options":["Replace * with +","Remove the numbers","Store the expression in a String","Put parentheses around 2 + 3"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without parentheses the multiplication wins. (2 + 3) * 4 evaluates to 20.","kind":"debug","code":"int answer = 2 + 3 * 4;","fix":"int answer = (2 + 3) * 4;","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_conditional_statements_interactive.html","code":"03","name":"Conditional statements","sections":[{"title":"Programs can choose what happens next","teach":"A conditional runs one block or another based on a true-or-false condition, or on a matching value. The condition sits in parentheses, and the matching statements sit in braces.","questions":[{"q":"What does a conditional statement do?","options":["It runs different code depending on a condition or a matching value","It repeats one line a fixed number of times","It stores every value in an array","It declares a class"],"answer":0,"explain":"The weather example takes an umbrella only when it is raining. Otherwise it takes the other path.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where is the condition written?","options":["In the class name","Inside parentheses after if","Inside a comment","After the closing brace"],"answer":1,"explain":"Java checks the parenthesised condition and then runs the matching block.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do the braces after if contain?","options":["A value that is always printed","The else keyword","The statements that run when that branch is chosen","The condition itself"],"answer":2,"explain":"The block is the work for that path. The condition stays in the parentheses.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In the raining example, when does the else block run?","options":["When isRaining is true","Before the condition is checked","On every run, after the if block","When isRaining is false"],"answer":3,"explain":"else is the other path. If the condition is true, the if block runs and the else block does not.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"isRaining is true. Which message prints?","options":["Take an umbrella!","Enjoy the weather!","Both messages","Neither message"],"answer":0,"explain":"The condition is true, so Java runs the if block and skips else.","kind":"practice","code":"boolean isRaining = true;\nif (isRaining) {\n    System.out.println(\"Take an umbrella!\");\n} else {\n    System.out.println(\"Enjoy the weather!\");\n}"},{"q":"isRaining is false. Which message prints?","options":["true","Enjoy the weather!","Take an umbrella!","Both messages"],"answer":1,"explain":"A false condition skips the if block and runs the else block.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which snippet checks a boolean and has two paths?","options":["// if it rains","println(isRaining)","if (isRaining) { ... } else { ... }","isRaining = true;"],"answer":2,"explain":"The if and else are the decision. An assignment only stores a value, and a comment does not choose a path.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the condition isRaining rather than the text \"isRaining\"?","options":["Quotes make the condition faster","A boolean must be written in quotes","The condition has to be a number","isRaining is the boolean being tested. Quotes would make text"],"answer":3,"explain":"The variable holds true or false. The quoted word would be a String, not that flag.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What runs when the if condition is true and there is no else?","options":["Only the if block","The if block and a hidden else","Nothing","Every later line in the file, inside the if"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without else, a true condition runs the if block. A false condition skips that block and continues after it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which piece of the weather program is the decision rather than the message?","options":["boolean isRaining = true;","if (isRaining)","System.out.println(\"Take an umbrella!\");","System.out.println(\"Enjoy the weather!\");"],"answer":1,"explain":"The if line chooses a path. The println lines are the messages on those paths, and the first line only stores the flag.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A program should print Inside only when the condition is true. Which shape matches?","options":["// if (condition) println(\"Inside\");","if { condition }","if (condition) { println(\"Inside\"); }","println(\"Inside\"); if (condition) { }"],"answer":2,"explain":"The message belongs in the block that the condition guards. A comment is not a decision, and the condition belongs in parentheses.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this decision?","options":["Java cannot test a boolean","println must come before if","true has to be a number","The else message is not inside a block that belongs to else, so the braces do not group the two paths correctly"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each path needs its own block. The else message must be the body of else, not left outside the decision.","kind":"debug","code":"if (isRaining)\n    System.out.println(\"Take an umbrella!\");\nSystem.out.println(\"Enjoy the weather!\");","fix":"if (isRaining) {\n    System.out.println(\"Take an umbrella!\");\n} else {\n    System.out.println(\"Enjoy the weather!\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"if and else : two possible paths","teach":"if runs when the condition is true. else runs when it is false. The cinema gate uses age \u003e= minimumAge, with the comparison written correctly rather than as an assignment.","questions":[{"q":"When does the if block run?","options":["When its condition is true","Every time, regardless of the condition","Only when the condition is false","Only when there is no else"],"answer":0,"explain":"if is the true path. else handles the other case.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the else path for?","options":["A comment","The case when the if condition is false","A second true condition","Code that always runs after the if block"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ticket checker has two paths: enter, or visit the counter. The condition picks one.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which comparison matches the cinema gate?","options":["age == \"minimumAge\"","minimumAge \u003e= age, with the same meaning in every case","age \u003e= minimumAge","age = minimumAge"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says to write age \u003e= minimumAge. A single = would assign instead of compare.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the gate print when the age is below the minimum?","options":["Welcome in!","Both messages","The minimum age as a number","Not quite yet."],"answer":3,"explain":"The else path is the failure path. Welcome in! belongs to the true path.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"age is 20 and minimumAge is 18. Which path runs?","options":["Welcome in!","Not quite yet.","Both messages","Neither, because \u003e= excludes equality"],"answer":0,"explain":"20 \u003e= 18 is true, so the if block runs. \u003e= would also accept an age equal to the minimum.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"age is 15 and minimumAge is 18. Which message prints?","options":["18","Not quite yet.","Welcome in!","15"],"answer":1,"explain":"15 \u003e= 18 is false, so the else block runs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"age is 18 and minimumAge is 18. Which path runs?","options":["Both paths","No path, because equal values are an error","Welcome in!, because \u003e= includes the equal age","Not quite yet., because the age must be greater"],"answer":2,"explain":"The boundary age passes the check. \u003e without the equal sign would reject it, but this gate uses \u003e=.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which code is the lesson's ticket gate?","options":["if (age = minimumAge) { welcome }","if (minimumAge) { welcome }","age \u003e= minimumAge;","if (age \u003e= minimumAge) { welcome } else { wait }"],"answer":3,"explain":"The condition is a comparison in parentheses, with a block for each path.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is there only one message when the program runs?","options":["if and else are alternatives. Java runs one of them","println always prints both strings","else runs only as a comment","The condition is stored and both blocks are skipped"],"answer":0,"explain":"A two-path decision selects one branch.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"minimumAge is 12 and age is 12. What is the condition age \u003e= minimumAge?","options":["0","true","false","12"],"answer":1,"explain":"Equal to the minimum still satisfies \u003e=.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which mistake assigns instead of testing the gate?","options":["age \u003e minimumAge","age == minimumAge","age = minimumAge","age \u003e= minimumAge"],"answer":2,"explain":"One equals sign stores a value. The gate needs a comparison such as \u003e= or ==.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does this gate do wrong?","options":["The \u003e= comparison is illegal","Welcome in must be a number","Braces cannot follow if","The else message is outside the decision, so it prints even when the visitor is allowed in"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without braces and an else block, the second println is an ordinary statement and runs after a one-line if.","kind":"debug","code":"if (age \u003e= minimumAge)\n    System.out.println(\"Welcome in!\");\nSystem.out.println(\"Not quite yet.\");","fix":"if (age \u003e= minimumAge) {\n    System.out.println(\"Welcome in!\");\n} else {\n    System.out.println(\"Not quite yet.\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"else if : more than two choices","teach":"Java checks an else-if chain from top to bottom and runs the first matching branch. Grades: 90 A, 80 B, 70 C, 60 D, otherwise Needs more practice.","questions":[{"q":"How does an else-if chain choose a branch?","options":["It tests from top to bottom and runs the first condition that is true","It runs every true branch","It always runs the final else","It picks the last matching condition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Order matters. A later branch is considered only when the earlier conditions were false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What grade does the lesson give to a score of 90 or more?","options":["Needs more practice","A","B","C"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first test is score \u003e= 90. A score that passes it never reaches the lower branches.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why would putting score \u003e= 60 above score \u003e= 90 be a problem?","options":["The chain would refuse to compile","Every score would become A","A high score would match 60 first and never be considered for A","Java would sort the conditions automatically"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first true test wins. A 95 is also \u003e= 60, so a too-early low boundary steals the higher grades.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which grade matches a score that fails 90, 80, and 70, but passes 60?","options":["A","C","Needs more practice","D"],"answer":3,"explain":"D is the branch for score \u003e= 60 after the higher boundaries have failed. Below 60, the final else says Needs more practice.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What grade is 84?","options":["B","A","C","D"],"answer":0,"explain":"84 fails \u003e= 90 and passes \u003e= 80, so the chain stops at B.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What grade is 90?","options":["Needs more practice","A","B","C"],"answer":1,"explain":"90 passes the first test, score \u003e= 90.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What grade is 70?","options":["D","A","C","B"],"answer":2,"explain":"70 fails 90 and 80, then passes \u003e= 70.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What grade is 60?","options":["C","Needs more practice","A","D"],"answer":3,"explain":"60 fails the higher tests and passes \u003e= 60.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What grade is 59?","options":["Needs more practice","D","C","B"],"answer":0,"explain":"59 fails every numbered boundary, including \u003e= 60, so the final else runs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A score is 95, but the first test written is score \u003e= 80. Which grade does that chain assign?","options":["Needs more practice","B, because 95 matches the first true test and the A test is never reached","A, because 95 is still at least 90","Both A and B"],"answer":1,"explain":"Top to bottom means the first success wins, even if a later condition would also have been true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which structure matches the grade calculator?","options":["a loop that adds the score","five independent if statements that can all run","if, then else if for each lower boundary, then else","one if with no other branches"],"answer":2,"explain":"else if ties the tests into one chain so only one grade is chosen.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this grade decision?","options":["90 is too large for an int","else if cannot follow if","A score cannot be compared with \u003e=","The assignment to B is outside the if, so B is stored even when the score is an A"],"answer":3,"explain":"The B assignment must live in its own else-if branch. Outside the chain, it runs for every score that reaches it.","kind":"debug","code":"if (score \u003e= 90) {\n    grade = \"A\";\n}\ngrade = \"B\";","fix":"if (score \u003e= 90) {\n    grade = \"A\";\n} else if (score \u003e= 80) {\n    grade = \"B\";\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"switch : choose from known options","teach":"switch matches one value to known cases. In the vending machine, 1 is Water, 2 is Juice, 3 is Milk, and any other value is the default Other choice. break stops a case from continuing into the next one.","questions":[{"q":"When is switch a natural fit?","options":["When one value is matched against specific known options, such as a menu number","When you need a range such as score \u003e= 80","When you need to repeat a block","When you are declaring a variable"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vending machine matches 1, 2, or 3. Ranges and combined conditions fit if better.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the default branch for?","options":["A comment","A value that matches none of the cases","The first case","A case that always runs as well as another case"],"answer":1,"explain":"Choice 9 is not 1, 2, or 3, so the lesson's default prints Other choice.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does break do in a case?","options":["It compares the value again","It stores the menu number","It stops the switch from continuing into the next case","It ends the program"],"answer":2,"explain":"Without break, execution can fall into the following case and run that code too.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which case prints Juice?","options":["case 1","case 3","default","case 2"],"answer":3,"explain":"The machine's menu is 1 Water, 2 Juice, and 3 Milk.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"choice is 2. What is printed?","options":["Juice","Water","Milk","Other choice"],"answer":0,"explain":"case 2 matches Juice, and break keeps the later cases from running.","kind":"practice","code":"switch (choice) {\n    case 1: System.out.println(\"Water\"); break;\n    case 2: System.out.println(\"Juice\"); break;\n    case 3: System.out.println(\"Milk\"); break;\n    default: System.out.println(\"Other choice\");\n}"},{"q":"choice is 9. What is printed?","options":["Milk","Other choice","Water","Juice"],"answer":1,"explain":"9 matches no case, so default runs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"choice is 1. What is printed?","options":["Milk","Other choice","Water","Juice"],"answer":2,"explain":"case 1 is Water.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"choice is 3. What is printed?","options":["Water","Juice","Other choice","Milk"],"answer":3,"explain":"case 3 is Milk.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which tool fits a meal chosen by menu option 1, 2, or 3?","options":["switch","an else-if range starting at 90","a loop from 1 to 3 that always prints every meal","a comment listing the meals"],"answer":0,"explain":"The options are specific known values, which is the lesson's switch situation.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which tool fits assigning a grade from score ranges?","options":["default only","if and else if","switch on every possible score","a single case with no condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ranges such as \u003e= 90 are comparisons. switch is for matching one value to listed cases.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What happens if case 1 prints Water and has no break before case 2?","options":["case 2 is skipped forever","The switch does not compile because break is optional decoration","Execution can continue into case 2 and print Juice as well","Java inserts a break automatically"],"answer":2,"explain":"break is what separates the cases in the lesson's machine. Leaving it out lets control fall through.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can this menu print more than Water when choice is 1?","options":["println cannot be used in a switch","1 is not a legal case label","default always runs before the cases","case 1 has no break, so control falls through into later cases"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each case in the lesson ends with break so only the matching drink is printed.","kind":"debug","code":"switch (choice) {\n    case 1:\n        System.out.println(\"Water\");\n    case 2:\n        System.out.println(\"Juice\");\n        break;\n}","fix":"switch (choice) {\n    case 1:\n        System.out.println(\"Water\");\n        break;\n    case 2:\n        System.out.println(\"Juice\");\n        break;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"When should you use if or switch ?","teach":"Use if or else if for ranges and combined conditions. Use switch when one expression is matched against specific known values. A logged-in admin check is a combination; a menu number is a switch.","questions":[{"q":"Which decision fits if better than switch?","options":["A range, a comparison of different variables, or two conditions combined","A menu option that is exactly 1, 2, or 3","A day number matched to one case","A single known command code"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson reserves switch for matching one expression to listed values, and if for ranges and combinations.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which decision fits switch better than a chain of ranges?","options":["Check that a user is logged in and is an admin","Show a meal based on menu option 1, 2, or 3","Check whether a person is old enough to vote","Assign a grade from score ranges"],"answer":1,"explain":"The meal is one number compared with specific cases. The other tasks are ranges or combined booleans.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the useful rule from this section?","options":["Always use if, even for menu numbers","Use whichever keyword appears first in the file","if for ranges and combined tests, switch for one value against known cases","Always use switch because it is shorter"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both control flow, but each matches a different kind of decision.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is \"logged in and admin\" an if?","options":["It is a single menu number","It needs a default case","It is a loop","It combines two conditions, which \u0026\u0026 can express"],"answer":3,"explain":"switch does not combine two different booleans the way \u0026\u0026 does. The lesson points this task at if.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A person can vote when age \u003e= 18. Which tool does the lesson recommend?","options":["if","switch with a case for every age","default","A comment"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is a range check, not a list of individual ages.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Print a message for day number 1, 2, or 3. Which tool fits?","options":["assignment day = 1","switch","else if (day \u003e= 90)","a boolean named day with no cases"],"answer":1,"explain":"A specific day number is a known option, like the vending cases.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The lab uses age 20 and menu choice 2. What does the sample print?","options":["Adult ticket only","Menu item unavailable","Adult ticket, then Idli","Child ticket, then Dosa"],"answer":2,"explain":"20 \u003e= 18 chooses the adult message. case 2 prints Idli.","kind":"practice","code":"int age = 20;\nint menuChoice = 2;\nif (age \u003e= 18) {\n    System.out.println(\"Adult ticket\");\n} else {\n    System.out.println(\"Child ticket\");\n}\nswitch (menuChoice) {\n    case 1: System.out.println(\"Dosa\"); break;\n    case 2: System.out.println(\"Idli\"); break;\n    default: System.out.println(\"Menu item unavailable\");\n}"},{"q":"age is 12 and menuChoice is 9 in that same program shape. What is printed?","options":["Adult ticket, then Idli","Child ticket, then Dosa","Adult ticket, then Menu item unavailable","Child ticket, then Menu item unavailable"],"answer":3,"explain":"12 fails age \u003e= 18. 9 matches default, not case 1 or case 2.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is the range test in the lab?","options":["if (age \u003e= 18)","case 2:","switch (menuChoice)","break;"],"answer":0,"explain":"The age rule is a comparison. The menu is a switch.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is a specific match rather than a range?","options":["score \u003e= 80","case 2:","age \u003e= 18","age \u003c 18"],"answer":1,"explain":"case 2 matches one known value. The age and score tests are ranges.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need both \"logged in\" and \"admin\". Which expression fits the if?","options":["switch (loggedIn)","case admin:","loggedIn \u0026\u0026 admin","loggedIn || admin, if both are required"],"answer":2,"explain":"AND requires both. OR would allow either condition alone, which is a different rule.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"choice is 1, but the program prints both Dosa and Idli. What is missing?","options":["A second switch around case 2","An else after default","Quotes around the number 1","A break at the end of case 1"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without break, case 1 falls through into case 2. Each matching case should end before the next one starts.","kind":"debug","code":"switch (menuChoice) {\n    case 1:\n        System.out.println(\"Dosa\");\n    case 2:\n        System.out.println(\"Idli\");\n        break;\n}","fix":"switch (menuChoice) {\n    case 1:\n        System.out.println(\"Dosa\");\n        break;\n    case 2:\n        System.out.println(\"Idli\");\n        break;\n}","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_loops_interactive.html","code":"04","name":"Loops","sections":[{"title":"Why do we need loops?","teach":"A loop repeats an instruction with a start, a condition for continuing, and an update. That is shorter and safer than copying the same statement once per repetition.","questions":[{"q":"What problem does a loop solve?","options":["Repeating the same kind of instruction without copying it for every repetition","Choosing between two paths","Storing a single value","Declaring a class"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sweeping analogy repeats one action until the floor is clean, instead of a separate instruction for every square.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What three parts does every loop plan need?","options":["A switch and a default","Where it starts, when it should continue, and how it moves toward stopping","A class, a file name, and a comment","Only a print statement"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without an update that moves toward the stopping rule, a condition that starts true can stay true.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is the five-println version worse than a loop for printing 1 through 5?","options":["A loop cannot print numbers","The five statements run in reverse","The repeated code is long and has to be edited in every copy","Java cannot print numbers outside a loop"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson shows one compact for instruction in place of five copied println lines.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the difference between a loop and an if?","options":["They are the same statement","if always repeats and a loop never does","A loop cannot contain a condition","A loop can repeat. An if chooses a path once"],"answer":3,"explain":"if selects a branch. A loop returns to its body while its plan says to continue.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which code prints 1 through 5 without five separate println lines?","options":["for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 5; i++) println(i);","println(1); println(2); println(3); println(4); println(5);","if (i \u003c= 5) println(i);","switch (5)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The for loop is the compact plan: start at 1, continue while i is at most 5, and add one after each pass.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the body of a counting loop do?","options":["The file name","The work that should happen on each repetition","The rule that creates the class","A comment Java executes twice"],"answer":1,"explain":"The body is the repeated instruction. The header says how the repetition is controlled.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need the numbers 1 through 20. Why is a loop the lesson's tool?","options":["A boolean can store all 20 numbers","Comments can repeat themselves","The count is known, and one instruction can cover every number","Each number needs its own class"],"answer":2,"explain":"The choosing-a-loop section uses exactly this scenario for a for loop.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What would go wrong if the repeated step never changed the value being tested?","options":["Java would run the body once and then stop","The condition would become a String","The loop would skip its body automatically","The stopping condition might stay true and the loop might never end"],"answer":3,"explain":"The water lesson says that removing water++ leaves the condition true, so the loop never stops.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which is a loop plan rather than a copy of the same line?","options":["Start at 1, continue while the counter is small enough, then increase the counter","println(1) written five times","one if with no repetition","a variable that never changes and is never tested"],"answer":0,"explain":"The plan names the start, the continuing test, and the update.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Printing a greeting once is not a loop. Which extra idea makes it a loop?","options":["Adding a semicolon","A rule for repeating that greeting until a stop condition is reached","Changing println to print","Putting the greeting in quotes"],"answer":1,"explain":"One print is one instruction. A loop adds repetition and a reason to stop.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The without-loop sample uses five println calls for 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. What does the loop version avoid?","options":["Using a condition","Starting from 1","Editing five copies when the repeated instruction changes","Printing numbers"],"answer":2,"explain":"Repeated code is harder to maintain. The loop keeps the instruction in one place.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"This was meant to print 1, 2, and 3, but the counter never changes. What is missing?","options":["A second println","Quotes around i","A class named Loop","An update such as i++ so the condition can eventually become false"],"answer":3,"explain":"The condition i \u003c= 3 stays true if i stays 1. The body needs to move i toward the stop.","kind":"debug","code":"int i = 1;\nwhile (i \u003c= 3) {\n    System.out.println(i);\n}","fix":"int i = 1;\nwhile (i \u003c= 3) {\n    System.out.println(i);\n    i++;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"The for loop: repeat a known number of times","teach":"A for loop fits a known count. The header initialises the counter, tests it, and updates it. for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 5; i++) visits 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5.","questions":[{"q":"When is a for loop especially convenient?","options":["When you know how many repetitions you need, or a counter controls the loop","When the body must run before any test","When you may need zero runs and you are waiting on input","When you are choosing one menu case"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's example is counting laps when the number of laps is known.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What are the three parts of for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 5; i++)?","options":["Three separate loops","Initialise i to 1, continue while i \u003c= 5, then increase i by one","Only a condition","A class, a method, and a field"],"answer":1,"explain":"The header collects the start, the test, and the update in one place.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When is the update i++ applied?","options":["Instead of the body","Only when the condition is false","After the body runs","Before i is created"],"answer":2,"explain":"The body uses the current i, then the update moves i, then the condition is checked again.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does i \u003c= 5 mean in the counting loop?","options":["Stop before printing 5","Print only 5","Skip odd numbers","Keep going while the counter is 5 or less"],"answer":3,"explain":"The condition includes 5, so the loop prints 1 through 5.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How many numbers does for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 5; i++) print?","options":["5","4","6","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"i takes the values 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. The next value, 6, fails i \u003c= 5.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the last number printed by for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 4; i++)?","options":["1","4","5","3"],"answer":1,"explain":"The test is \u003c= 4, so 4 is printed and 5 is not.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does for (int i = 1; i \u003c 4; i++) print?","options":["4 only","1 and 4","1, 2, and 3","1, 2, 3, and 4"],"answer":2,"explain":"\u003c stops before 4. \u003c= would have included 4.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header counts from 1 through 20?","options":["for (int i = 20; i \u003c= 1; i++)","for (int i = 1; i \u003c 20; i--)","while (i \u003c= 20) with no start and no update","for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 20; i++)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Start at 1, continue while i is at most 20, and add one each time. i-- would move the wrong way.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which part of the header creates the counter?","options":["int i = 1","i \u003c= 5","i++","the braces"],"answer":0,"explain":"The initialisation creates i and starts it at 1. The middle part tests, and i++ updates.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After the body prints i when i is 5, what happens next in for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 5; i++)?","options":["The loop starts again at 1","i becomes 6, the test fails, and the loop stops","The body prints 5 again","i stays 5 forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"The update runs after the body. 6 is not \u003c= 5, so there is no further pass.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You know a password prompt must appear at least once. Is for the lesson's best description of that job?","options":["Yes, because for cannot count","No, because for has no condition","No. A known numeric count fits for; a body that must run before the test fits do-while","Yes. for always runs before its test"],"answer":2,"explain":"The for header tests as part of a known counter plan. The do-while section is the one that guarantees a first run.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"This was meant to print 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5, but the counter gets smaller. What is the update doing?","options":["i-- prints in reverse once and then stops at 5","The test i \u003c= 5 excludes 1","println cannot print i","i-- moves away from the stopping condition i \u003c= 5, so the loop does not advance through 1 to 5"],"answer":3,"explain":"Counting upward needs i++. i-- makes the counter 0, -1, and so on, while i \u003c= 5 stays true.","kind":"debug","code":"for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 5; i--) {\n    System.out.println(i);\n}","fix":"for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 5; i++) {\n    System.out.println(i);\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"The while loop: keep going while true","teach":"while tests the condition before the body. If the condition is already false, the body runs zero times. The body must change something the condition depends on.","questions":[{"q":"When does a while loop check its condition?","options":["Before each attempt to run the body","Only after the body, with no check first","It never checks","Only while compiling"],"answer":0,"explain":"The bucket is checked before every pour. If it is already full, there are zero pours.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can a while body run zero times?","options":["Yes, but only when the condition is true","Yes, when the condition is false at the start","No. while always runs once","Only if the body is empty"],"answer":1,"explain":"A false start skips the body. That is the difference from do-while.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What happens if water++ is removed while the condition is water \u003c 3 and water starts at 0?","options":["water becomes 3 immediately","The loop prints nothing and ends","The condition stays true and the loop does not stop","The body runs once"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says the variable must change inside the loop so the condition can become false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which job fits while in the comparison section?","options":["Print the numbers 1 through 20, where the count is known","Display a menu once and then ask","Choose one grade with else if","Keep asking for a password until it is correct"],"answer":3,"explain":"while fits a check before every repetition, including the possibility of no repetition.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"water starts at 0 and the body adds 1 while water \u003c 3. How many pours happen?","options":["3","2","4","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"The body runs for water 0, 1, and 2. water then becomes 3 and the test fails.","kind":"practice","code":"int water = 0;\nwhile (water \u003c 3) {\n    water++;\n}"},{"q":"water starts at 3 and the test is water \u003c 3. How many times does the body run?","options":["It never stops","0","1","3"],"answer":1,"explain":"The condition is false before the first pour, so while does not enter.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is water after the loop that starts at 0 and adds 1 while water \u003c 3?","options":["4","0","3","2"],"answer":2,"explain":"The last successful test is water \u003c 3 when water is 2. The body then makes water 3, and the next test fails.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header checks before the body and can skip it?","options":["do { } while (water \u003c 3)","for with no condition","if (water \u003c 3) else","while (water \u003c 3)"],"answer":3,"explain":"do-while runs first. if chooses once and does not repeat. while tests first and can repeat.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A reading loop should not run when there is already nothing to read. Which behaviour matches while?","options":["Skip the body when the starting condition is false","Run the body once anyway","Run the body forever","Skip the test"],"answer":0,"explain":"The pre-check is why while can represent \"do this only while the condition still holds.\"","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which loop shape adds one on each pass and stops at the target?","options":["for water = target","int water = 0; while (water \u003c target) water++;","int water = target; while (water \u003c target) water++;","water \u003c target;"],"answer":1,"explain":"Starting below the target lets the body run. Starting at the target makes the first test false.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the password loop a while rather than a fixed for from 1 to 3?","options":["for cannot count","A password must be a number","You do not know how many attempts are needed; you know the condition to keep going","while cannot test a boolean"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's while scenario is \"keep asking until it is correct,\" which may be soon or may take many tries.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is missing from this bucket loop?","options":["A do keyword before while","A semicolon after the condition only, which would still not fill the bucket by itself","Quotes around water","water++ inside the body. Without it, water \u003c 3 stays true"],"answer":3,"explain":"The condition uses water, so the body has to change water. The empty progress leaves the loop stuck.","kind":"debug","code":"int water = 0;\nwhile (water \u003c 3) {\n    System.out.println(\"Pour\");\n}","fix":"int water = 0;\nwhile (water \u003c 3) {\n    System.out.println(\"Pour\");\n    water++;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"The do while loop: run once before checking","teach":"do-while runs the body first and tests afterwards, so the body happens at least once even when the condition starts false.","questions":[{"q":"What is guaranteed about a do-while body?","options":["It runs at least once, before the condition is tested","It may run zero times","It never tests a condition","It runs only when the condition starts true"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cookie taste happens first. The decision to continue comes after that first run.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does the order differ from while?","options":["while always runs the body first","do-while runs and then checks. while checks and then maybe runs","They check at the same time","do-while has no condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's comparison chart puts the check first for while and the body first for do-while.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"If the condition is false at the start, how many times does do-while run the body?","options":["Forever","Twice, because the check is repeated first","Once","Zero times"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first run happens before the false condition can stop a second run.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which scenario is the lesson's do-while example?","options":["Print 1 through 20 with a known count","Skip the body when input is already valid","Choose a grade","Show a menu once, then ask whether to show it again"],"answer":3,"explain":"A menu that must appear before the user answers fits a body-first loop.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"value starts at 5 and the test is value \u003c 3. How many times does do { value++; } while (value \u003c 3); run the body?","options":["Once","Zero times","Five times","It never stops"],"answer":0,"explain":"The body runs, value becomes 6, and 6 \u003c 3 is false, so it does not run again.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"With the same start and test, how many times does while (value \u003c 3) value++; run?","options":["Twice","Zero times","Once","Five times"],"answer":1,"explain":"while checks first. 5 \u003c 3 is already false, so the body never starts.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After do { value++; } while (value \u003c 3); with value starting at 5, what is value?","options":["3","8","6","5"],"answer":2,"explain":"The single run adds one. The following test fails, so it does not continue.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header shows a menu and only then asks whether to repeat?","options":["while (user wants it again) { show menu }, which might never show it","for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 0; i++)","if (menu)","do { show menu } while (user wants it again);"],"answer":3,"explain":"The body-first shape matches the lesson. A while might skip the menu when the condition starts false.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"value starts at 1 and the loop is do { value++; } while (value \u003c 4); How many times does the body run?","options":["3","1","4","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"It runs at 1, 2, and 3. After the third run value is 4, and 4 \u003c 4 is false.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can do-while print something even though its condition is already false?","options":["The test is a comment","The print is in the body, and the body runs before the test","A false condition is treated as true","println ignores the loop"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first bite happens before the decision. That is the whole point of the cookie analogy.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair of results matches value = 5 and the test value \u003c 3?","options":["Both run 1 time","while runs 1 time and do-while runs 0","while runs 0 times, and do-while runs 1 time","Both run 0 times"],"answer":2,"explain":"The same starting numbers show the order difference: check-first can skip, body-first cannot.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The menu was supposed to appear at least once, but this loop can skip it. What shape was used?","options":["for cannot be replaced","The braces run twice automatically","println is illegal in a loop","while tests first. A do-while is needed when the menu must be shown before the answer"],"answer":3,"explain":"If the condition starts false, while never reaches the menu. do { show } while (again); shows it first.","kind":"debug","code":"while (again) {\n    showMenu();\n}","fix":"do {\n    showMenu();\n} while (again);","debug":true}]},{"title":"Which loop should you choose?","teach":"for fits a known count. while checks before every pass and may run zero times. do-while runs once before the check. break leaves the loop, and continue skips the rest of the current pass.","questions":[{"q":"Which loop prints the numbers 1 through 20 most directly?","options":["for","do-while with an unknown menu","else if","switch"],"answer":0,"explain":"The count is known and a counter controls the loop, which is the lesson's for case.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which loop keeps asking for a password until it is correct, and might not know the number of tries?","options":["a single if","while","for from 1 to 20 only","a switch"],"answer":1,"explain":"The condition is checked before each attempt. The number of repetitions is not a fixed count.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does break do?","options":["It restarts the class","It is a comment","It exits the loop","It skips only the rest of this pass and continues the loop"],"answer":2,"explain":"continue is the tool that skips the rest of the current iteration. break leaves entirely.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does continue do?","options":["It exits the loop","It ends the program","It repeats the current statement without the update","It skips the rest of the current iteration and moves toward the next one"],"answer":3,"explain":"continue does not abandon the loop. It abandons the remainder of this pass.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Display a menu once, then ask whether to display it again. Which loop fits?","options":["do-while","for with a zero count","while that might never enter","a grade chain"],"answer":0,"explain":"The menu must happen before the user can answer, so the body comes first.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Read input until it is valid, and do not read at all if there is nothing waiting. Which behaviour fits?","options":["break before the loop","while, because a false start means zero runs","do-while, because it must run anyway","for from 1 to 1 only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pre-check is the while lesson. do-while would perform the body once even when it should not.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A search loop should stop as soon as the target is found. Which statement leaves the loop?","options":["else","The update i++ by itself, which only moves to the next index","break","continue"],"answer":2,"explain":"break exits. continue would only skip the rest of this pass and keep searching.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A loop should ignore one unwanted value and keep going through the others. Which statement fits?","options":["break","return from main immediately in every case","A missing update","continue"],"answer":3,"explain":"continue moves on to the next iteration. break would stop the whole loop.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header is the known-count choice for 1 through 10?","options":["for (int i = 1; i \u003c= 10; i++)","do { } while (false); once, with no counter","while (false)","if (i \u003c= 10)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ten repetitions are known in advance. That is the for scenario in the comparison section.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The password may already be correct before any prompt. You still want a prompt first. Which loop matches \"ask, then decide\"?","options":["switch","do-while","while","for with i \u003c= 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"Body first means the question is asked even if you might stop immediately afterwards.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Match the lesson's three intentions.","options":["All three are switch cases","while always runs once and for may run zero times with no test","Known count: for. Check first: while. Body at least once: do-while","Known count: do-while. Check first: for. Body once: if"],"answer":2,"explain":"The comparison section assigns each loop a different intention. They can overlap in power, but the structure should say what you mean.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The loop finds the target and should stop, but it keeps scanning because the author used continue. What should replace continue?","options":["another continue","a semicolon after continue","println","break"],"answer":3,"explain":"continue only skips the rest of this pass. break is what leaves the search loop.","kind":"debug","code":"if (scores[i] == target) {\n    continue;\n}","fix":"if (scores[i] == target) {\n    break;\n}","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_methods_interactive_tutorial.html","code":"05","name":"Methods","sections":[{"title":"1. What is a method?","teach":"A method is a named set of instructions you can call whenever that task is needed. Writing it once avoids copying the same lines into many places.","questions":[{"q":"What is a method?","options":["A named set of instructions that performs a particular task","A variable that stores one number","A comment Java repeats","The console window"],"answer":0,"explain":"The coffee-machine analogy gives the steps a button. Calling the method runs those stored instructions.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why prefer a method over copying the same lines into several places?","options":["A method deletes the caller's variables","The instructions live in one place, so a change does not have to be repeated in every copy","Java can only store one println in a file","Copied lines run faster than a call"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson contrasts repeated code with reusable code. sayHello can be called twice without pasting its body twice.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does void say about sayHello in the example?","options":["The method cannot print","The name is optional","The method does not send a value back to the caller","The method returns a String"],"answer":2,"explain":"void means there is no returned result. The method can still print or do other work.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a call such as sayHello() do?","options":["It creates a new class","It is a comment","It compares two strings","It runs the instructions stored under that name"],"answer":3,"explain":"The call uses the name and parentheses. Java then follows the method body.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How many times does this main method run the greeting instructions?","options":["Twice","Once","Zero times, because the body is above main","Four times"],"answer":0,"explain":"There are two calls. Each call runs the body.","kind":"practice","code":"static void sayHello() {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello!\");\n}\nsayHello();\nsayHello();"},{"q":"What does the program print?","options":["Nothing, because void methods cannot print","Hello! on two lines","Hello! once","sayHello"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each call prints Hello! and println ends the line. void does not forbid printing.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is the call, not the definition?","options":["System.out.println(\"Hello!\");","}","sayHello();","static void sayHello() {"],"answer":2,"explain":"A call is the name and parentheses used from main. The static void line starts the definition.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need the same receipt printed from three places. What does the lesson recommend?","options":["Three copies of the println lines","A variable named print","A comment in each place","One method, called from each place"],"answer":3,"explain":"The method stores the instructions once. Each place only needs the call.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which signature matches a greeting that prints and gives nothing back?","options":["static void sayHello()","static int sayHello()","static String sayHello(int name)","void, with no name and no parentheses"],"answer":0,"explain":"void matches \"nothing returned.\" The other forms promise a number or text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Where do the instructions themselves go?","options":["After the final brace of the method","In the method body, between the braces","In the call's semicolon","In a comment above main"],"answer":1,"explain":"The body is the stored recipe. The call only names it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the coffee-machine idea applied to sayHello?","options":["println is optional if the method has a name","A void method returns the drink","Pressing the button runs the stored steps, instead of describing every step at every call","The method grinds coffee at runtime"],"answer":2,"explain":"The analogy is about reuse. The name stands for the whole task.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The call is there, but the greeting method never prints because its body is only a comment. What should the body contain?","options":["Another call to sayHello with no body anywhere","A return of the text Hello from a void method","The word static inside the braces","The println instruction, not a comment describing it"],"answer":3,"explain":"A comment does not run. The reusable instruction has to be real code in the body.","kind":"debug","code":"static void sayHello() {\n    // System.out.println(\"Hello!\");\n}","fix":"static void sayHello() {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello!\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Create and call a method","teach":"A basic method has a return type, a name, parentheses, and a body. A call is the name followed by parentheses. void means nothing is returned. For now, static means you can call it without creating an object.","questions":[{"q":"What does the lesson say to treat static as, for now?","options":["A way to call the method from the class without creating an object","A loop","The returned number","A String literal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Objects come later. static lets main call sayHello directly.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do the parentheses in the definition hold?","options":["The class name","Parameters, the method's inputs. They can be empty","The return type","A comment"],"answer":1,"explain":"sayHello() has empty parentheses because that method needs no input. Parameters would be listed there.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do the braces hold?","options":["A required return value even for void","The file name","The method body, the instructions that run when the method is called","The caller's variables only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The body is inside the braces. A call from main does not repeat those lines.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which naming style does the lesson use for methods?","options":["PascalCase, the lesson's usual style for class names","ALL CAPS","A name with spaces","lower camel case, such as printReceipt or calculateTotal"],"answer":3,"explain":"Class names such as BankAccount use PascalCase later. Method names start with a lowercase letter and capitalise later words.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which line calls the method that prints the greeting?","options":["sayHello();","static void sayHello() {","public static void main(String[] args) {","System.out.println(\"Hello, learner!\");"],"answer":0,"explain":"The call is in main. The println line is inside the method, and the static void line defines it.","kind":"practice","code":"static void sayHello() {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello, learner!\");\n}\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    sayHello();\n}"},{"q":"What is printed when main calls sayHello once?","options":["Nothing, because static methods are skipped","Hello, learner!","sayHello","main"],"answer":1,"explain":"The call runs the body, and the body prints that message.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which piece is the return type of sayHello?","options":["sayHello","main","void","static"],"answer":2,"explain":"void is the return type. static is the modifier the lesson treats as \"call without an object.\"","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call matches a method defined as static void welcome()?","options":["welcome;","void welcome();","static.welcome","welcome();"],"answer":3,"explain":"A call uses the name and parentheses. It does not repeat void or static.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why are the parentheses required on the call even when there are no parameters?","options":["They show that the name is being called","They print a blank line","They declare a variable","They start a comment"],"answer":0,"explain":"sayHello without parentheses would look like a variable. The call needs ().","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Read sayHello from the outside in, as the lesson does. Which order is right?","options":["parentheses, then the class, then void","static, void, the name, parentheses, then the body","the body, then the name, then void","main, then static, then a comment"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is the lesson's reading order for this method.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Where should the call be so that a simple program actually runs the method?","options":["Before the class, outside every method","In the file name","Inside main, the starting point","Inside a comment"],"answer":2,"explain":"main is where the sample starts. A method that is defined but never called does not run its body.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this attempt to call the method?","options":["sayHello cannot be static","Quotes are required around the call","main cannot contain a call","The call repeats void and the body. A call is only the name and parentheses"],"answer":3,"explain":"The definition already has the body. main should contain sayHello();","kind":"debug","code":"public static void main(String[] args) {\n    static void sayHello() {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello\");\n    }\n}","fix":"static void sayHello() {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello\");\n}\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    sayHello();\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Parameters: give a method information","teach":"Parameters are the named inputs in the definition. Arguments are the actual values in the call. greet(\"Anu\") passes the text Anu to the parameter name.","questions":[{"q":"In greet(\"Anu\"), what is \"Anu\"?","options":["The argument, the actual value supplied at the call","The return type","A comment","The method body"],"answer":0,"explain":"The parameter is the name in the definition, such as name. The argument is the value at the call.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is a parameter?","options":["The semicolon after the call","A named input listed in the method definition","The value returned with return","A variable that every method shares automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"greet(String name) says the method expects one String and will call it name inside the body.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the sandwich analogy map to arguments?","options":["The receipt total","The word static","Your choices, such as wheat and paneer, filled in on the order form","The shop's name"],"answer":2,"explain":"The form's fields are parameters. What you choose for those fields are the arguments.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can a method accept more than one parameter?","options":["No. Java allows only one input","Only if the method is void","Only by using a comment","Yes. introduce(String name, int age) accepts a name and an age"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson shows a two-parameter method and a call such as introduce(\"Anu\", 12).","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which call matches introduce(String name, int age)?","options":["introduce(\"Anu\", 12);","introduce(12, \"Anu\");","introduce(\"Anu\");","introduce(name, age) without supplying values, as the only call"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first argument must be the String and the second the int, matching the parameter list.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is printed?","options":["Hello, Ravi","Hello, Anu","Hello, name","Anu"],"answer":1,"explain":"The argument Anu is stored in the parameter name, and the body joins it to the greeting.","kind":"practice","code":"static void greet(String name) {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello, \" + name);\n}\ngreet(\"Anu\");"},{"q":"What does introduce(\"Maya\", 14) print if the body prints name + \" is \" + age?","options":["name is age","Maya14","Maya is 14","14 is Maya"],"answer":2,"explain":"The arguments arrive in parameter order. Text and the number are joined with the word is.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which definition accepts a caller's name and uses it in the print?","options":["static void greet()","static void greet(name)","static String name()","static void greet(String name)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The parameter needs a type and a name. Empty parentheses accept nothing.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"greet(\"Anu\") and greet(\"Ravi\") use the same method. What changes between the calls?","options":["The argument, so the same body prints a different name","The method body must be copied and edited","The return type","The parameter's type"],"answer":0,"explain":"One definition serves both calls because the input arrives as an argument.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does introduce(\"Anu\", \"12\") fail to match introduce(String name, int age)?","options":["String must come second","The second argument is text, but the parameter expects an int","A method cannot take two arguments","12 cannot appear in a call"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"12\" is a String. The age parameter is an int, so the call should pass the number 12.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which list is parameters rather than arguments?","options":["Hello, in the printed text","static void","String name, int age in the definition","\"Maya\", 14 in the call"],"answer":2,"explain":"Parameters are the declared inputs. Arguments are the values at a particular call.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The call passes a name, but the method ignores it and always prints Hello, learner. What is missing?","options":["A second copy of greet","The word argument in the source","A loop","A parameter that the body actually uses"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without a parameter, the call's text has nowhere to go. greet(String name) can print that name.","kind":"debug","code":"static void greet() {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello, learner\");\n}\ngreet(\"Anu\");","fix":"static void greet(String name) {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello, \" + name);\n}\ngreet(\"Anu\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Return values: get a result back","teach":"return sends a result back to the caller. The returned value must match the return type. void does not return a value. add(4, 6) returns 10, which the caller can store.","questions":[{"q":"What does return a + b; do in static int add(int a, int b)?","options":["It sends the sum back to the caller as an int","It prints the sum and returns nothing","It stores the sum in a global comment","It creates a new method"],"answer":0,"explain":"The caller can write int total = add(4, 6); and total receives 10.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What must match the value that is returned?","options":["Only the parameter names","The method's return type","The number of comments","The file name"],"answer":1,"explain":"int methods return a whole number, String methods return text, boolean methods return true or false, and void returns nothing.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What can the caller do with a returned result?","options":["Turn it into a class name","Use it as a parameter name only","Store it, print it, or use it in another calculation","Only ignore it, because return prints automatically"],"answer":2,"explain":"The vending-machine analogy says the caller receives something it can use. return does not by itself print.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which return type means the method gives nothing back?","options":["int","String","boolean","void"],"answer":3,"explain":"void can still do work such as printing. It just has no result to assign.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"static int add(int a, int b) returns a + b. What does add(4, 6) return?","options":["10","46","4","Nothing, because return prints instead"],"answer":0,"explain":"4 + 6 is 10. The caller receives that int.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does this print?","options":["Nothing, until total is returned again","10","add","4 + 6"],"answer":1,"explain":"add returns 10, total stores it, and println prints that stored result.","kind":"practice","code":"static int add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\nint total = add(4, 6);\nSystem.out.println(total);"},{"q":"Which signature fits a method that must give back the area of a rectangle as a whole number?","options":["static int area()","static String area(String width)","static int area(int width, int height)","static void area(int width, int height)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lab needs two inputs and a numeric result. void would give nothing back.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"area returns width * height. What does area(2, 3) return?","options":["5","23","2","6"],"answer":3,"explain":"The method multiplies the arguments and returns 6.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which return matches static String status()?","options":["return \"Done\";","return 42;","return true;","return; with no value, as the only result"],"answer":0,"explain":"A String method must return text. A bare return fits void, not String.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which return matches static boolean bigEnough(int n) when the method should report whether n \u003e= 10?","options":["System.out.println(n \u003e= 10); with no return","return n \u003e= 10;","return n;","return \"true\";"],"answer":1,"explain":"The return type is boolean, so the method returns the comparison's true or false result.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can println(add(2, 3)) show 5?","options":["2 and 3 are printed separately as text","void methods print their name","add returns 5, and println prints that returned value","println calls are ignored inside expressions"],"answer":2,"explain":"The call is an expression whose value is the returned int.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does this method need in order to compile and return the sum?","options":["The word void instead of int","No parameters","A println instead of a result","A return of the int sum. Storing it in total does not send it back"],"answer":3,"explain":"The return type int promises a whole number. return total; is what gives that number to the caller.","kind":"debug","code":"static int add(int a, int b) {\n    int total = a + b;\n}","fix":"static int add(int a, int b) {\n    int total = a + b;\n    return total;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Overloading and local variables","teach":"Overloading reuses a method name when the parameter lists differ. Changing only the return type does not overload. A local variable exists only inside the method that declares it.","questions":[{"q":"Which pair can be overloaded together?","options":["add(int a, int b) and add(int a, int b, int c)","add(int a, int b) and add(int a, int b) with a different return type","two methods whose names differ","a method and a comment with the same words"],"answer":0,"explain":"Java tells the versions apart by the number and types of parameters. The name add can stay the same.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can overloading be done by changing only the return type?","options":["Only if the name also changes","No. The parameter lists must differ","Yes. The return type is enough","Only for void"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two methods with the same name and the same parameter types are not a legal overload, even if one returns int and the other returns double.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is a local variable?","options":["A parameter of a different method","The class name","A variable declared inside a method, available only there","A variable every method can see automatically"],"answer":2,"explain":"score declared inside one method is that method's local variable. Other methods do not see it.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does Java choose between add(2, 3) and add(2, 3, 4)?","options":["By the order of the methods in the file only, ignoring the arguments","By the return type the caller hopes for","By a comment above the call","By the number of arguments"],"answer":3,"explain":"Two ints match the two-parameter version. Three ints match the three-parameter version.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which call uses the three-parameter add?","options":["add(2, 3, 4)","add(2, 3)","add(2)","add()"],"answer":0,"explain":"The three arguments select the version that accepts three ints.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does add(2, 3) return when that version returns a + b?","options":["2","5","9","24"],"answer":1,"explain":"The two-parameter body adds its two arguments.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does add(2, 3, 4) return when that version returns a + b + c?","options":["24","234","9","5"],"answer":2,"explain":"All three arguments are added.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which second method overloads add(int a, int b)?","options":["static double add(int a, int b)","static int add(int a, int b)","static int plus(int a, int b)","static double add(double a, double b)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The double version has a different parameter list, so the name add can be reused. The same int list is not an overload, and plus is a different name.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"int score = 7; is declared inside one method. Where can that score be used?","options":["Inside that method","In every method automatically","Only in main, even if it was declared elsewhere","As a class name"],"answer":0,"explain":"A local variable's region is the method that declares it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The explorer offers add(2.5, 3.2). Which parameter list matches those arguments?","options":["no parameters","double and double","int and int","three ints"],"answer":1,"explain":"2.5 and 3.2 are decimals, so they match a double version rather than the int version.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is a calculator a useful picture of overloading?","options":["Each button must have a different name","Overloading removes the need for parameters","The name add stays the same while the arguments say which version you mean","The calculator returns text for every sum"],"answer":2,"explain":"Same task name, different inputs. That is the lesson's idea.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does this pair fail as an overload?","options":["The name add is illegal","Java cannot return int","Three parameters are required for every overload","The parameter lists are identical. Only the return type changed"],"answer":3,"explain":"Overloading needs a different parameter list. Rename one method or change the parameters.","kind":"debug","code":"static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nstatic double add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }","fix":"static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nstatic double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Practice lab","teach":"Choose the signature from the task. A displayed message with no result is void. A calculated area needs inputs and a return type. Decimals need double when the result is not a whole number.","questions":[{"q":"A task displays Welcome! and returns no value. Which signature fits?","options":["static void welcome()","static String welcome()","static int welcome(int name)","static boolean welcome(String message)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Printing without giving a result back is void, and this task needs no parameters.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A task receives two decimal numbers and returns their sum as a decimal. Which signature fits?","options":["static double add()","static double add(double a, double b)","static void add(double a, double b)","static int add(int a, int b)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The inputs and the result are decimals, so double is the lesson's match. void would return nothing.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the rectangle lab use a return type?","options":["void methods cannot use parameters","A return type replaces the braces","The caller needs the area as a result, not only a printed message","Every method must return int"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lab says two inputs and a result. return width * height; gives that result back.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When would the area method use double instead of int?","options":["Whenever the method prints","Only when there are no parameters","When the method is named main","When the width, height, or area needs a fractional part"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lab says to choose int or double according to whether decimals are needed.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"static int area(int width, int height) returns width * height. What does area(4, 5) return?","options":["20","9","45","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"The method multiplies the two arguments.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call matches static int area(int width, int height)?","options":["area()","area(4, 5)","area(4)","area(\"4\", \"5\")"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both parameters are ints, so the call passes two whole numbers.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A task only prints Done and gives nothing back. Which signature fits?","options":["static String done(int value)","static boolean done()","static void done()","static int done()"],"answer":2,"explain":"There is no result to return and no input to accept.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which body matches static int area(int width, int height)?","options":["System.out.println(width * height); with no return","return \"width * height\";","return;","return width * height;"],"answer":3,"explain":"The return type is int, so the body returns the numeric product.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Width is 2.5 and height is 4.0, and the area should keep the fraction. Which method fits?","options":["static double area(double width, double height)","static int area(int width, int height)","static void area(double width, double height)","static String area(double width)"],"answer":0,"explain":"int would not be the lesson's choice for fractional measurements, and void would not give the area back.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with choosing static int welcome(int name) for a method that only prints Welcome!?","options":["Parentheses cannot be empty","It demands an int the task does not have, and it promises a number the task does not return","void is illegal","A greeting cannot be a method"],"answer":1,"explain":"The simplest matching signature is static void welcome().","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The lab simulates the call in the page. What does that simulation not do?","options":["It forbids parameters","It changes the meaning of return","It does not compile Java on a server","It cannot show an area"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says the output is simulated to explain the idea.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The area method prints 20 but the caller cannot store the area. What is missing?","options":["A second method with the same parameters","The word static inside the return","A loop around the multiplication","A returned int. println shows a message, but it is not the method's result"],"answer":3,"explain":"int total = area(4, 5); needs area to return 20. A void method that only prints does not give total a value.","kind":"debug","code":"static void area(int width, int height) {\n    System.out.println(width * height);\n}","fix":"static int area(int width, int height) {\n    return width * height;\n}","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_input_output_interactive_tutorial.html","code":"06","name":"Input and output","sections":[{"title":"1. Input and output: the conversation","teach":"Input is information the program receives. Output is information it gives back. A program can receive data, process it, and then show a result.","questions":[{"q":"What is input?","options":["Information the program receives, such as a name or a number a person types","Only the text println displays","A comment","The class name"],"answer":0,"explain":"Input comes in. Output is what the program gives back, such as a greeting or a total.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is output?","options":["The import line","Information the program gives back, such as a printed message or result","Whatever the user types","A variable's type"],"answer":1,"explain":"println is output. nextLine is a way to receive input.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the restaurant analogy map to output?","options":["The kitchen's name only","The menu that is never shown","The meal and the bill that come back after the order","The customer's order"],"answer":2,"explain":"The order is input. The returned meal and bill are output.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What pattern does the lesson say a program follows?","options":["Print first and never receive data","Store comments as input","Skip processing whenever input exists","Receive data, process it, then show a result"],"answer":3,"explain":"The conversation is two directions: information in, a result out.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which line is output?","options":["System.out.println(\"Welcome!\");","String name = input.nextLine();","import java.util.Scanner;","Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);"],"answer":0,"explain":"println displays a message. The Scanner lines prepare or read input.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line reads a full line of input?","options":["// input","String name = input.nextLine();","System.out.println(\"Welcome!\");","class Main {"],"answer":1,"explain":"nextLine reads what the person types up to Enter. println does not read.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A person types a price and the program prints a total. Which part is the price?","options":["A comment","The return type of println","Input","Output"],"answer":2,"explain":"The typed price is received. The printed total is output.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which sequence matches the lesson's conversation?","options":["Read a value with println","Print the result before any instruction exists","Use nextLine to display text","Show a prompt, read a value, then show a result"],"answer":3,"explain":"The prompt is output, the typed value is input, and the result is output again.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is Scanner mentioned beside println in this opening?","options":["println shows output, and Scanner is the setup for reading input","Scanner replaces println","They do the same job","Scanner compiles the program"],"answer":0,"explain":"The section introduces both directions before the later sections explain each one.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The program prints Welcome and then waits for a name. How many directions of the conversation has it used?","options":["Neither, because Welcome is a comment","Both output and input","Output only","Input only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The printed welcome is output. Waiting for the name is input.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which of these cannot be input from the keyboard in the lesson's examples?","options":["An age","A price","The word import","A name"],"answer":2,"explain":"import is source code, not a value the user types into a running program.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The author wanted to display Welcome, but called nextLine instead. What did they mix up?","options":["nextLine prints and reads at the same time","Welcome must be an int","Scanner cannot be created before a prompt","nextLine reads input. Displaying a message uses println"],"answer":3,"explain":"Output and input are different methods. The greeting should be println(\"Welcome!\");","kind":"debug","code":"input.nextLine(\"Welcome!\");","fix":"System.out.println(\"Welcome!\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Display output with print and println","teach":"println displays a value and moves to a new line. print stays on the same line. A text value plus a number joins into one message.","questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between print and println?","options":["print stays on the same line. println moves to a new line after the value","print works only for numbers","println cannot print text","They always produce two lines"],"answer":0,"explain":"Name: followed by print, then println(\"Arun\"), puts Arun on that same line and then ends it.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What can println display, according to this section?","options":["Only variable names","Text, whole numbers, decimals, and boolean values","Only Strings","Only comments"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's table includes Hello, 25, 3.14, and true.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"Total: \" + total do?","options":["It compares them","It reads keyboard input","It joins the text and the value into one message","It always adds total to the length of the text"],"answer":2,"explain":"When one side of + is text, Java builds one string to display.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When does + perform arithmetic instead of joining?","options":["Whenever println is used","Only inside nextLine","Never in this lesson","When it is used with numbers, not with text"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson warns that the same symbol can add numbers or join a message, depending on the values.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is displayed?","options":["Name: Arun, then Age: 15 on the next line","Name: Arun Age: 15 on one line","Arun Name:","15 only"],"answer":0,"explain":"print(\"Name: \") stays put, println(\"Arun\") finishes that line, and the next println starts the age line.","kind":"practice","code":"System.out.print(\"Name: \");\nSystem.out.println(\"Arun\");\nSystem.out.println(\"Age: \" + 15);"},{"q":"Which pair prints Good and morning on separate lines?","options":["println(\"Good\" + \"morning\");","println(\"Good\"); println(\"morning\");","print(\"Good\"); print(\"morning\");","print(\"Good morning\");"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each println ends its line. print and + would keep the words together.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does println(\"Age: \" + 15) display?","options":["30","Age:","Age: 15","Age:  + 15"],"answer":2,"explain":"The text and the number are joined. 15 is not added to the word Age.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does println(3.14) display?","options":["\"3.14\" including quote marks","314","true","3.14"],"answer":3,"explain":"A decimal is a valid value for println. The quote marks are syntax and are not printed.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does println(true) display?","options":["true","1","yes","Nothing, because a boolean is not text"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's table includes boolean true as output.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call leaves the cursor on the same line?","options":["System.out.println();","System.out.print(\"Name: \");","System.out.println(\"Name: \");","input.nextLine();"],"answer":1,"explain":"print is the same-line method. An empty println would finish a line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You want Total: 20 from a numeric total of 20. Which expression fits?","options":["Total: + total without quotes","nextInt()","\"Total: \" + total","total + \"Total: \" with the label after the number, if the label must come first"],"answer":2,"explain":"The label has to be text, and + joins it to the number. Without quotes, Total is a name, not text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The two words were supposed to share a line, but each appears on its own line. Why?","options":["print cannot accept text","Arun is an int","+ is required between print calls","Both calls are println, so each one starts a new line. The first call should be print if the second word continues beside it"],"answer":3,"explain":"println(\"Name: \") already finishes the line before Arun is printed.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(\"Name: \");\nSystem.out.println(\"Arun\");","fix":"System.out.print(\"Name: \");\nSystem.out.println(\"Arun\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Read keyboard input using Scanner","teach":"Scanner reads from a source. For the keyboard, that source is System.in. import java.util.Scanner lets you use the short name. nextLine reads until Enter.","questions":[{"q":"What is Scanner in this lesson?","options":["A class that can read input from a source such as the keyboard","A method that prints a line","The console window itself","A type of comment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson compares it to a careful reader that takes the next piece of input.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does import java.util.Scanner; do?","options":["It creates System.in","It lets this file use the short name Scanner","It reads the user's name","It prints the import line"],"answer":1,"explain":"The import makes the class available. It does not by itself read anything.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does new Scanner(System.in) create?","options":["A String already containing the user's name","A file named System.in","A Scanner that reads standard input, usually the keyboard","A Scanner that prints to the screen"],"answer":2,"explain":"System.in is the common source for console input. The variable input is the reader.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does nextLine read?","options":["One character only","A whole number only","The next println","A full line of text, until the user presses Enter"],"answer":3,"explain":"The name prompt in the sample is answered by nextLine, which takes the whole line.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which line creates the keyboard reader?","options":["Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);","System.out.println(\"What is your name?\");","String name = input.nextLine();","import java.util.Scanner;"],"answer":0,"explain":"new Scanner(System.in) is the creation. The import only makes the name available, and nextLine does the reading.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the sample print after the user types Maya and presses Enter?","options":["Scanner","Hello, Maya","What is your name? Maya","Maya Hello,"],"answer":1,"explain":"The prompt is printed first. nextLine stores Maya, and the last println joins it to Hello.","kind":"practice","code":"System.out.println(\"What is your name?\");\nString name = input.nextLine();\nSystem.out.println(\"Hello, \" + name);"},{"q":"Why is the prompt printed before nextLine?","options":["Scanner cannot be created after a println","The name is read from the prompt string","So the person knows what to type before the program waits","nextLine prints the prompt itself"],"answer":2,"explain":"The prompt is output. The following nextLine is the wait for input.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which expression greets the name that was read?","options":["\"Hello, \" + input","nextLine(\"Hello\")","System.in","\"Hello, \" + name"],"answer":3,"explain":"name holds the line that was read. input is the Scanner, not the text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Where does the sample usually place the import?","options":["At the top of the source, before the class uses the short name Scanner","Inside nextLine","After the greeting","In the user's typed name"],"answer":0,"explain":"The import belongs with the source, not in the input the person types.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"System.in in this section means what?","options":["The class Scanner","The standard input source, usually the keyboard","The standard output stream","A String variable"],"answer":1,"explain":"out is for display. in is for reading. Scanner connects to in.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call reads the entire typed city, including a space in Chennai city?","options":["nextInt()","println()","nextLine()","next(), which stops at whitespace"],"answer":2,"explain":"The later section says nextLine reads a full line, while next reads one token. A city with a space needs the full line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The file uses Scanner but does not import it, and it is not referred to by its full java.util name. What is missing?","options":["System.out.println","A semicolon after the class name only","nextLine written as next","import java.util.Scanner;"],"answer":3,"explain":"The short name Scanner needs the import shown in the lesson, unless the code spells java.util.Scanner everywhere.","kind":"debug","code":"Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);","fix":"import java.util.Scanner;\nScanner input = new Scanner(System.in);","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Read text, integers and decimals","teach":"Choose the Scanner method that matches the value. nextLine reads a line, next reads one token, nextInt reads a whole number, nextDouble reads a decimal, and nextBoolean reads true or false.","questions":[{"q":"Which method reads a whole number such as 42?","options":["nextInt()","nextLine(), which reads a line of text","nextDouble(), which reads a decimal","nextBoolean()"],"answer":0,"explain":"The method should match the expected type. 42 as a whole number is nextInt.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which method reads one token and stops at whitespace?","options":["println()","next()","nextLine()","nextInt()"],"answer":1,"explain":"next() would take Chennai from an input that continues with more words. nextLine() would take the rest of the line.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which method reads true or false?","options":["nextLine(), if you need the boolean value rather than the text line","nextDouble()","nextBoolean()","nextInt()"],"answer":2,"explain":"nextBoolean matches the boolean type. A line of text is still text even when the letters are t-r-u-e.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the lesson say the typed input must do?","options":["Any text can be read with nextInt","nextDouble accepts a person's name","Scanner converts every word into a number","It must match the method you chose"],"answer":3,"explain":"A method such as nextInt expects a whole number. A different kind of input does not match that read.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"The user types 42 and the program needs an int. Which call fits?","options":["int age = input.nextInt();","int age = input.nextLine();","int age = input.nextDouble();","String age = input.nextInt();"],"answer":0,"explain":"nextInt returns a whole number, which matches int and the typed 42.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The user types 3.75 and the program needs a decimal. Which call fits?","options":["boolean price = input.nextBoolean();","double price = input.nextDouble();","double price = input.nextInt();","int price = input.nextDouble();"],"answer":1,"explain":"3.75 is a decimal, so nextDouble matches double.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The user types Chennai and the program needs one word. Which call fits?","options":["int city = input.next();","String city = input.nextBoolean();","String city = input.next();","String city = input.nextInt();"],"answer":2,"explain":"One token is next(). nextInt would be looking for a whole number.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The user types Chennai city and the program needs both words. Which call fits?","options":["String place = input.next();","int place = input.nextLine();","double place = input.nextDouble();","String place = input.nextLine();"],"answer":3,"explain":"nextLine reads the full line, including the space. next would stop after Chennai.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The user types true. Which variable declaration matches nextBoolean()?","options":["boolean ok = input.nextBoolean();","String ok = input.nextBoolean();","int ok = input.nextBoolean();","boolean ok = input.nextInt();"],"answer":0,"explain":"The method's result is a boolean, so the variable should be a boolean.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pairing is correct?","options":["nextLine for a boolean value that must be stored as boolean","nextInt for 42, nextDouble for 3.75, nextLine for a full sentence","nextInt for 3.75","nextDouble for a sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each row of the lesson's table matches one method to one kind of input.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A bill uses a decimal price and a whole-number quantity. Which pair of reads matches?","options":["nextLine() for the price and nextBoolean() for the quantity","next() for the price","nextDouble() for the price and nextInt() for the quantity","nextInt() for both"],"answer":2,"explain":"The price has a fraction, so it is a double. The quantity is a whole number.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The variable is an int, but the read uses nextLine. What is wrong?","options":["int can store a line of text","nextLine returns an int","Scanner has no numeric methods","nextLine returns text. A whole number should be read with nextInt"],"answer":3,"explain":"The type and the Scanner method need to agree. nextInt returns the int this variable expects.","kind":"debug","code":"int age = input.nextLine();","fix":"int age = input.nextInt();","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Common Scanner issues","teach":"nextInt reads the number and leaves the Enter newline behind. A following nextLine can consume that empty leftover unless another nextLine consumes it first.","questions":[{"q":"What does nextInt typically leave behind?","options":["The newline from pressing Enter","The number itself, unread","The next word the user has not typed","A boolean"],"answer":0,"explain":"The number is taken. The line ending is still waiting in the input.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What can the next nextLine do with that leftover newline?","options":["Delete the Scanner","Consume it and return an empty string, instead of the line the user types next","Skip every future read","Turn the number into text automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is the beginner surprise. The line you expected is still waiting behind the empty leftover.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the lesson's fix before reading a full name?","options":["Remove the variable","Use nextInt for the name","Call nextLine once to consume the leftover newline, then call nextLine again for the name","Replace nextInt with println"],"answer":2,"explain":"The extra nextLine takes the leftover Enter. The following nextLine reads the full name.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the ticket-queue analogy?","options":["nextLine prints a ticket","The queue sorts the input","Enter deletes the number","nextInt takes the number ticket but leaves the Enter keypress in the queue"],"answer":3,"explain":"The next line reader sees that leftover Enter unless something consumes it first.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"age is read with nextInt, then fullName is read with nextLine and comes back empty. What was read by that nextLine?","options":["The leftover newline, not the name the user typed afterwards","The age as text","The next integer","The import line"],"answer":0,"explain":"The name is still waiting. The first nextLine after nextInt took the empty remainder of the number's line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which sequence consumes the leftover newline before the name?","options":["nextInt(); nextInt();","nextInt(); nextLine(); nextLine();","nextInt(); nextLine(); with only one nextLine for the name","nextLine(); nextInt(); with no extra step needed for this particular bug"],"answer":1,"explain":"The middle nextLine is the consumer. The last nextLine reads the name. The lesson shows exactly this order.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the empty string possible even though the user did type a name?","options":["nextInt reads the name as well","Java removes names that contain spaces","The empty result is the leftover Enter, and the name has not been read yet","nextLine cannot read letters"],"answer":2,"explain":"The reads happen in order. The surprise is which piece of input the first nextLine receives.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which later read is harmed by the leftover newline?","options":["nextInt reading another number, which is a different issue from this section's empty line","println","import","nextLine"],"answer":3,"explain":"This section's bug is specifically the next full-line read after a numeric read.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The fix variable is not stored: input.nextLine(); after nextInt. What is its job?","options":["To discard the leftover newline","To read the age again","To print a blank line","To create a new Scanner"],"answer":0,"explain":"The return value is ignored because the call's purpose is to consume the leftover, not to keep it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After the extra nextLine, what does the following nextLine return?","options":["true","The full line the user types next, such as their name","Another empty string always","The integer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once the leftover is gone, nextLine waits for a real line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which ordering matches the lesson's corrected snippet?","options":["Print the name, then read the int","Consume a line before any input exists and call that the age","Read the int, consume the leftover, then read the full line","Read the full line, then consume a leftover that has not been created"],"answer":2,"explain":"The leftover exists because nextInt already ran. The consumer comes before the real nextLine.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does fullName become empty?","options":["nextInt stored the name in age","Strings cannot be empty","Scanner cannot read after an int","nextLine immediately after nextInt consumes the leftover newline. A nextLine should consume that leftover before the name is read"],"answer":3,"explain":"The number read does not take the Enter. The name read must not be the call that takes that Enter.","kind":"debug","code":"int age = input.nextInt();\nString fullName = input.nextLine();","fix":"int age = input.nextInt();\ninput.nextLine();\nString fullName = input.nextLine();","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Guided mini projects","teach":"The projects combine prompts, Scanner reads, and println results. A greeting uses nextLine. Adding whole numbers uses nextInt. A bill uses nextDouble for the price and nextInt for the quantity.","questions":[{"q":"What do these mini projects combine?","options":["Input, output, variables, and a simple calculation","Only comments","A class hierarchy","File sorting"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each project reads values, computes or joins them, and prints a result. The controls simulate a console program.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which reads fit the personal greeting?","options":["println for both reads","nextLine for the name and nextLine for the city","nextInt for the name","nextBoolean for the city"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both answers are text lines. The output is Hello and the city on the following line.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which reads fit adding two whole numbers?","options":["nextBoolean twice","next for both, stored in int variables","nextInt and nextInt","nextLine and nextDouble"],"answer":2,"explain":"The total is a + b, and both inputs are whole numbers.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which reads fit the bill?","options":["nextInt for the price and nextLine for the quantity","two nextBoolean calls","println of the price before it is read","nextDouble for the price and nextInt for the quantity"],"answer":3,"explain":"Price can have a fraction. Quantity is a whole number. The result is price times quantity.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Name is Maya and city is Chennai. What does the greeting project print?","options":["Hello Maya, then You are from Chennai","Maya Chennai on one line","Hello only","Chennai Maya"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each println finishes its own line after joining the stored text.","kind":"practice","code":"String name = input.nextLine();\nString city = input.nextLine();\nSystem.out.println(\"Hello \" + name);\nSystem.out.println(\"You are from \" + city);"},{"q":"The user enters 4 and 6 for the adding project. What is printed?","options":["4 + 6","Total: 10","Total: 46","10 Total:"],"answer":1,"explain":"nextInt reads both ints, a + b is 10, and the message joins the word Total to that sum.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Price is 2.5 and quantity is 4. What is the bill?","options":["2.54","8","10.0","6.5"],"answer":2,"explain":"The decimal price times the whole quantity is 10.0. The lesson uses double arithmetic for the price.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which print matches the adding project?","options":["System.out.println(a + b) with no stored total, as the only legal form","input.nextInt(\"Total\")","System.out.print(total) after skipping the reads","System.out.println(\"Total: \" + total);"],"answer":3,"explain":"The project stores the sum and then prints it with a label.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the city read with nextLine rather than next?","options":["A city such as Chennai city has a space, and nextLine keeps the full line","nextLine returns an int","next cannot read letters","The greeting project forbids Strings"],"answer":0,"explain":"The section on text already distinguished a token from a full line. The greeting wants the whole city line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What would nextInt do with a price of 2.5 in the bill project?","options":["It would print the bill","It would not match the decimal the lesson reads with nextDouble","It would keep the .5 in an int","It would read the quantity instead"],"answer":1,"explain":"The bill's price is a double read. nextInt is the quantity's method.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which project does not need arithmetic?","options":["The bill calculator","A project that multiplies price by quantity","The personal greeting","Adding two numbers"],"answer":2,"explain":"The greeting only joins text. The other two compute a number.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The bill uses nextInt for a decimal price. What should change?","options":["Read the quantity with nextLine","Print the price before reading it","Replace * with +","Read the price with nextDouble and store it in a double"],"answer":3,"explain":"2.5 is not a whole number. The lesson's bill reads double price = input.nextDouble().","kind":"debug","code":"int price = input.nextInt();\nint quantity = input.nextInt();","fix":"double price = input.nextDouble();\nint quantity = input.nextInt();","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Exercises","teach":"The exercises ask you to choose the read and the print that match the task. Text lines use nextLine, whole numbers use nextInt, decimals use nextDouble, and labels are joined with +.","questions":[{"q":"What is the focus of these exercises?","options":["Choosing the input method and the output statement that match the task","Writing a new class for every read","Sorting the input","Catching every exception"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says to focus on the right read and the right print, not on a new topic.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A prompt asks for a full name that may contain spaces. Which read matches?","options":["nextDouble","nextLine","next","nextInt"],"answer":1,"explain":"A full name is a line of text. next would stop at the first space.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A prompt asks how many tickets, and the answer is a whole number. Which read matches?","options":["nextDouble","nextBoolean","nextInt","nextLine stored directly in an int"],"answer":2,"explain":"A count is an int, so the matching Scanner method is nextInt.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After reading two ints, the program should display their sum with a label. Which print matches?","options":["println(\"Total: \" + a + b), which joins the digits instead of adding if you are not careful about order","println(a + \" + \" + b) with no sum","nextInt()","println(\"Total: \" + (a + b))"],"answer":3,"explain":"Parentheses make the addition happen before the text join. \"Total: \" + a + b would glue the digits onto the label.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"a is 7 and b is 5. What does println(\"Total: \" + (a + b)) display?","options":["Total: 12","Total: 75","Total: 7 + 5","12 Total:"],"answer":0,"explain":"The parentheses add 7 and 5 first. Then + joins 12 to the label.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does println(\"Total: \" + a + b) display when a is 7 and b is 5?","options":["Total: 7 + 5","Total: 75","Total: 12","75"],"answer":1,"explain":"The text comes first, so 7 is joined and then 5 is joined. The numbers are not added.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair reads a word token and then a whole number?","options":["nextDouble() then nextBoolean()","println() then next()","next() then nextInt()","nextLine() then nextLine(), stored in an int"],"answer":2,"explain":"A single word is a token. The number matches nextInt. This is a different pair from a full line followed by a number.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The user types 19 for an age. Which statement stores it?","options":["String age = input.nextInt();","int age = input.nextLine();","double age = input.nextBoolean();","int age = input.nextInt();"],"answer":3,"explain":"The type, the method, and the typed value are all whole numbers.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need to print a blank line after a message. Which call fits?","options":["System.out.println();","System.out.print(\" \"); only, which prints a space and stays on the line","input.nextLine() as output","nextInt()"],"answer":0,"explain":"println with no value finishes the line. That is output, not a read.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A decimal discount is typed as 0.5. Which statement stores it?","options":["boolean discount = input.nextDouble();","double discount = input.nextDouble();","int discount = input.nextDouble();","double discount = input.nextInt();"],"answer":1,"explain":"The fraction needs double and nextDouble.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which task is output only, with no Scanner method?","options":["Read an age","Read a price","Print Hello before asking any question","Read a city"],"answer":2,"explain":"A prompt or a greeting is println. Scanner methods are the reads that follow.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The total prints as Total: 75 for the ints 7 and 5. What went wrong?","options":["nextInt added the digits","println cannot print 12","7 and 5 are Strings","The numbers were joined onto the text. They need to be added before that join"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Total: \" + a + b walks left to right and joins. \"Total: \" + (a + b) adds first.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(\"Total: \" + a + b);","fix":"System.out.println(\"Total: \" + (a + b));","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_arrays_storage_traversal_search.html","code":"07","name":"Arrays","sections":[{"title":"1. What is an array?","teach":"An array stores several values of the same type under one name. Each value has an index, and the first index is 0. Four elements use indexes 0, 1, 2, and 3.","questions":[{"q":"What does an array store?","options":["Multiple values of the same type under one variable name","One value only, like a single int","Values of any mixture of types in the beginner examples","A comment for each locker"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's picture is a row of lockers. The array name is the row, and each locker holds one item of the same type.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is an index?","options":["The array's type","The numbered position of one element","The value stored in every locker","The length plus one"],"answer":1,"explain":"scores[0] is the first locker. The index identifies the position, and the value is what is stored there.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where does array indexing start in this lesson?","options":["At the length","At -1","At 0","At 1"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first element of {82, 95, 71, 88} is index 0, not index 1.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"For an array of four elements, which indexes are valid?","options":["1, 2, 3, and 4","0, 1, 2, 3, and 4","Only 4","0, 1, 2, and 3"],"answer":3,"explain":"Four lockers are numbered from 0 through one less than the length.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"int[] scores = {82, 95, 71, 88}; What is scores[0]?","options":["82","95","88","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Index 0 is the first value in the brace list.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is scores[3] in {82, 95, 71, 88}?","options":["4","88","71","82"],"answer":1,"explain":"The indexes are 0, 1, 2, and 3. Index 3 is the last value, 88.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why use an array instead of score1, score2, and score3?","options":["Separate variables cannot hold numbers","An array starts at index 1 so the names are shorter","Related values stay under one name and can be visited by index","An array can change the type of each locker"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says separate variables become hard to organise. The array keeps one row of scores.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration matches four int scores?","options":["int scores = {82, 95, 71, 88};","String[] scores = {82, 95, 71, 88};","int[] scores = 82, 95, 71, 88;","int[] scores = {82, 95, 71, 88};"],"answer":3,"explain":"The type is an int array, and the braces list the values.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How many elements does {82, 95, 71, 88} contain?","options":["4","3","5","88"],"answer":0,"explain":"There are four listed values. The last index is 3, not 4.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the locker analogy call the array name?","options":["The key that opens every locker to the same item","The row of lockers","The number on one locker","The item inside locker 0 only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The name identifies the row. The index identifies one position in that row.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which access gets the third element of an array?","options":["index 1","index 0","index 2","index 3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Counting from 0, the third element is index 2. Index 3 is the fourth element.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"This tries to read the fourth element of a four-element array. What is wrong with the index?","options":["Index 0 is the fourth element","Arrays cannot store 88","length is required in the declaration","Index 4 is past the end. The valid indexes are 0 through 3"],"answer":3,"explain":"Four elements do not create an index 4. The last position is length - 1.","kind":"debug","code":"int[] scores = {82, 95, 71, 88};\nSystem.out.println(scores[4]);","fix":"int[] scores = {82, 95, 71, 88};\nSystem.out.println(scores[3]);","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Create an array and store values","teach":"An array's length is fixed when it is created. new int[5] starts every element at 0. A brace list can supply the values immediately. An assignment such as ages[1] = 15 replaces one element.","questions":[{"q":"What is fixed when you create a Java array in this lesson?","options":["Its length","The type of each later element, which can change per index","The names of the elements","Whether indexes start at 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"You choose the length at creation. You can replace the value at an index, but that does not add a new locker.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does new int[5] store before you assign anything?","options":["5 in index 0","0 in every element","null in every element","false in every element"],"answer":1,"explain":"A new int array starts at 0. New boolean elements start at false, and String elements start at null.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does new String[3] store in each element at the start?","options":["0","false","null","\"\""],"answer":2,"explain":"Object references such as String start at null. 0 is the int default, and false is the boolean default.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does ages[1] = 15 do?","options":["It creates a second array","It changes the length to 15","It shifts every later element","It replaces the value at index 1"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's third pattern stores a new value in an existing position.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which line creates space for 5 ints?","options":["int[] marks = new int[5];","int marks = new int[5];","int[] marks = {5};","int[] marks = new int[];"],"answer":0,"explain":"new int[5] is the length. {5} would be a one-element array whose value is 5.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line creates an array that already holds 12, 13, and 14?","options":["int[] ages = new int[] 12, 13, 14;","int[] ages = {12, 13, 14};","int[] ages = new int[3]; with no further assignments","int ages = {12, 13, 14};"],"answer":1,"explain":"The brace form supplies the values in one step.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"ages starts as {12, 13, 14} and then ages[1] = 15. What is the array?","options":["15, 13, 14","12, 13, 14, 15","12, 15, 14","12, 13, 15"],"answer":2,"explain":"Index 1 is the middle value. The length stays 3.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is marks[0] immediately after int[] marks = new int[5];?","options":["5","null","undefined","0"],"answer":3,"explain":"The int default fills every position, including index 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is flags[0] immediately after boolean[] flags = new boolean[2];?","options":["false","true","0","null"],"answer":0,"explain":"New boolean elements start at false.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which assignment changes only the last of three ages?","options":["ages.length = 16;","ages[2] = 16;","ages[3] = 16;","ages[1] = 16; if the last value was the target"],"answer":1,"explain":"Indexes 0, 1, and 2 are the three positions. length is not assigned a new size in this lesson.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After int[] marks = new int[5]; can the program store 80 at index 4?","options":["No. Index 4 would be the sixth element","Only by creating a String array","Yes. Index 4 is the last of the five positions","No. new int[5] cannot be changed"],"answer":2,"explain":"The length is fixed, but the values inside the existing indexes can be replaced.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this store?","options":["new int[5] starts at 1","0 cannot be replaced","The braces create a variable length","Index 5 is outside a 5-element array. The last valid index is 4"],"answer":3,"explain":"Five elements use indexes 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. Assignment cannot create index 5.","kind":"debug","code":"int[] marks = new int[5];\nmarks[5] = 80;","fix":"int[] marks = new int[5];\nmarks[4] = 80;","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Indexes, length and boundaries","teach":"length is the number of elements. The last index is length - 1. Reading a missing index throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. A loop should test i \u003c length, not i \u003c= length.","questions":[{"q":"What does numbers.length mean?","options":["How many elements the array contains","The last index","The first value","A method call written length()"],"answer":0,"explain":"For {10, 20, 30}, length is 3. The last index is length - 1, which is 2. Array length is a field, not a method.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the last index of an array?","options":["length + 1","length - 1","length","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"{10, 20, 30} has last index 2, and numbers[numbers.length - 1] is 30.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException in this section?","options":["A way to grow the array","The value -1","The error from using an index that does not exist","A successful read of the last element"],"answer":2,"explain":"For length 4, index 4 is invalid because the valid indexes are 0 through 3.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which loop test stays inside the array?","options":["i \u003c= array.length","i == array.length as the only visit","i \u003c array.length()","i \u003c array.length"],"answer":3,"explain":"i \u003c= length eventually uses an index equal to the length, and that index does not exist. length is not called as a method.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30}; What is numbers[0]?","options":["10","20","30","3"],"answer":0,"explain":"Index 0 is the first listed value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is numbers.length for {10, 20, 30}?","options":["4","3","2","30"],"answer":1,"explain":"There are three elements. The last index is 2, which is not the length.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is numbers[numbers.length - 1] for {10, 20, 30}?","options":["3","20","30","10"],"answer":2,"explain":"length - 1 is 2, and index 2 holds 30.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"An array has length 4. Which index is invalid?","options":["0","3","1","4"],"answer":3,"explain":"Valid indexes are 0, 1, 2, and 3. 4 is one past the end.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which expression is the last element of scores?","options":["scores[scores.length - 1]","scores[scores.length]","scores.length","scores[1] for every array"],"answer":0,"explain":"The last position is one less than the number of elements.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does a loop headed by i \u003c= scores.length do on its last try?","options":["It reads index 0 twice","It attempts an index that does not exist","It stops on the last valid element","It shortens the array"],"answer":1,"explain":"When i equals length, scores[i] is outside the lockers.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is numbers.length() wrong for an array?","options":["length includes only the last index","Parentheses change the index to 1","length is a field on an array. The parentheses belong to String's length method","Arrays have no size"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson writes numbers.length with no parentheses. A String uses length().","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What goes wrong on the last pass of this loop?","options":["i starts too high","length is 2","The array cannot be printed","i \u003c= numbers.length reaches index 3 on a length-3 array"],"answer":3,"explain":"The safe test is i \u003c numbers.length. Index 3 is not a position in {10, 20, 30}.","kind":"debug","code":"for (int i = 0; i \u003c= numbers.length; i++) {\n    System.out.println(numbers[i]);\n}","fix":"for (int i = 0; i \u003c numbers.length; i++) {\n    System.out.println(numbers[i]);\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Traversal: visit every element","teach":"Traversal visits elements one by one. A classic for loop gives you the index. An enhanced for loop gives you each value and does not give you the index.","questions":[{"q":"What is traversal?","options":["Visiting the elements one by one","Jumping straight to a missing index","Sorting the array","Creating a new array of a different length"],"answer":0,"explain":"A loop repeats the same operation for each index or each value.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How do you read for (int i = 0; i \u003c scores.length; i++)?","options":["Decrease i until it is negative","Start at 0, continue while i is less than the length, and add one after each pass","Start at 1 and continue through length","Visit only index 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"That header walks every valid index.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When does the lesson prefer an enhanced for loop?","options":["When you need the position of a match","When the array length is unknown and may change during the loop","When you need each value and do not need its index","When you must replace values at chosen indexes"],"answer":2,"explain":"for (int score : scores) hands you the value. The classic loop is the one that names the index.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When does the lesson prefer the classic for loop?","options":["When you only want to print values and the index does not matter","When the array is a String","When you want length()","When you need the index, or you want to update a position"],"answer":3,"explain":"The index is available as i in the classic loop. The enhanced loop does not provide it.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does this classic loop print for {82, 95, 71}?","options":["82, then 95, then 71","71, 95, 82","3 lines containing the indexes only","82 only"],"answer":0,"explain":"i runs through 0, 1, and 2, and each pass prints scores[i].","kind":"practice","code":"int[] scores = {82, 95, 71};\nfor (int i = 0; i \u003c scores.length; i++) {\n    System.out.println(scores[i]);\n}"},{"q":"What does for (int score : scores) give you on each pass?","options":["A new array","The next value, not its index","The index only","The length"],"answer":1,"explain":"The enhanced header names the element. It does not name i.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You must add 1 to every element. Which loop lets you write scores[i] = scores[i] + 1?","options":["A loop that visits length itself","A single assignment outside any loop, which changes only one known index if you write it once","The classic for loop with an index","The enhanced for loop, which does not give an index to assign through"],"answer":2,"explain":"Updating a chosen position needs the index. The enhanced loop is for reading values.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How many times does the body run for an array of length 3?","options":["2","4","1","3"],"answer":3,"explain":"i is 0, 1, and 2. The test i \u003c length stops before 3.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header visits every index exactly once?","options":["for (int i = 0; i \u003c scores.length; i++)","for (int i = 1; i \u003c= scores.length; i++)","for (int i = 0; i \u003c= scores.length; i++)","for (int score : scores[i])"],"answer":0,"explain":"Start at 0 and stop before length. Starting at 1 skips the first element, and \u003c= length steps past the end.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is printed by the enhanced loop over {82, 95, 71}?","options":["71 only","82, 95, and 71, one value per pass","0, 1, and 2","The word score three times"],"answer":1,"explain":"The enhanced loop still walks the values in order. It just does not tell you the index.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can the same array be traversed by either loop?","options":["The classic loop cannot print","length changes between the two forms","Both can visit every element. They differ in whether you receive the index","The enhanced loop skips duplicates"],"answer":2,"explain":"Traversal is the visit. The header chooses what information each visit gives you.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"This enhanced loop tries to print the index, but i was never declared. What does the enhanced header provide?","options":["The length as a value named i","A variable i automatically","Only the last element","Each value. Use a classic for loop when the index is required"],"answer":3,"explain":"for (int score : scores) has no index variable. for (int i = 0; i \u003c scores.length; i++) does.","kind":"debug","code":"for (int score : scores) {\n    System.out.println(i);\n}","fix":"for (int i = 0; i \u003c scores.length; i++) {\n    System.out.println(i);\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Search an array","teach":"Linear search checks from left to right until it finds the target or reaches the end. The lesson uses -1 to mean not found. In the worst case it checks every element.","questions":[{"q":"What does a linear search do?","options":["It checks elements from left to right until it finds the target or reaches the end","It jumps to the middle only","It sorts the array first","It checks index 0 and then stops"],"answer":0,"explain":"The target is compared with each element. break stops the scan once the value is found.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does -1 mean in the lesson's search?","options":["The first element","The target was not found. It is not a valid index","The target is at the last index","The array is empty"],"answer":1,"explain":"foundIndex starts at -1 and changes only when a match is stored.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the loop break after a match?","options":["The first element is always the match","length becomes -1","The index is already known, so the rest of the array does not need to be checked","break resets the array"],"answer":2,"explain":"The search can stop early. If it never matches, the loop ends with -1 still stored.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the worst-case amount of work?","options":["Only the first element","The length is never used","One comparison, whatever the size","Every element is checked"],"answer":3,"explain":"If the target is missing or is last, the scan looks through the whole array.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"scores is {82, 95, 71} and the target is 71. What index does the lesson's search return?","options":["2","-1","0","71"],"answer":0,"explain":"71 is at index 2. The loop stores 2 and breaks.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The target is 50 and it is not in {82, 95, 71}. What does foundIndex stay?","options":["50","-1","0","3"],"answer":1,"explain":"No assignment replaces the starting -1, so not found stays -1.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The target is 82. What is foundIndex?","options":["-1","82","0","1"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first element matches immediately, so the stored index is 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which comparison matches an int target to an element?","options":["scores[i].equals(target) as the lesson's int search","scores[i] = target","scores.length == target","scores[i] == target"],"answer":3,"explain":"The elements are ints, so == compares the numbers. = would assign.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Where is foundIndex set to the matching position?","options":["Inside the if, before break","Before the loop, as -1, which already means found","After the loop, always to 0","In the array length"],"answer":0,"explain":"-1 is only the starting signal. The index i is stored when the values are equal.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is -1 safe as a not-found signal here?","options":["length is -1 when the search fails","Valid indexes of this array are 0 and upward, so -1 cannot be confused with a real position","-1 is the last index","Arrays can hold a value at -1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says -1 is not a valid index.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A match is at index 1. How many elements must be checked before it can break?","options":["Every element, because break is ignored","None","The element at 0 and then the element at 1","Only the last element"],"answer":2,"explain":"Linear search reaches index 1 only after checking from the left. It can stop when that match is stored.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The search assigns the target into the array instead of comparing. What operator is wrong?","options":["break should be removed","-1 should be 0 even when the value is missing","length should replace i","A single = assigns. The test should be =="],"answer":3,"explain":"scores[i] = target overwrites the element and is not a question. == asks whether they are equal.","kind":"debug","code":"if (scores[i] = target) {\n    foundIndex = i;\n    break;\n}","fix":"if (scores[i] == target) {\n    foundIndex = i;\n    break;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Guided practice lab","teach":"The lab totals and averages the values, and it finds a maximum by starting with the first element and then comparing the rest. An int total divided by the length needs a double conversion when the average should keep a fraction.","questions":[{"q":"How does the average task start its total?","options":["At 0, then each score is added","At the length","At the last score","At -1"],"answer":0,"explain":"int total = 0; and the enhanced loop does total += score. The average divides that total by the number of scores.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the average cast total to double?","options":["To start the total at 0.0 automatically","So the division can keep a fractional part instead of using int division","To change the array length","To sort the scores"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson writes double average = (double) total / scores.length;","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does the maximum task start?","options":["Start max at the length","Start max at -1","Assume the array is not empty, and start max at the first element","Start max at 0, which fails if every value is negative"],"answer":2,"explain":"int max = numbers[0]; then the loop compares the later elements.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the maximum loop start at i = 1?","options":["Index 1 is always the maximum","Index 0 is invalid","The length is 1","Index 0 is already the starting maximum, so the scan continues with the remaining elements"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first element is the initial champion. Later elements replace it only when they are larger.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"scores is {80, 90, 70, 100}. What is the total?","options":["340","85","100","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"80 + 90 + 70 + 100 = 340.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the average of {80, 90, 70, 100} when the division keeps the fraction?","options":["21","85","84","340"],"answer":1,"explain":"340 / 4 is 85. The cast matters more when the total does not divide evenly.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the maximum of {4, 9, 2, 9}?","options":["2","24","9","4"],"answer":2,"explain":"Start at 4. 9 replaces it. 2 does not. The later 9 is not greater, so the maximum stays 9.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the maximum of {5}?","options":["0","1","-1","5"],"answer":3,"explain":"The only element is the starting maximum, and the loop from 1 does not run.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"total is 5 and length is 2. What is (double) total / length?","options":["2.5","2","3","5"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cast makes the division keep the fraction. An int division 5 / 2 would have been 2.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which snippet is the lab's maximum update?","options":["if (i \u003e max) max = numbers[i];","if (numbers[i] \u003e max) max = numbers[i];","if (numbers[i] \u003e max) max = i;","max = numbers.length;"],"answer":1,"explain":"max tracks the largest value, not the index, in this lab's snippet.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why would starting max at 0 be a bad match for this lab when every number is negative?","options":["Negative numbers cannot be compared","length would become negative","0 would be larger than every element, so it would not be a value from the array","0 is always the first index"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lab starts from numbers[0], which is safe for a non-empty array of any ints.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The average of 5 and 2 prints 2. What was lost?","options":["The array has the wrong length","+= subtracted the scores","A maximum was calculated instead","The division used ints, so the fraction was dropped. Cast the total to double before dividing"],"answer":3,"explain":"(double) total / length keeps 2.5. total / length, with both ints, is 2.","kind":"debug","code":"double average = total / scores.length;","fix":"double average = (double) total / scores.length;","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Exercises","teach":"These exercises use the array skills together: create an array, read and update by index, stay inside length - 1, traverse, and search.","questions":[{"q":"What should you be able to do before leaving the arrays lesson?","options":["Create an array, access and update by index, loop through every element, and write a basic linear search","Resize a Java array by assigning length","Start indexes at 1","Use length() on an array"],"answer":0,"explain":"The quiz section lists those four skills. The exercises are where they are combined.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which update replaces only index 2?","options":["values.length = 8;","values[2] = 8;","values = 8;","values[values.length] = 8;"],"answer":1,"explain":"The index selects one element. Assigning to the array name or to length is not that update.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A search should report the index or say the value is absent. Which signal matches the lesson?","options":["Return the length when it is absent","Throw out the first element","Return the index, or -1 when the scan finishes with no match","Return 0 when it is absent"],"answer":2,"explain":"-1 is the lesson's not-found signal because it is not a valid index.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which traversal prints every element of prices?","options":["for (int i = 1; i \u003c= prices.length; i++) println(prices[i]);","println(prices.length);","prices[0] only","for (int i = 0; i \u003c prices.length; i++) println(prices[i]);"],"answer":3,"explain":"Start at 0 and stop before length. That visits each price once.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"values is {4, 7, 1}. After values[1] = 9, what is the array?","options":["4, 9, 1","9, 7, 1","4, 7, 9","4, 7, 1, 9"],"answer":0,"explain":"Index 1 is the middle element. The other positions stay put.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the last index of {4, 7, 1}?","options":["4","2","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"length is 3, so the last index is 2.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Linear search for 7 in {4, 7, 1} returns what?","options":["0","7","1","-1"],"answer":2,"explain":"7 is at index 1.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Linear search for 5 in {4, 7, 1} returns what?","options":["0","3","5","-1"],"answer":3,"explain":"5 is not an element, so the not-found signal remains.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the total of {4, 7, 1}?","options":["12","3","7","471"],"answer":0,"explain":"Traversal can add every element: 4 + 7 + 1.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which element is values[values.length - 1] when values is {4, 7, 1}?","options":["7","1","4","3"],"answer":1,"explain":"length - 1 is 2, and index 2 holds 1.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need the index of the largest value, not the value itself. Which loop fits?","options":["A single read of length","A search that stops at the first element","A classic for loop that remembers the index whenever a larger value appears","An enhanced loop, because it also provides the index"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tracking a position needs the index variable. The enhanced loop does not give one.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The last element is never printed. What is the header doing?","options":["It starts at 1 and skips only index 0","length is 0","println skips the last value on its own","i \u003c values.length - 1 stops one element early"],"answer":3,"explain":"The last valid index is length - 1, so the test must allow i to become that index. i \u003c length does.","kind":"debug","code":"for (int i = 0; i \u003c values.length - 1; i++) {\n    System.out.println(values[i]);\n}","fix":"for (int i = 0; i \u003c values.length; i++) {\n    System.out.println(values[i]);\n}","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_collections_framework_interactive.html","code":"08","name":"Collections framework","sections":[{"title":"1. Why does Java need a Collections Framework?","teach":"A to-do app needs to store, add, remove, find, and sometimes order tasks. The Collections Framework supplies ready-made structures such as lists, sets, queues, and maps, plus reusable operations such as sorting and searching. One container is not the right tool for every job.","questions":[{"q":"What does the Collections Framework give you?","options":["Ready-made structures and reusable operations","One container that is best for every job","A replacement for variables","Only arrays of length 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lists, sets, queues, and maps are different tools. Sorting and searching come with them.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the kitchen analogy?","options":["The framework replaces methods","Different jobs use different containers","Every ingredient goes in one jar","A queue is the only container"],"answer":1,"explain":"Plates, labelled jars, and the cooking order are different kinds of storage.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which pair does the lesson put under the framework?","options":["Files and paths","try and catch","Data structures and algorithms","Only constructors"],"answer":2,"explain":"The structures organise items. The algorithms include sorting, searching, reversing, and finding a minimum.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why not write all of the storage yourself for a to-do app?","options":["Java cannot store more than one task","A List is illegal","Tasks must be static fields","The framework already supplies tested structures for those jobs"],"answer":3,"explain":"Add, remove, find, and process-in-order are the jobs the introduction names.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does the lesson say one collection fits every requirement?","options":["No. The container follows the job","Yes. ArrayList is required everywhere","Yes. A Map forbids every other type","Only arrays are collections"],"answer":0,"explain":"The later choice table exists because the jobs differ.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"You need tasks in order, unique tags, and a lookup from id to details. What is the lesson's starting point?","options":["Use a queue for the lookup","Choose a structure for each of those jobs","Store all three jobs in one int","Avoid the framework and use a single public field"],"answer":1,"explain":"Order, uniqueness, and key-value lookup are different.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which operation is named as a reusable algorithm?","options":["Throwing IOException","Reading a keyboard line with Scanner","Sorting","Declaring a class"],"answer":2,"explain":"Searching, reversing, and finding a minimum are in the same list.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A waiting line and a labelled jar solve different problems. Which lesson sentence is that?","options":["A queue keeps key-value pairs","A label is an index starting at 1","The framework has only arrays","You do not use one container for everything"],"answer":3,"explain":"The kitchen analogy is the introduction's picture.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What problem opens the lesson?","options":["A to-do app that must store and process tasks","A single println","A bank balance","A regex"],"answer":0,"explain":"The structures are motivated by that app, not by a definition alone.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Finding a task and processing tasks in order are which part of the story?","options":["Static fields","Jobs the framework's structures can take on","Syntax errors","File paths"],"answer":1,"explain":"The introduction lists those jobs before naming the families.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which list matches the structures named in the introduction?","options":["try, catch, and finally","class, field, and method","List, set, queue, and key-value map","Only array and String"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each one is for a different organisation of items.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Every task, tag, and id lookup is stored in one array because the program uses a single container. What does the introduction argue?","options":["An array is the Collections Framework","One container is always enough","The framework cannot store tasks","Pick a structure that matches each job"],"answer":3,"explain":"The kitchen does not put plates, ingredients, and the cooking order in one jar.","kind":"debug","code":"Object[] everything = new Object[3];\neverything[0] = tasks;\neverything[1] = tags;\neverything[2] = ids;","fix":"List\u003cString\u003e tasks = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();\nSet\u003cString\u003e tags = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\nMap\u003cInteger, String\u003e byId = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. The big family: interfaces and implementations","teach":"An interface is a promise about operations. An implementation is the class that does the work. List\u003cString\u003e tasks = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e() uses the List type and an ArrayList object. Map is in the framework but does not extend Collection. A Collection stores elements. A Map stores pairs.","questions":[{"q":"In List\u003cString\u003e tasks = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e(), what is List?","options":["The interface type","The concrete class","A primitive","A file name"],"answer":0,"explain":"ArrayList is the implementation. The variable depends on List.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is ArrayList in that line?","options":["An element","The concrete implementation","The interface","A method"],"answer":1,"explain":"new creates the object that performs the List operations.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the USB analogy?","options":["USB stores duplicate keys","The port is the object created by new","A port describes the connection. Many devices can use it","One device is the only implementation forever"],"answer":2,"explain":"Code that depends on List can work with a suitable List implementation.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which family is an ordered sequence that allows duplicates?","options":["Set","Map","Queue only","List, commonly ArrayList or LinkedList"],"answer":3,"explain":"The mental map gives List that model.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does Map relate to the Collection interface?","options":["Map is in the Collections Framework and does not extend Collection","Map extends Collection","Collection extends Map","They are the same interface"],"answer":0,"explain":"A Map stores pairs. A Collection stores individual elements.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which line matches the sample?","options":["Collection\u003cString\u003e tasks = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();","List\u003cString\u003e tasks = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();","ArrayList\u003cString\u003e tasks = new List\u003c\u003e();","List\u003cString\u003e tasks = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":1,"explain":"The variable is the interface. The object is an ArrayList.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"tasks.add(\"Read\") and tasks.add(\"Practise Java\") use which promised operation?","options":["offer","a constructor named add","add, from the List the variable depends on","put"],"answer":2,"explain":"The interface promise is what the sample calls.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which implementation pair belongs to Set in the mental map?","options":["ArrayList and LinkedList","HashMap and TreeMap","ArrayDeque only","HashSet, LinkedHashSet, and TreeSet"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Set row is unique elements.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which implementation pair belongs to Map?","options":["HashMap, LinkedHashMap, and TreeMap","ArrayList and LinkedList","HashSet and TreeSet","PriorityQueue only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Map row is key-to-value lookup.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can a method accept List\u003cString\u003e instead of ArrayList\u003cString\u003e?","options":["The method then accepts a Map","Callers can pass a suitable List implementation","List can be constructed with new List\u003c\u003e()","ArrayList is not a List"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is the USB idea applied to the parameter.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A method needs individual elements, not pairs. Which interface family fits the warning?","options":["Set used as a pair store","Queue used as a key-value store","Collection, not Map","Map, because it extends Collection"],"answer":2,"explain":"The section's small warning separates elements from pairs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The code constructs the interface with new List\u003c\u003e(). What should be constructed?","options":["HashMap, assigned to List","a Set, with no change to new List","the element \"Read\"","An implementation such as ArrayList"],"answer":3,"explain":"List is the promise. ArrayList is a class the sample constructs.","kind":"debug","code":"List\u003cString\u003e tasks = new List\u003c\u003e();","fix":"List\u003cString\u003e tasks = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. List: an ordered sequence","teach":"A List keeps order, indexes from 0, and allows duplicates. add appends. get reads an index. set replaces an index. remove can remove by index or by value. size is the number of elements. Milk, Bread, Milk has size 3, and get(0) is Milk.","questions":[{"q":"What does a List keep?","options":["A sequence, including duplicates","Unique elements only","Key-value pairs","No indexes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seat index 0 is the first seat. Two bookings may share a name.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where do List indexes start?","options":["At size","0","1","-1"],"answer":1,"explain":"get(0) is the first element.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does add do in the sample?","options":["Sorts the list","Rejects a duplicate","Appends an item","Replaces index 0"],"answer":2,"explain":"The second Milk is allowed.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does set(index, value) do?","options":["Appends the value","Removes the index","Looks up a key","Replaces the item at that index"],"answer":3,"explain":"The position stays. The element changes.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does size() report for Milk, Bread, Milk?","options":["3","2","1","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Duplicates count. There are three positions.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does get(0) return after adding Milk, then Bread, then Milk?","options":["null","Milk","Bread","2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Index 0 is the first add.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does get(2) return in that list?","options":["The size","An exception, because duplicates are removed","Milk","Bread"],"answer":2,"explain":"Index 2 is the third add, the second Milk.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which structure fits a shopping list that may contain Milk twice?","options":["HashSet","HashMap with no values","A queue that forbids duplicates","ArrayList"],"answer":3,"explain":"ArrayList is the lesson's List implementation for an ordered sequence.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"set(1, \"Eggs\") runs on Milk, Bread, Milk. What is index 1?","options":["Eggs","Bread","Milk","Eggs appended as a fourth item"],"answer":0,"explain":"set replaces. It does not append.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration matches the sample style?","options":["Map\u003cString, String\u003e shopping = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();","List\u003cString\u003e shopping = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();","List\u003cString\u003e shopping = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();","Set\u003cString\u003e shopping = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":1,"explain":"The variable is a List. The object is an ArrayList.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"remove is described as remove(index) or remove(value). What do those forms share?","options":["They both reject duplicates on add","They look up a key","They take an element out of the sequence","They both sort the list"],"answer":2,"explain":"The section's point is that a List can delete, not only append.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A second Milk is rejected with a Set, but the shopping list must keep both. Which type matches the section?","options":["HashSet","TreeSet","TreeMap","List\u003cString\u003e, implemented by ArrayList"],"answer":3,"explain":"Order and duplicates are the List rules.","kind":"debug","code":"Set\u003cString\u003e shopping = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\nshopping.add(\"Milk\");\nshopping.add(\"Milk\");","fix":"List\u003cString\u003e shopping = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();\nshopping.add(\"Milk\");\nshopping.add(\"Milk\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Set: uniqueness without duplicate entries","teach":"A Set keeps unique elements. Adding java, beginner, and java again leaves size 2. A second add does not create a duplicate. HashSet does not promise order. LinkedHashSet keeps insertion order. TreeSet keeps elements sorted. A count of appearances needs a Map, not a Set.","questions":[{"q":"What does a second tags.add(\"java\") do?","options":["It does not create another element","It increases the size to 2","It sorts the set","It throws because java is a keyword"],"answer":0,"explain":"The guest-list analogy records who is present, not how many times they signed.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is tags.size() after java, beginner, and java?","options":["0","2","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"beginner is the second distinct tag.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which Set promises no particular iteration order?","options":["TreeSet","ArrayList","HashSet","LinkedHashSet"],"answer":2,"explain":"Do not print a HashSet and expect insertion order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which Set keeps insertion order?","options":["HashSet","TreeSet","PriorityQueue","LinkedHashSet"],"answer":3,"explain":"Choose it when the first-seen order matters.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which Set keeps elements sorted?","options":["TreeSet","HashSet","LinkedHashSet","ArrayDeque"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sorted order is TreeSet's job in this section.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"java is added twice to a new HashSet. What is size()?","options":["4","1","2","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"One distinct tag.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A, B, A, C, B are added. What is size()?","options":["2","1","3","5"],"answer":2,"explain":"A, B, and C are the distinct values.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need the number of times java appears. What does the lesson say to use?","options":["A HashSet's size, even when other tags exist","contains","TreeSet's first element","A counter or a Map"],"answer":3,"explain":"A Set answers presence. It does not store a repeat count.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration matches the sample?","options":["Set\u003cString\u003e tags = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();","Set\u003cString\u003e tags = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();","List\u003cString\u003e tags = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();","Map\u003cString, String\u003e tags = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":0,"explain":"The variable is the Set interface. The object is a HashSet.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Order is part of the requirement and the items must stay unique. What should you avoid relying on?","options":["add","HashSet's iteration order","TreeSet when you need sorted order","LinkedHashSet when you need insertion order"],"answer":1,"explain":"The section says to choose LinkedHashSet or TreeSet when order matters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which question does contains answer for a Set?","options":["How many times it was added","The sorted position in a HashSet","Whether the element is present","Its index"],"answer":2,"explain":"Membership is separate from a count.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The code uses a HashSet and then expects the print order java then beginner. What is the mismatch?","options":["add sorted the tags","size is 1","beginner is a duplicate of java","HashSet does not promise that order"],"answer":3,"explain":"Use LinkedHashSet for insertion order, or TreeSet for sorted order.","kind":"debug","code":"Set\u003cString\u003e tags = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\ntags.add(\"java\");\ntags.add(\"beginner\");","fix":"Set\u003cString\u003e tags = new LinkedHashSet\u003c\u003e();\ntags.add(\"java\");\ntags.add(\"beginner\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Queue and Deque: items waiting their turn","teach":"A common queue is FIFO. offer adds. poll returns and removes the head, or null if the queue is empty. peek returns the head without removing it, or null if empty. After Arun, Maya, and Kavi, poll returns Arun and the next peek returns Maya. A PriorityQueue uses priority rather than arrival. push then pop on an ArrayDeque is LIFO: Products is popped before Home.","questions":[{"q":"What does FIFO mean?","options":["The first item offered is the first item polled","The last item offered is polled first","Items are polled by name","peek sorts the queue"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arun joined first, so poll returns Arun.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does poll do on a non-empty queue?","options":["Sorts the queue","Returns and removes the head","Returns the head and leaves it there","Returns the last item"],"answer":1,"explain":"peek is the call that does not remove.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do poll and peek return when the queue is empty?","options":["false","an empty String","null","0"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both are defined that way in this section.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a PriorityQueue use instead of plain arrival order?","options":["FIFO only","The length of the name","Index 0 of an array that the lesson says you must manage","Priority or natural ordering"],"answer":3,"explain":"The triage analogy serves the most urgent case first.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do push and pop make an ArrayDeque act like?","options":["A stack: last in, first out","A FIFO queue","A Map","A sorted TreeSet"],"answer":0,"explain":"Home is pushed, then Products. pop returns Products.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"offer Arun, Maya, then Kavi. What does poll return?","options":["null","Arun","Maya","Kavi"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arun is the head.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After that poll, what does peek return?","options":["Kavi","null","Maya","Arun"],"answer":2,"explain":"Maya is now the head, and peek does not remove her.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"peek is called before any poll. What does it return, and does Arun stay?","options":["Arun, and Arun is removed","Maya, and Arun stays","null, because peek requires poll","Arun, and Arun stays at the head"],"answer":3,"explain":"peek inspects. poll removes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"history.push(\"Home\") then history.push(\"Products\"). What does pop return?","options":["Products","Home","null","Both, in offer order"],"answer":0,"explain":"The later push is the first pop.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration matches the FIFO sample?","options":["Queue\u003cString\u003e line = new PriorityQueue\u003c\u003e(); as the lesson's FIFO example","Queue\u003cString\u003e line = new ArrayDeque\u003c\u003e();","Queue\u003cString\u003e line = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();","Map\u003cString, String\u003e line = new ArrayDeque\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":1,"explain":"ArrayDeque is the class the FIFO sample constructs. PriorityQueue is the different case.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does offer do?","options":["Reads without removing","Sorts by priority","Adds an item and returns whether it was accepted","Removes the head"],"answer":2,"explain":"The three methods to learn are offer, poll, and peek.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The code uses peek and then expects the queue to have lost Arun. What did peek do?","options":["It removed Arun","It returned Maya","It emptied the queue","It returned Arun and left the queue unchanged"],"answer":3,"explain":"Removal is poll.","kind":"debug","code":"line.offer(\"Arun\");\nline.offer(\"Maya\");\nString next = line.peek();\n// comment expects Maya to be the new head","fix":"line.offer(\"Arun\");\nline.offer(\"Maya\");\nString next = line.poll();","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Map: look up a value by key","teach":"A Map stores key-value pairs. Keys are unique. Values may repeat. put replaces the value for an existing key. Notebook goes from 20 to 18. getOrDefault(\"Eraser\", 0) is 0. HashMap does not promise order. LinkedHashMap keeps insertion order. TreeMap keeps keys sorted.","questions":[{"q":"What is unique in a Map?","options":["The key","The value","Both, always","Neither"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two products can have the same quantity. They cannot share a key.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the second put(\"Notebook\", 18) do?","options":["Adds 20 and 18","Replaces 20 with 18","Stores both quantities","Removes Notebook"],"answer":1,"explain":"get then returns 18. There is one Notebook key.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Eraser was never stored. What does getOrDefault(\"Eraser\", 0) return?","options":["20","an exception","0","null"],"answer":2,"explain":"The fallback is the second argument.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which Map keeps keys sorted?","options":["HashMap","LinkedHashMap","HashSet","TreeMap"],"answer":3,"explain":"HashMap is the general lookup. LinkedHashMap keeps insertion order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the labelled-bin analogy?","options":["The product name is the key and the quantity is the value","The quantity is the key","The bin's position is the only data","A second bin is created for the same label"],"answer":0,"explain":"Updating stock changes the value for that label.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does get(\"Notebook\") return after the two puts in the sample?","options":["null","18","20","38"],"answer":1,"explain":"The later put wins.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Pen is 50 and Notebook is 18. How many keys is that?","options":["1","68","2","3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Two labels. Replacing Notebook did not add a key.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration matches the sample?","options":["Map\u003cString, Integer\u003e stock = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();","Set\u003cString\u003e stock = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();","List\u003cInteger\u003e stock = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();","Map\u003cString, Integer\u003e stock = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":3,"explain":"HashMap is the implementation. Map is the interface.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need keys in insertion order. Which class fits this section?","options":["LinkedHashMap","HashMap","TreeMap","ArrayList"],"answer":0,"explain":"TreeMap would sort the keys instead.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two products both have quantity 50. Is that allowed?","options":["Only in a TreeSet","Yes. Values may repeat","No. The second put is rejected","No. The quantities are added"],"answer":1,"explain":"The keys differ, so both entries exist.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call walks every pair in the section's loop?","options":["length","poll","entrySet, then getKey and getValue","iterator on the Map as if it were a List of ints"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson shows a for loop over Map.Entry.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"put is called twice for Notebook and the comment says the stock is 20 + 18. What does get return?","options":["38","20","0","18"],"answer":3,"explain":"put replaces. It does not add the quantities.","kind":"debug","code":"stock.put(\"Notebook\", 20);\nstock.put(\"Notebook\", 18);\nSystem.out.println(stock.get(\"Notebook\")); // comment expects 38","fix":"stock.put(\"Notebook\", 20);\nstock.put(\"Notebook\", 18);\nSystem.out.println(stock.get(\"Notebook\")); // 18","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Generics, iteration and the Iterator","teach":"List\u003cString\u003e means the list holds Strings. names.add(42) is a compile error. An enhanced for loop visits each element when you do not need the index. An Iterator can remove the current item safely. names.remove inside an enhanced for loop can throw ConcurrentModificationException. Collections is the utility class. Collection is the interface.","questions":[{"q":"What does List\u003cString\u003e tell the compiler?","options":["The list is meant to hold Strings","The list may hold any value until it runs","The list keeps unique Strings only","The list cannot be looped"],"answer":0,"explain":"names.add(\"Maya\") is allowed. names.add(42) is rejected at compile time.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the labelled-container analogy?","options":["A label makes the list a Set","The type argument stops the wrong kind of value going in","Generics run after the file is deleted","A label sorts the list"],"answer":1,"explain":"A box marked for books should not receive a bicycle.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When is the enhanced for loop the lesson's easiest visit?","options":["When you need the index for set","When the collection is a Map pair and you ignore entrySet","When you need each element and do not need to manage the index","When you must remove items inside that loop"],"answer":2,"explain":"The loop variable is the element.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is an Iterator for?","options":["Sorting by a Comparator","Constructing an ArrayList","Catching IOException","Walking one item at a time, including a safe remove of the current item"],"answer":3,"explain":"hasNext, next, and remove are the sample.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the difference between Collections and Collection?","options":["Collections is a utility class of static algorithms. Collection is an interface","They are spellings of the same type","Collection is the utility class","Collections extends Map"],"answer":0,"explain":"The capital S is the class with sort, reverse, and similar methods.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What happens to names.add(42) when names is List\u003cString\u003e?","options":["The list becomes a Set","The code does not compile","42 is stored","42 becomes \"42\" automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integer is not String.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which loop prints every name when the index is irrelevant?","options":["names.getKey()","while (names.poll() != null) as the List example","for (String name : names)","for (int i = 0; i \u003c= names.size(); i++)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The enhanced loop is the lesson's easiest visit.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A name starts with A and must be removed during the walk. Which call matches the sample?","options":["names.remove(name) inside the enhanced for loop","names.set(0, name)","Collections.reverse","iterator.remove()"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sample removes through the Iterator, not through the list, during that loop.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What can names.remove(name) inside an enhanced for loop cause?","options":["ConcurrentModificationException","A sorted list","A compile error because remove does not exist at all","An empty String"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says to use the Iterator, removeIf, or a separate collection of items to delete.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call is a utility algorithm rather than a List method in the sample?","options":["new ArrayList\u003c\u003e()","Collections.sort(names)","names.add(\"Maya\")","names.size()"],"answer":1,"explain":"Collections.reverse is shown beside it. contains is called on the list.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"boolean found = names.contains(\"Maya\"). What does found mean?","options":["The size","Whether Maya was removed","Whether Maya is an element","Maya's index"],"answer":2,"explain":"contains is the search in the algorithm list.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"names.add(42) is written against List\u003cString\u003e. What is a correction that stores the digits as text?","options":["names.add(42) left as it is","names.put(42)","new HashSet\u003cString\u003e().add(42)","names.add(\"42\")"],"answer":3,"explain":"The type argument is String. The quoted text is a String.","kind":"debug","code":"List\u003cString\u003e names = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();\nnames.add(42);","fix":"List\u003cString\u003e names = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();\nnames.add(\"42\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"8. Build a tiny task manager","teach":"The task manager is a browser simulation. A List keeps tasks in display order. A Set collects the distinct categories. A real app might also use a Map from task id to details. Home, Work, and Home again are two categories.","questions":[{"q":"What does the lab actually run?","options":["A visual simulation in the browser","A Java compiler","A write to a task file","A queue of files"],"answer":0,"explain":"The page demonstrates collection behaviour. It does not replace the Java types.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which structure imitates the task order?","options":["An array that deletes duplicates","A List","A Set, because order is the Set promise","A Map of categories only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The tasks stay in display order, and the same category may appear on more than one task.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which structure imitates the category area?","options":["A Queue","A Comparator","A Set of distinct categories","A List that keeps Home twice"],"answer":2,"explain":"Home and Work are two categories when Home was used twice.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What extra structure does the lesson mention for a real app?","options":["A second Set of every duplicate task","An array of length 1","A static int for the names","A Map from task id to task details"],"answer":3,"explain":"Lookup by id is the Map job.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Adding a task and choosing its category uses which pair?","options":["The List grows by the task, and the Set records the category once","Both reject the task","The Set keeps task order","The Map stores the category twice"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two collections answer two questions.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Categories Home, Work, and Home are recorded in a Set. How many categories are stored?","options":["0","2","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The second Home is not a new element.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Three tasks are added in order: Read, Practise, Review. What does the List remember?","options":["Only the unique first letters","One task, because the List rejects later adds","That order, including three tasks","The sorted order Practise, Read, Review"],"answer":2,"explain":"Display order is the List's job.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You mark a task done and the tasks stay on screen in place. Which collection is suited to that ordered display?","options":["HashSet","PriorityQueue as the lesson's display list","TreeMap of the button labels","List"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lab keeps the tasks in their display order.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two tasks share the category Study. The category Set's size is 1. What does that show?","options":["The Set stored Study once","The List deleted a task","Study is a key whose value is the task name in this lab","The simulation writes a file"],"answer":0,"explain":"Uniqueness applies to the categories, not to the task sequence.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A real lookup \"details for task 14\" fits which lesson suggestion?","options":["Queue poll","Map from id to details","Set of ids, with the details discarded","List index equal to the id with no other choice"],"answer":1,"explain":"The section names that Map as the fast lookup.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Clear all would empty the simulated tasks. Which List fact still applies to the Java version of \"remove everything you stored\"?","options":["size stays at the historical maximum","clear turns the List into a Set","The List's size can return to 0 after the tasks are removed","A List cannot remove"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lab button is a simulation of removing the stored tasks.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Categories are stored in a List, so Home, Work, Home displays as three categories. Which type matches the lab?","options":["Another List","A FIFO queue of categories","An array that keeps every repeat","A Set"],"answer":3,"explain":"The distinct-category area is the Set.","kind":"debug","code":"List\u003cString\u003e categories = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();\ncategories.add(\"Home\");\ncategories.add(\"Work\");\ncategories.add(\"Home\");","fix":"Set\u003cString\u003e categories = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\ncategories.add(\"Home\");\ncategories.add(\"Work\");\ncategories.add(\"Home\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"9. Sorting and Comparators","teach":"Collections.sort on Kavi, Arun, Maya uses natural String order: Arun, Kavi, Maya. A Comparator is the rule for which object comes first. comparingInt on score sorts students by score. The same objects can be sorted with a different rule. The race analogy is the rule, not the runners.","questions":[{"q":"What does a Comparator supply?","options":["The rule for which object comes before another","A database connection","A second copy of every student","The index of the first student only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Different rules can order the same objects differently.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does Collections.sort(names) use for Strings?","options":["Reverse order, unless a comparator is passed","Natural String order","The order of the original list, unchanged","Score order"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arun comes before Kavi, and Kavi comes before Maya.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the race analogy?","options":["The sort deletes the slowest runner","Natural order means arrival order for every class","The runners are the objects. The comparator is the rule","The comparator is a runner"],"answer":2,"explain":"Lower time or higher score would be two different rules.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does Comparator.comparingInt(s -\u003e s.score) compare?","options":["The name's length, in that particular call","The list's size","The student's index","The score field"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson uses it to order students by score.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can the same students be ordered in more than one way?","options":["Yes. Use a different comparator","No. A class has one permanent order","Only by copying them into a Set","Only with bubble sort"],"answer":0,"explain":"The section's point is that the rule is separate from the objects.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Start with Kavi, Arun, Maya. After Collections.sort, what is first?","options":["The original Kavi, because sort returns a copy in this lesson","Arun","Kavi","Maya"],"answer":1,"explain":"Natural String order puts Arun first. The lesson sorts the list itself.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the natural order of those three names?","options":["Maya, Kavi, Arun","Kavi, Maya, Arun","Arun, Kavi, Maya","Kavi, Arun, Maya"],"answer":2,"explain":"A before K before M.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A rule puts the higher score first. Low has 40 and High has 90. Who is first?","options":["Low","They stay in construction order","Neither, because a Comparator sorts names only","High"],"answer":3,"explain":"The comparator decides. The higher score wins for that rule.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call matches the String sample?","options":["Collections.sort(names)","names.bubbleSort()","Arrays.sort(names) on the ArrayList in this sample","Comparator.sort()"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sample passes the list to Collections.sort.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call matches the score rule in the lesson?","options":["names.contains(score)","students.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(s -\u003e s.score))","students.sort() with no rule, as the score example","Collections.reverse(students) as the score example"],"answer":1,"explain":"comparingInt builds the comparison from the score.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the comparator not a field of Student in the lesson's story?","options":["sort changes the class name","Natural order forbids a score","It is the ordering rule applied to the students, and another rule can be applied later","A Student cannot have fields"],"answer":2,"explain":"The runners stay themselves. The judging rule can change.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Students are sorted with Collections.sort and no comparator, and the comment expects score order. Strings would use natural order. What does the lesson use for score?","options":["HashSet","poll","a second List that sorts by being constructed","Comparator.comparingInt on the score"],"answer":3,"explain":"A class of students does not automatically sort by score.","kind":"debug","code":"Collections.sort(students); // comment expects score order","fix":"students.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(s -\u003e s.score));","debug":true}]},{"title":"10. Choose a collection based on the requirement","teach":"Start with ArrayList for an ordered sequence that allows duplicates, HashSet for uniqueness without an order requirement, LinkedHashSet for insertion order, HashMap for lookup, ArrayDeque as a queue for arrival order, ArrayDeque as a deque for stack behaviour, PriorityQueue for priority, and TreeMap for sorted keys.","questions":[{"q":"Ordered tasks, duplicates allowed. What is the starting choice?","options":["ArrayList","HashSet","TreeMap","PriorityQueue"],"answer":0,"explain":"ArrayList is the general-purpose List.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Unique values, and order does not matter. What is the starting choice?","options":["ArrayDeque","HashSet","ArrayList","LinkedHashSet, which is for when order does matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"HashSet is membership and uniqueness.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Unique values in insertion order. What is the starting choice?","options":["TreeSet","HashMap","LinkedHashSet","HashSet"],"answer":2,"explain":"LinkedHashSet preserves insertion order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Process people in arrival order. What is the starting choice?","options":["TreeMap","HashSet","PriorityQueue, which is the priority case","ArrayDeque used as a Queue"],"answer":3,"explain":"offer, poll, and peek are the queue operations.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the beginner default for key-value lookup?","options":["HashMap","TreeMap","ArrayList","LinkedHashSet"],"answer":0,"explain":"TreeMap is the later choice when the keys must be sorted.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Last item first, such as undo. What does the table suggest?","options":["TreeSet","ArrayDeque used as a Deque, with push and pop","ArrayDeque used as a FIFO queue","HashMap"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stack behaviour is the deque row.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The next item must be the highest priority, not the oldest. What fits?","options":["HashSet","ArrayList as a priority structure in this table","PriorityQueue","ArrayDeque as a FIFO queue"],"answer":2,"explain":"The head is based on priority or order.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Keys must stay sorted. What fits?","options":["HashMap","LinkedHashMap","HashSet","TreeMap"],"answer":3,"explain":"TreeMap maintains key order.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A shopping list must keep order and may repeat Milk. Which interface does the practice answer name?","options":["List, commonly an ArrayList","Set","Map","Queue"],"answer":0,"explain":"Duplicates and indexes are the List.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Unique email addresses, order irrelevant. Which pairing fits?","options":["TreeMap","Set, commonly a HashSet","List, commonly an ArrayList","ArrayDeque as a stack"],"answer":1,"explain":"The practice answer is a Set of Strings.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which sentence is the section's day-one advice?","options":["Write your own list before using the framework","Use one HashSet for queues and maps too","ArrayList, HashSet, HashMap, and ArrayDeque cover the ordinary starting jobs","Always start with TreeMap and PriorityQueue"],"answer":2,"explain":"The table's beginner default is those four.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Arrival order is implemented with a PriorityQueue, and the first offered person is expected to leave first. Which type matches FIFO?","options":["PriorityQueue","HashSet","TreeMap","ArrayDeque used as a Queue"],"answer":3,"explain":"PriorityQueue does not promise arrival order.","kind":"debug","code":"Queue\u003cString\u003e line = new PriorityQueue\u003c\u003e();\nline.offer(\"Arun\");\nline.offer(\"Maya\");","fix":"Queue\u003cString\u003e line = new ArrayDeque\u003c\u003e();\nline.offer(\"Arun\");\nline.offer(\"Maya\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"11. Practice problems","teach":"Shopping order with duplicates is a List. Unique emails are a HashSet when order does not matter. poll removes the head and peek does not. Both return null on an empty queue. A Map looks up by key. List\u003cString\u003e catches a wrong-type add at compile time. Do not remove from an ArrayList inside an enhanced for loop. FIFO starts with ArrayDeque. A Comparator is the ordering rule.","questions":[{"q":"Which interface fits a shopping list that keeps order and may repeat an item?","options":["List","Set","Map","Queue, because a queue forbids duplicates in this lesson"],"answer":0,"explain":"ArrayList is the usual implementation.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Unique email addresses, order irrelevant. Which collection?","options":["PriorityQueue","A Set, commonly HashSet","ArrayList","ArrayDeque as a stack"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Set rejects the second copy of an address.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How do poll and peek differ?","options":["They both sort","peek returns null whenever poll would return a value","poll returns and removes the head. peek returns it and leaves it","They both remove"],"answer":2,"explain":"On an empty queue, both return null.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is a Map different from a List?","options":["A Map has indexes and allows duplicate keys","A List stores pairs","They are the same interface","A List is an indexed sequence. A Map stores pairs and looks up by key"],"answer":3,"explain":"Notebook to 18 is a lookup. Milk at index 0 is a position.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the main benefit of List\u003cString\u003e over a raw list?","options":["The compiler can reject an element of the wrong type","The list becomes unique","add starts at index 1","Generics close files"],"answer":0,"explain":"names.add(42) fails to compile.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Offer Arun then Maya. poll runs. What is returned?","options":["Both","Arun","Maya","null"],"answer":1,"explain":"FIFO removes the head.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The same queue is only peeked. What is returned, and who remains at the head?","options":["null","Maya, and the queue is empty","Arun remains, and peek returns Arun","Maya, and Arun is removed"],"answer":2,"explain":"peek does not remove.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"An empty queue is polled. What is the result?","options":["0","an empty String","the last offer, still remembered","null"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson states null for an empty poll and an empty peek.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why avoid names.remove inside an enhanced for over an ArrayList?","options":["It can throw ConcurrentModificationException","remove deletes the list type","The loop cannot see Strings","Collections.sort is called automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"Use the Iterator's remove, removeIf, or a separate pass.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which implementation starts a FIFO queue in the practice answer?","options":["TreeMap","ArrayDeque, used as a Queue","PriorityQueue","HashSet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arrival order is the queue row.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is a Comparator used for?","options":["Duplicate keys","Closing a file","A comparison rule, such as score or name length","A database connection"],"answer":2,"explain":"The same students can be ordered by a different rule.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The enhanced loop calls names.remove(name). What does the lesson use instead during the walk?","options":["names.get(0)","poll","a HashSet add","iterator.remove()"],"answer":3,"explain":"Removing through the list inside that loop is the case that can throw.","kind":"debug","code":"for (String name : names) {\n    if (name.startsWith(\"A\")) {\n        names.remove(name);\n    }\n}","fix":"Iterator\u003cString\u003e iterator = names.iterator();\nwhile (iterator.hasNext()) {\n    String name = iterator.next();\n    if (name.startsWith(\"A\")) {\n        iterator.remove();\n    }\n}","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_sorting_techniques_practice_problems.html","code":"09","name":"Sorting","sections":[{"title":"1. Why do we sort?","teach":"Sorting arranges values into an order. Ascending means smallest to largest. Descending is the reverse. The lesson first learns bubble sort and selection sort, then the library methods used in real programs.","questions":[{"q":"What does ascending order mean?","options":["Smallest to largest","Largest to smallest","The original order","Alphabetical only when the values are numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson turns 7, 2, 9, 1, 5 into 1, 2, 5, 7, 9.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is descending order for 9, 7, 5, 2, 1?","options":["Sorted by index 0 only","Already descending: largest to smallest","Ascending","Unsorted"],"answer":1,"explain":"Descending is the reverse of ascending.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a sorting algorithm do?","options":["It changes the type of the values","It removes duplicates only","It follows precise steps to arrange data into a chosen order","It searches for one target and stops"],"answer":2,"explain":"The height-line analogy compares items and places them until the line is in order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where does sorting help, according to the lesson?","options":["Reading keyboard input","Declaring a class","Catching IOException","Ranking marks, showing prices in order, arranging names, and preparing reports"],"answer":3,"explain":"Those are the lesson's examples. The goal is to understand the steps before using library sort.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which order is ascending for 7, 2, 9, 1, 5?","options":["1, 2, 5, 7, 9","9, 7, 5, 2, 1","7, 2, 9, 1, 5","5, 1, 9, 2, 7"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ascending puts the smallest value first and the largest last.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which order is descending for those numbers?","options":["7, 9, 5, 2, 1","9, 7, 5, 2, 1","1, 2, 5, 7, 9","2, 1, 5, 7, 9"],"answer":1,"explain":"Descending is largest to smallest.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Is 1, 2, 5, 7, 9 ascending or descending?","options":["Neither, because it contains 5","Both","Ascending","Descending"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each value is smaller than the one after it, through to the largest at the end.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the mixed row 7, 2, 9, 1, 5 not sorted ascending?","options":["It has five numbers","It starts with 7, and ascending must start with 7","Indexes start at 1","A later value is smaller than an earlier one, such as 2 before 7's position is resolved"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ascending for this row begins at 1, not at 7.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What should you learn before relying on a library sort, according to the goal?","options":["The logic of bubble sort and selection sort","Every sorting algorithm in the JDK source","How to avoid arranging data","Scanner nextLine"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson wants the idea of comparing and placing, then the built-in methods.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Arranging names A to Z is which kind of order?","options":["An unsorted guest list","Ascending alphabetical order","Descending numeric order","A search"],"answer":1,"explain":"A to Z is the same idea as smallest to largest, applied to letters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which row is the lesson's after-sorting picture?","options":["9, 7, 5, 2, 1 as the ascending picture","2, 7, 1, 9, 5","1, 2, 5, 7, 9","7, 2, 9, 1, 5"],"answer":2,"explain":"The after picture in the lesson is the ascending row.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The result is listed as 9, 7, 5, 2, 1 and labelled ascending. What is mislabelled?","options":["The numbers cannot be sorted","9 is the smallest","A sort must always start with 7","That row is descending. Ascending would be 1, 2, 5, 7, 9"],"answer":3,"explain":"Check the direction of the comparison. Largest-first is descending.","kind":"debug","code":"ascending: 9, 7, 5, 2, 1","fix":"ascending: 1, 2, 5, 7, 9","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Bubble sort","teach":"Bubble sort compares neighbours and swaps them when they are out of order. One ascending pass moves the largest unsorted value to the end. A swap needs a temporary variable.","questions":[{"q":"What does one pass of bubble sort move toward the end, in the ascending version?","options":["The largest unsorted value","The smallest value","A new empty slot","The index 0 only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Neighbours are compared, and the large value drifts to the right like a bubble.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When does the ascending version swap two neighbours?","options":["Only when they are equal","When the left one is greater than the right one","When the left one is smaller","Always"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's test is numbers[j] \u003e numbers[j + 1].","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does a swap need a temporary variable?","options":["It sorts the array by itself","It stores the length","It holds one value while the other is moved into its place","It increases the array length"],"answer":2,"explain":"Without the spare cup, the first value is overwritten and lost.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What are the two loops doing?","options":["The inner loop reads input","The outer loop is a switch","Both loops search for -1","The inner loop walks neighbours. The outer loop repeats passes so later large values can also finish moving"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each pass places one more value at the end. Later passes ignore that settled end.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After one ascending bubble pass over 7, 5, 3, 1, what is the array?","options":["5, 3, 1, 7","1, 3, 5, 7","7, 5, 3, 1","1, 7, 5, 3"],"answer":0,"explain":"7 swaps with 5, then 5's right side continues until 7 reaches the end. One pass does not finish the whole sort.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What do a and b hold after int temp = a; a = b; b = temp; if a was 4 and b was 9?","options":["both are 9","a is 9 and b is 4","a is 4 and b is 9","both are 4"],"answer":1,"explain":"temp saved 4, a received 9, and b received the saved 4.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair is swapped in 2, 9 when the order is ascending?","options":["9 only","The length and the index","No swap. 2 is already smaller than 9","2 and 9, because any pair swaps"],"answer":2,"explain":"A swap happens when the left neighbour is greater, not whenever two numbers exist.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"In 4, 1, which value moves right on an ascending comparison?","options":["1","Both stay, because the smaller one is on the right already... 4 is greater, so 4 moves right","The index","4"],"answer":3,"explain":"4 \u003e 1, so they swap and 4 moves toward the end.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does the inner loop stop earlier on later passes?","options":["The end already holds placed large values, so those positions are not compared again","The array shrinks its length field","break always runs on the first pair","Indexes start at 1 after pass one"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's inner bound is length - 1 - i.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is lost if you assign a = b before saving a?","options":["Nothing, because b still has a copy of a","The old value of a","The length","Only the index"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once a is overwritten, its old value is gone unless temp kept it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which test matches one ascending neighbour comparison?","options":["if (numbers[j] == numbers[j + 1]) swap","if (j \u003e numbers.length) swap","if (numbers[j] \u003e numbers[j + 1]) swap","if (numbers[j] \u003c numbers[j + 1]) swap, for ascending"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greater-on-the-left means the pair is out of ascending order.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is lost in this swap?","options":["The value 9","The variable names","Only the semicolon","The value 4. After a = b, both variables hold 9 because nothing saved 4"],"answer":3,"explain":"The spare variable has to capture 4 before a is overwritten.","kind":"debug","code":"int a = 4;\nint b = 9;\na = b;\nb = a;","fix":"int a = 4;\nint b = 9;\nint temp = a;\na = b;\nb = temp;","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Selection sort","teach":"Selection sort finds the smallest value in the unsorted part and swaps it into the next position. minIndex remembers where that smallest value is, not the value alone.","questions":[{"q":"What does selection sort place next?","options":["The smallest value in the still-unsorted part","The largest neighbour only","A random value","The length"],"answer":0,"explain":"The line analogy picks the shortest remaining person and puts them in the next finished place.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does minIndex track?","options":["The last index of the whole array, forever","The index of the smallest value found so far in the unsorted part","The smallest value's text name","The number of swaps only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The scan updates minIndex when it sees a smaller element. The swap then uses that index.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where does the first placement go?","options":["Index 1, leaving index 0 unsorted","length","Index 0","The last index"],"answer":2,"explain":"The outer loop starts i at 0, so the first chosen minimum is swapped into the front.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How is this different from bubble sort?","options":["They compare the same pair only once and stop","Selection cannot use a temporary variable","Bubble finds the minimum index directly","Selection chooses a minimum and places it. Bubble repeatedly swaps neighbours"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both can sort, but the lesson presents different steps.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Selection sort is arranging 7, 2, 9 into ascending order. What is placed first?","options":["2","7","9","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"2 is the smallest of the three, so it is swapped into index 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After that first placement of 7, 2, 9, what is index 0?","options":["1","2","7","9"],"answer":1,"explain":"The minimum moves to the front. The other two values occupy the remaining unsorted part.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The unsorted part starts at i = 2 and holds 8, 3. What does minIndex point at before the swap?","options":["The value 2","The length","The index of 3","The index of 8"],"answer":2,"explain":"3 is smaller than 8, so the scan remembers 3's index.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why start the inner scan at i + 1?","options":["Index 0 is always skipped","The array has no element at i","minIndex cannot start at i","Index i is the first candidate, so the scan looks at the positions after it"],"answer":3,"explain":"minIndex begins as i, then j walks the rest of the unsorted part.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which update matches the lesson?","options":["if (numbers[j] \u003c numbers[minIndex]) minIndex = j;","if (numbers[j] \u003c numbers[minIndex]) minIndex = numbers[j];","minIndex = numbers.length;","if (numbers[j] \u003e numbers[minIndex]) minIndex = j; for an ascending minimum"],"answer":0,"explain":"minIndex stores an index. The comparison looks for a smaller value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After the scan, what is swapped?","options":["Nothing, if minIndex is an index","The value at i and the value at minIndex","Every neighbour","The length and the first value"],"answer":1,"explain":"The chosen minimum moves into position i, and whatever was at i moves to the old minimum's place.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Selection sort of 3, 1, 2 places which value at index 0?","options":["2","0","1","3"],"answer":2,"explain":"1 is the minimum of the whole array, so the first placement is 1.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"minIndex is assigned the value numbers[j] instead of j. What goes wrong?","options":["The smallest value must not be read","j cannot be an index","Selection sort swaps neighbours only","minIndex should be a position. Storing the value makes the later swap use the wrong index"],"answer":3,"explain":"The swap needs numbers[minIndex], so minIndex has to be j when numbers[j] is smaller.","kind":"debug","code":"if (numbers[j] \u003c numbers[minIndex]) {\n    minIndex = numbers[j];\n}","fix":"if (numbers[j] \u003c numbers[minIndex]) {\n    minIndex = j;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Built-in Java sorting","teach":"In ordinary programs, use the library. Arrays.sort sorts an int array in place into ascending order. Collections.sort sorts a list. reverseOrder makes that list descending.","questions":[{"q":"Which call sorts a primitive int array into ascending order?","options":["Arrays.sort(marks)","Collections.sort(marks) on an int array","marks.order()","marks.sort() as if the array were a list"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson uses Arrays.sort for the array and Collections.sort for the ArrayList.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does Arrays.sort change?","options":["Only index 0","The order of the original array","A new array, leaving the original untouched, in this lesson's description","The length"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says Arrays.sort(array) changes the order in the original array.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does the lesson sort an ArrayList from highest to lowest?","options":["Collections.sort(prices) with no comparator, which is ascending","prices.reverse() as the only shown method","Collections.sort(prices, Collections.reverseOrder())","Arrays.sort(prices)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The no-comparator list sort is ascending. reverseOrder flips that order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What should everyday Java prefer?","options":["Always bubble sort","A new selection sort for every list","Not sorting","The standard library, rather than a hand-written bubble sort"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson introduces the algorithms so you understand them, then points real programs at the library.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After Arrays.sort on {72, 45, 90, 61}, what is the array?","options":["45, 61, 72, 90","90, 72, 61, 45","72, 45, 90, 61","61, 90, 45, 72"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ascending numeric order starts at 45 and ends at 90.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After Arrays.sort on {8, 3, 6, 1, 4}, what is the array?","options":["4, 1, 6, 3, 8","1, 3, 4, 6, 8","8, 6, 4, 3, 1","8, 3, 6, 1, 4"],"answer":1,"explain":"The practice problem's sorted array is 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, and the original array is the one that changed.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A list holds 500, 120, 300. After Collections.sort, what is first?","options":["300","0","120","500"],"answer":2,"explain":"The no-comparator sort is ascending, so 120 comes first.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After Collections.sort(prices, Collections.reverseOrder()) on 500, 120, 300, what is first?","options":["120","300","500, 120, 300 unchanged","500"],"answer":3,"explain":"reverseOrder puts the highest value at index 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which import belongs with Arrays.sort?","options":["java.util.Arrays","java.util.Scanner","java.io.IOException","No import, because sort is a keyword"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson imports java.util.Arrays for the array call.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Does the list sort leave the original list's order alone?","options":["Only when the list has one element","No. The lesson says sorting a list also changes that list","Yes. It always returns a copy","Only reverseOrder copies it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both of the lesson's library sorts rearrange the collection you pass.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You have an int[] and need ascending order. Which call is the lesson's?","options":["Collections.reverseOrder(values)","values.selectionSort()","Arrays.sort(values)","Collections.sort(values)"],"answer":2,"explain":"An array uses Arrays.sort. Collections.sort is shown for the list.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Collections.sort is called on an int[] marks. What is the mismatch?","options":["The array is already descending only","sort cannot order numbers","reverseOrder is required for ascending","An int array is sorted with Arrays.sort. Collections.sort is the list method in this lesson"],"answer":3,"explain":"Match the method to the container: array or list.","kind":"debug","code":"Collections.sort(marks);","fix":"Arrays.sort(marks);","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Compare the approaches","teach":"Bubble sort and selection sort are O(n\u00b2) learning algorithms. Arrays.sort and Collections.sort are the library tools for real arrays and lists. O(n\u00b2) means the work grows much faster as the number of items grows.","questions":[{"q":"What is bubble sort's core idea?","options":["Compare adjacent pairs and swap","Find the minimum of the unsorted section and place it","Call the library","Look up a key"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is the comparison table's bubble row. Selection has the minimum-placement idea.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is selection sort's core idea?","options":["Keep items in insertion order","Find the minimum in the unsorted section and place it","Swap only neighbours","Use Arrays.sort"],"answer":1,"explain":"The table separates the two learning algorithms.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the lesson say O(n\u00b2) means?","options":["The data is already sorted","The library method is slower than bubble sort for every size","As the number of items grows, the work grows much faster, roughly with the square of the size","The algorithm always takes two steps"],"answer":2,"explain":"If the list doubles, a quadratic algorithm may do about four times as much comparing.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which approach does the table recommend for sorting an array in a real program?","options":["Bubble sort","Selection sort","A hand-written swap with no loop","Arrays.sort"],"answer":3,"explain":"The library row is the practical choice. Bubble and selection are for learning the steps.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A list grows from 20 items to a much larger size. What does the lesson want you to remember about bubble sort?","options":["Its O(n\u00b2) work becomes expensive compared with the library sort","It becomes faster than Arrays.sort because the list is longer","O(n\u00b2) means it checks only two items","The algorithm changes into a Map"],"answer":0,"explain":"The comparison section is about growth, not about a different result.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which row matches Collections.sort?","options":["Search for -1","Standard library method for lists","Compare adjacent pairs","Find the minimum and place it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The table gives Collections.sort the list job.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two algorithms both end with 1, 2, 5, 7, 9. What can still differ?","options":["The type of the numbers","The first index","The steps and the amount of work","Whether ascending means smallest first"],"answer":2,"explain":"Different approaches can solve the same ordering problem.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which learning algorithm places a chosen minimum at the next index?","options":["Bubble sort","Arrays.sort as a learning exercise in the first sections","indexOf","Selection sort"],"answer":3,"explain":"Selection's scan chooses the minimum. Bubble moves a large neighbour toward the end.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which learning algorithm can move a large value to the end in one pass of neighbour swaps?","options":["Bubble sort","Selection sort's first placement, which puts the small value at the front","A Map lookup","equals"],"answer":0,"explain":"One bubble pass settles the current largest at the end. Selection's first move settles the smallest at the front.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need to sort marks in an int array in production code. What does the table say to use?","options":["reverseOrder on the array directly","Arrays.sort","A new bubble-sort method","Selection sort because O(n\u00b2) is required"],"answer":1,"explain":"The practical array row is Arrays.sort.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the table list as the typical time for both bubble sort and selection sort?","options":["The same cost as a single equals call","No growth as n grows","O(n\u00b2)","Constant time for any size"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both learning algorithms are quadratic in the lesson's table. The library methods are the efficient choice.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A program sorts ten thousand ints with a fresh bubble sort because the author remembers only the learning section. What does the comparison section say to use instead?","options":["Another nested bubble loop","Selection sort, because it is not O(n\u00b2)","A Scanner","Arrays.sort"],"answer":3,"explain":"The learning algorithms explain the idea. The library is the everyday tool.","kind":"debug","code":"bubbleSort(values);","fix":"Arrays.sort(values);","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Practice problems","teach":"Sort an int array with Arrays.sort, sort a list descending with reverseOrder, and read the smallest and largest after an ascending sort from the ends of the array.","questions":[{"q":"What is the ascending result of {8, 3, 6, 1, 4}?","options":["1, 3, 4, 6, 8","8, 6, 4, 3, 1","8, 3, 6, 1, 4","4, 1, 6, 3, 8"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arrays.sort rearranges the same array into that order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does the practice display the highest mark first in an ArrayList?","options":["A bubble pass","marks.sort(Collections.reverseOrder()) or Collections.sort with reverseOrder","Arrays.sort(marks) on the list","An ascending Collections.sort and then read index 0 as the highest"],"answer":1,"explain":"Highest first is descending order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After an ascending sort, where are the smallest and largest values?","options":["Largest at index 0","They are removed","Smallest at the start and largest at the end","Both at index 0"],"answer":2,"explain":"Once 1, 3, 4, 6, 8 is sorted, the ends are the extremes.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What must you import for Collections.reverseOrder in the practice solution?","options":["java.util.Scanner","java.io.File","Nothing, it is in java.lang automatically in the lesson's note","java.util.Collections"],"answer":3,"explain":"The practice solution says to import java.util.Collections.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After Arrays.sort on {8, 3, 6, 1, 4}, what is index 0?","options":["1","8","4","3"],"answer":0,"explain":"The smallest value moves to the front.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the last element of that sorted array?","options":["6","8","1","4"],"answer":1,"explain":"The largest value is at the end of an ascending sort.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A list is 20, 50, 10. After reverseOrder, what is index 0?","options":["20","0","50","10"],"answer":2,"explain":"Descending order starts with the greatest value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Does Arrays.sort on {8, 3, 6, 1, 4} leave the variable values in the old order?","options":["Yes. sort returns a copy and values stays mixed","Only the first element changes","The length becomes 1","No. The same array becomes 1, 3, 4, 6, 8"],"answer":3,"explain":"The practice prints the same array after the call.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair of ends reports the lowest and highest after ascending sort of {12, 5, 29, 8, 17}?","options":["5 and 29","12 and 17","29 and 5, if 29 is called the lowest","8 and 12"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sorted ascending, the array starts at 5 and ends at 29.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Equal values such as three 4s in a sort should end up where?","options":["Turned into one element so the length shrinks","Next to each other in the sorted order","Removed","At both ends only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sorting groups equal values in the position their order requires. It does not delete duplicates; a Set would do that.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call is the practice's array solution?","options":["values.reverseOrder();","selectionSort as the required production call","Arrays.sort(values);","Collections.sort(values);"],"answer":2,"explain":"The shown solution sorts the int array with Arrays.sort.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The highest mark was supposed to be first, but Collections.sort(marks) put the lowest first. What is missing?","options":["Arrays.sort on the list","A bubble pass before the library call","index 0, which lists do not have","reverseOrder. The plain sort is ascending"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ascending index 0 is the smallest. Descending needs the reverse comparator.","kind":"debug","code":"Collections.sort(marks);","fix":"Collections.sort(marks, Collections.reverseOrder());","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_string_manipulation_split_replace.html","code":"10","name":"Strings","sections":[{"title":"1. What is a String?","teach":"A String is a sequence of characters. Indexes start at 0. length() is the number of characters, and the last index is length() - 1. Strings are immutable: methods such as toUpperCase return a new String.","questions":[{"q":"What is a String?","options":["A sequence of characters, such as a name or a sentence","A single int","A mutable list of numbers","A comment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's necklace picture puts one character in each position, counted from 0.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the first index of a Java String?","options":["-1","0","1","The length"],"answer":1,"explain":"In \"Nituna\", N is at index 0 and a is at index 5.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How are length and the last index related?","options":["The last index is length()","The last index is always 0","The last index is length() - 1","They are the same number"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Nituna\" has length 6 and last index 5.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does immutable mean for a String in this lesson?","options":["The text can be edited in place by charAt","length() changes the characters","A String variable cannot be assigned again","Methods such as toUpperCase return a new String and do not change the original automatically"],"answer":3,"explain":"You can point the variable at a new String. Calling toUpperCase does not rewrite the old one unless you use the result.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"For String name = \"Nituna\"; what is name.length()?","options":["6","5","7","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"There are six characters. The last index is 5, not the length.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the last index of \"Nituna\"?","options":["4","5","6","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"last index = length - 1 = 5, the position of a.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Nituna\".charAt(0) return?","options":["a","6","N","i"],"answer":2,"explain":"Index 0 is the first character.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Java\".charAt(1) return?","options":["J","v","4","a"],"answer":3,"explain":"J is 0, a is 1, v is 2, and a is 3.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"String word = \"hi\"; word = word.toUpperCase(); What is word?","options":["HI","hi","Hi","hiHI"],"answer":0,"explain":"The result is stored back into word. The method itself returned a new String, and the assignment keeps it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"String word = \"hi\"; word.toUpperCase(); What is word if the result is not stored?","options":["i","hi","HI","h"],"answer":1,"explain":"The new String was ignored. The variable still refers to the original text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call gets the last character of \"Java\"?","options":["charAt(1) for every word","length()","charAt(word.length() - 1)","charAt(word.length())"],"answer":2,"explain":"The last index of \"Java\" is 3, which is length 4 minus 1, and that character is a.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is wrong with this last-character read?","options":["length() is 0 for Java","charAt starts at 1","Strings cannot store Java","charAt(length()) uses an index equal to the length, which is one past the last character"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Java\" has indexes 0, 1, 2, and 3. Index 4 does not exist.","kind":"debug","code":"word.charAt(word.length())","fix":"word.charAt(word.length() - 1)","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Essential String methods","teach":"length counts characters, charAt reads one index, equals compares text, equalsIgnoreCase ignores case, trim removes leading and trailing spaces, and == is not the lesson's content comparison.","questions":[{"q":"Which call compares the characters in two Strings?","options":["equals","== as the lesson's content comparison","length","trim"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says to use equals for text content. == checks whether two references are the same object, which is a different question.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does equalsIgnoreCase do?","options":["It finds an index","It compares text while ignoring letter case","It removes spaces","It changes the original to uppercase"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"java\".equalsIgnoreCase(\"JAVA\") is true. equals would be false because the case differs.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does trim remove?","options":["The first character","Uppercase letters","Leading and trailing spaces","Spaces between words"],"answer":2,"explain":"\" hi \".trim() is \"hi\". The spaces inside a sentence are kept.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does contains answer?","options":["The index of that sequence","A new String with the text removed","The length","Whether a sequence occurs in the String"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Java\".contains(\"av\") is true. indexOf is the method that returns a position.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"Java\".length() return?","options":["4","3","5","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"J, a, v, and a are four characters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \" hi \".trim() return?","options":[" h i ","hi"," hi ","hi "],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the outer spaces go. The word itself remains.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Hi\".equals(\"Hi\") return?","options":["Hi","0","true","false"],"answer":2,"explain":"The characters match exactly, including case.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Hi\".equals(\"hi\") return?","options":["true","Hi","1","false"],"answer":3,"explain":"equals is case sensitive. equalsIgnoreCase would return true here.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"java\".equalsIgnoreCase(\"JAVA\") return?","options":["true","false","JAVA","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"The letters match when case is ignored.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"file.csv\".endsWith(\".csv\") return?","options":["9","true","false","csv"],"answer":1,"explain":"endsWith checks the final characters. startsWith checks the beginning.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call checks the beginning of report.csv?","options":["\"report.csv\".trim()","\"report.csv\".charAt(0) as a full-word test","\"report.csv\".startsWith(\"report\")","\"report.csv\".endsWith(\"report\")"],"answer":2,"explain":"startsWith is the beginning test. charAt(0) would only see r.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"This comparison uses == for text content. What should it use?","options":["length","trim","toUpperCase, which compares by itself","equals, because == does not ask whether the characters match"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's content comparison is equals. == answers a different question about references.","kind":"debug","code":"if (answer == \"yes\") {","fix":"if (answer.equals(\"yes\")) {","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Extract text with substring()","teach":"substring(start) takes from that index to the end. substring(start, end) includes start and stops before end. \"PROGRAM\".substring(1, 4) is ROG.","questions":[{"q":"What does substring(2) return on \"PROGRAM\"?","options":["OGRAM","PR","PROGRAM","OG"],"answer":0,"explain":"One argument means from that index through the end. Index 2 of PROGRAM is O.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does substring(1, 4) include?","options":["Indexes 0 and 1","Indexes 1, 2, and 3. Index 4 is the boundary and is not included","Indexes 1, 2, 3, and 4","Only index 4"],"answer":1,"explain":"The end index is exclusive. On PROGRAM, those characters are R, O, and G.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which seat picture matches substring(1, 4)?","options":["Take every seat including 4","Start at seat 4 and go backwards","Take seats 1, 2, and 3, and stop before seat 4","Take seat 4 only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson uses a row of seats: the end number is where you stop, not a seat you take.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When is a two-argument substring valid?","options":["The end index must be smaller than the start","The end must equal the length plus one","Any negative end is allowed","The start is less than or equal to the end, and the end is not past the length"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says the indexes must fit the String.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"PROGRAM\".substring(1, 4) return?","options":["ROG","PROG","ROGR","OG"],"answer":0,"explain":"P is 0, R is 1, O is 2, G is 3, and R is 4. The slice stops before 4.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"PROGRAM\".substring(2) return?","options":["GRAM","OGRAM","PR","ROG"],"answer":1,"explain":"From index 2 through the end is O, G, R, A, M.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Java\".substring(1, 3) return?","options":["Ja","v","av","ava"],"answer":2,"explain":"Indexes 1 and 2 are a and v. Index 3 is the stopping boundary.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Java\".substring(0, 1) return?","options":["Ja","a","Java","J"],"answer":3,"explain":"Index 0 is included and index 1 is not, so the result is the first character.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How do you take the first three characters of \"report\"?","options":["substring(0, 3)","substring(1, 3)","substring(0, 2)","substring(3)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Indexes 0, 1, and 2 are r, e, and p. substring(3) would be the remainder, port.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"\"report.csv\" should yield the extension csv. Which call matches the practice problem's idea?","options":["trim()","Take the text after the dot","substring(0, 3)","charAt(0)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The extension is the portion after the separator. For report.csv, that portion is csv. The exact call can find the dot and start just after it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is \"Chennai\".substring(0, 3)?","options":["Ch","ennai","Che","Chen"],"answer":2,"explain":"Indexes 0, 1, and 2 are C, h, and e. The end boundary 3 is not included.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The programmer wanted ROG but wrote substring(1, 3). What did the end index do?","options":["substring cannot start at 1","PROGRAM has no G","The start index is exclusive","It stopped before index 3, so G was left out. The end should be 4"],"answer":3,"explain":"substring(1, 3) is RO. Including G means stopping at 4.","kind":"debug","code":"\"PROGRAM\".substring(1, 3)","fix":"\"PROGRAM\".substring(1, 4)","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Replace text with replace()","teach":"replace returns a new String. It is case sensitive and treats the search text as literal text. The original String stays unchanged unless you use the returned value. replaceAll treats its pattern as a regex.","questions":[{"q":"What does replace return?","options":["A new String with the replacements. It does not change the original by itself","The original String rewritten in place","An int count only","A String array"],"answer":0,"explain":"message stays \"I like tea\" while updated becomes \"I like coffee\".","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Is replace case sensitive?","options":["Only after trim","Yes. \"tea\" does not match \"Tea\"","No. Case is ignored","Only for single characters"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says the search is case sensitive. \"Tea time\".replace(\"tea\", \"coffee\") does not change Tea.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How do character and text replacement differ in the call?","options":["Single quotes mean a regex","Double quotes change the original in place","Single quotes replace a character. Double quotes replace literal text","They are the same call"],"answer":2,"explain":"replace('a', 'o') is a character replacement. replace(\"cat\", \"dog\") is literal text.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does replace differ from replaceAll?","options":["replaceAll keeps the original and replace does not","They both ignore case","replaceAll can only remove spaces","replace treats the search as literal text. replaceAll treats its first argument as a regular expression"],"answer":3,"explain":"Regex is the next lesson. This section's replace is the literal tool.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"String message = \"I like tea\"; String updated = message.replace(\"tea\", \"coffee\"); What is updated?","options":["I like coffee","I like tea","coffee","I like tea coffee"],"answer":0,"explain":"The returned String contains the replacement.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After that replacement, what is message?","options":["empty","I like tea","I like coffee","tea"],"answer":1,"explain":"The original variable was not assigned the result, so it still refers to the old text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Tea time\".replace(\"tea\", \"coffee\") return?","options":["Tea time coffee","Coffee time","Tea time","coffee time"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tea and tea differ in case, so the literal search does not match.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"cat cat\".replace(\"cat\", \"dog\") return?","options":["dog cat","cat dog","cat cat","dog dog"],"answer":3,"explain":"Literal replace changes each matching piece of text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call replaces the letter a with o in \"Java\"?","options":["replace('a', 'o')","replace(\"a\", 'o') with mixed quote styles as one argument pair","replaceAll(\"a\") with no replacement","substring(1, 2)"],"answer":0,"explain":"A one-character substitution uses char values in single quotes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need the new text kept in message. What must you do with the result?","options":["Call length()","Assign it, such as message = message.replace(\"tea\", \"coffee\")","Call replace and do not assign it","Use == to copy it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The method returns a new String. The variable changes only if you store that result.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which task should wait for the regex lesson instead of using replace?","options":["Replacing one letter a","Keeping the original unchanged","A pattern such as any digit, rather than the exact characters you typed","Replacing the exact word tea"],"answer":2,"explain":"replace is for literal text. A pattern is replaceAll or the regex methods.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The code prints message after replace and expects coffee, but still shows tea. Why?","options":["replace cannot change tea","coffee is a regex","Strings are printed before the call automatically","The returned String was not stored. println(message) shows the original"],"answer":3,"explain":"Assign the result, or print the call's return value.","kind":"debug","code":"message.replace(\"tea\", \"coffee\");\nSystem.out.println(message);","fix":"System.out.println(message.replace(\"tea\", \"coffee\"));","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Split text with split()","teach":"split divides a String into a String array. The delimiter is a regular expression. A dot is special, so a literal dot is split(\"\\\\.\"). A comma split of red,blue gives two elements.","questions":[{"q":"What does split return?","options":["A String array of the pieces","One String with the delimiter removed only at the start","An int count","The original String rewritten in place"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"red,blue\".split(\",\") produces an array whose elements are red and blue.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the delimiter?","options":["A comment","The pattern that marks where to cut","The first piece","The length of the array"],"answer":1,"explain":"The comma, space, or hyphen in the lesson's table is the cut mark.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is a plain dot dangerous in split?","options":["A dot always means a comma","split ignores one-character delimiters","split receives a regular expression, and a dot means any character","Java cannot store dots"],"answer":2,"explain":"A literal dot is written as a regex escape. The lesson shows split(\"\\\\.\") in Java source.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is names[0] after \"Ravi,Anu,Kumar\".split(\",\")?","options":["Anu","Kumar","Ravi,Anu,Kumar","Ravi"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first piece is the text before the first comma. The array length is 3.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"red,blue\".split(\",\") produce?","options":["A String array with red and blue","One String, redblue","A number array","red, blue including the comma as an element"],"answer":0,"explain":"The comma is the cut. It does not remain as its own element.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the array length after \"red,blue,green\".split(\",\")?","options":["6","3","2","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"There are three pieces.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is index 1 after \"one two three\".split(\" \")?","options":["three","one two","two","one"],"answer":2,"explain":"A space cuts the words. Index 0 is one, index 1 is two, and index 2 is three.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What are the pieces of \"2026-10-10\".split(\"-\")?","options":["2026-10-10 as one piece","2026 and 1010","10 only","2026, 10, and 10"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each hyphen is a cut.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which Java call splits on a literal dot?","options":["split(\"\\\\.\")","split(\".\")","split(\",\")","split(\"dot\")"],"answer":0,"explain":"In a Java string, the backslash is escaped, so the source shows two backslashes before the dot. The regex then sees a literal dot.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"\"a|b|c\".split(\"\\\\|\") is the lesson's pattern for a pipe. What is index 0?","options":["b","a","a|b|c","|"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pipe is the delimiter once it is escaped as a regex. The first piece is a.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How many pieces does \"red,blue\".split(\",\") have?","options":["3","0","2","1"],"answer":2,"explain":"One comma cuts one string into two pieces.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does \"a.b.c\".split(\".\") fail to cut on each dot?","options":["split cannot return an array","A dot is always a comma","The string has no dots","The dot is a regex for any character, so it is not a literal dot. Escape it"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Java source for a literal dot is split(\"\\\\.\").","kind":"debug","code":"\"a.b.c\".split(\".\")","fix":"\"a.b.c\".split(\"\\\\.\")","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Interactive text lab","teach":"The lab demonstrates trim, toUpperCase, toLowerCase, length, and split on a sentence. The result is a new value. The page simulates the operations and does not compile Java.","questions":[{"q":"What does the lab do with your sentence?","options":["It shows the result of the String operation you choose","It compiles a .java file","It changes every String in the JDK","It sorts the characters with bubble sort"],"answer":0,"explain":"The controls demonstrate typical results. They are a browser simulation.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which operation removes only the outer spaces?","options":["split(\" \")","trim","toUpperCase","length"],"answer":1,"explain":"trim is the leading-and-trailing tool. split on a space cuts the sentence into words.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does toUpperCase return?","options":["An int","A String array","A new String in uppercase, leaving the original unchanged unless you store the result","The same String object rewritten in place"],"answer":2,"explain":"Immutability from the first section still applies in the lab.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does length return in the lab?","options":["The number of spaces only","The last index","The first word","The number of characters, not the number of words"],"answer":3,"explain":"length() counts characters, including spaces. split(\" \") is what separates words.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"java\".toUpperCase() return?","options":["JAVA","java","Java","j"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every letter becomes uppercase. The original \"java\" is unchanged if you do not store the result.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"JAVA\".toLowerCase() return?","options":["J","java","JAVA","Java"],"answer":1,"explain":"toLowerCase is the opposite case change.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \" hi \".trim() return?","options":["hi ","h i","hi"," hi"],"answer":2,"explain":"The spaces around the word are removed.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Java makes text\".split(\" \") give as its first element?","options":["makes","text","Java makes","Java"],"answer":3,"explain":"A space is the delimiter. The first word is index 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is \"Java makes text\".length()?","options":["15","3","14","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Count every character, including the two spaces: Java(4) + space + makes(5) + space + text(4) = 15.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You run toUpperCase and forget to use the result. What does the text box's original variable still hold?","options":["The length","The original casing","The uppercase text automatically","An empty string"],"answer":1,"explain":"The method returned a new String. The lab's idea matches the lesson: use the result.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which lab operation answers \"how many characters\"?","options":["trim","toLowerCase","length","split(\" \")"],"answer":2,"explain":"length counts characters. split answers how the sentence breaks into words.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The lab user expects the stored sentence to become uppercase, but the code discards the result. What is missing?","options":["A call to split","trim before every uppercase call","length() instead of toUpperCase()","An assignment of toUpperCase()'s return value"],"answer":3,"explain":"sentence = sentence.toUpperCase(); keeps the new text.","kind":"debug","code":"sentence.toUpperCase();\nSystem.out.println(sentence);","fix":"sentence = sentence.toUpperCase();\nSystem.out.println(sentence);","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Practice problems","teach":"Count characters with length, read ends with charAt, compare case with equalsIgnoreCase, take an extension after the dot, and remember that replace returns a new String.","questions":[{"q":"How do you count the characters in \"Chennai\"?","options":["\"Chennai\".length()","\"Chennai\".length","\"Chennai\".charAt(7)","\"Chennai\".split(\" \")"],"answer":0,"explain":"The practice answer is 7. length() is the method, with parentheses.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How do you get the first and last characters of \"Java\"?","options":["equals(\"Java\")","charAt(0) and charAt(length() - 1)","charAt(1) and charAt(length())","substring(1, 4)"],"answer":1,"explain":"J is index 0. a is index 3, which is length 4 minus 1.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How do you compare \"JAVA\" and \"java\" while ignoring case?","options":["==","trim","equalsIgnoreCase","equals, which would be false here"],"answer":2,"explain":"The practice solution prints true from equalsIgnoreCase.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"From \"report.csv\", what is the extension the practice asks for?","options":["report","report.csv",".csv including the dot, if the problem asked for csv without it","csv"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sample extracts the text after the dot, csv.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is \"Chennai\".length()?","options":["7","6","8","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"C-h-e-n-n-a-i is seven characters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the last character of \"Java\"?","options":["4","a","J","v"],"answer":1,"explain":"charAt(3) is a.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"JAVA\".equalsIgnoreCase(\"java\") return?","options":["JAVA","0","true","false"],"answer":2,"explain":"The letters match when case is ignored.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"JAVA\".equals(\"java\") return?","options":["true","java","1","false"],"answer":3,"explain":"equals requires the same case. This is why the practice uses equalsIgnoreCase.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"\"report.csv\".substring(\"report.csv\".indexOf('.') + 1) yields what?","options":["csv","report","csv with a leading dot",".csv"],"answer":0,"explain":"indexOf finds the dot, and starting one position later takes csv.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"I like tea\".replace(\"tea\", \"coffee\") return?","options":["I like tea coffee","I like coffee","I like tea","coffee tea"],"answer":1,"explain":"replace returns the new sentence. The original literal is unchanged.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which split gives the two colours in \"red,blue\"?","options":["substring(0, 3)","trim()","split(\",\")","split(\".\")"],"answer":2,"explain":"The comma is the delimiter between red and blue.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The extension read uses substring(0, 3) on report.csv. Why is that the wrong piece?","options":["csv is the first three characters","substring cannot use indexOf","The dot is a space","It takes the first three characters, rep, instead of the text after the dot"],"answer":3,"explain":"The extension starts after the dot, not at index 0.","kind":"debug","code":"\"report.csv\".substring(0, 3)","fix":"\"report.csv\".substring(\"report.csv\".indexOf('.') + 1)","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_contains_pattern_matching_regex.html","code":"11","name":"Pattern matching and regex","sections":[{"title":"1. Searching for text","teach":"contains reports whether a sequence occurs. startsWith and endsWith check an edge. These are exact text tools, used before regular expressions.","questions":[{"q":"What does contains return?","options":["A boolean: whether the sequence occurs","The index of the sequence","A new array of matches","The original String in uppercase"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Java for Beginners\".contains(\"Java\") is true. contains(\"Python\") is false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the book analogy say contains is for?","options":["Matching any three digits","Finding whether a word occurs, without understanding every sentence","Finding the page number","Replacing the word"],"answer":1,"explain":"This section is the simple search. Position is the next section, and patterns come after that.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does startsWith check?","options":["Any middle occurrence only","A regular expression","The beginning of the String","The ending"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"report.csv\".startsWith(\"report\") is true.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does endsWith(\".csv\") ask?","options":["Whether .csv occurs anywhere","Whether the String is exactly five digits","The index of the dot","Whether the String finishes with those exact characters"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's file check is endsWith(\".csv\").","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"Java for Beginners\".contains(\"Java\") return?","options":["true","false","0","Java"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sequence Java occurs at the start, so contains is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"Java for Beginners\".contains(\"Python\") return?","options":["Python","false","true","-1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Those characters do not occur.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"report.csv\".endsWith(\".csv\") return?","options":["csv","11","true","false"],"answer":2,"explain":"The file name finishes with .csv.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"report.csv\".startsWith(\"data\") return?","options":["true","report","0","false"],"answer":3,"explain":"The beginning is report, not data.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which method is the simplest check that a file name ends in .csv?","options":["endsWith(\".csv\")","matches(\".csv\"), which would require the whole String to be .csv","indexOf(-1)","contains(\"report\")"],"answer":0,"explain":"The quiz in this lesson gives endsWith as the simple ending check. matches(\".csv\") asks whether the entire text is only .csv.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"\"catapult\".contains(\"cat\") is true. What question did contains answer?","options":["How many cats appear","Whether those letters occur, not where they start","The index 0","Whether the whole String is exactly cat"],"answer":1,"explain":"The position is indexOf's job. contains is only yes or no.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call checks the file prefix notes?","options":["\"notes.txt\".contains(\".txt\") as a prefix test","\"notes.txt\".matches(\"notes\")","\"notes.txt\".startsWith(\"notes\")","\"notes.txt\".endsWith(\"notes\")"],"answer":2,"explain":"startsWith is the prefix. matches(\"notes\") would fail because the whole name is notes.txt, not notes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The code uses contains to get the seat number of Java. What method answers where it is?","options":["endsWith","trim","toUpperCase","indexOf, because contains only returns true or false"],"answer":3,"explain":"contains says the word occurs. indexOf says where it starts.","kind":"debug","code":"int seat = title.contains(\"Java\");","fix":"int seat = title.indexOf(\"Java\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Find the position with indexOf()","teach":"indexOf returns the first matching index. lastIndexOf returns the last one. Both return -1 when there is no match. Index 0 means the match starts at the beginning.","questions":[{"q":"What does indexOf return when the text is missing?","options":["-1","0","null","The length"],"answer":0,"explain":"0 would mean the match starts at the first character. Missing text is -1.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a result of 0 mean?","options":["The String is empty","The match starts at the beginning, not that the search failed","The text is absent","The match is at the end"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson treats 0 as a real index. Absence is -1.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How do indexOf and lastIndexOf differ?","options":["lastIndexOf returns a boolean","indexOf counts matches","indexOf finds the first match. lastIndexOf finds the last match","They always return the same index"],"answer":2,"explain":"In \"Java is fun. Java is useful.\", the first Java is at 0 and the last Java is at 13.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the seat-number analogy?","options":["Both return the seat","indexOf asks only yes or no","contains returns -1","contains asks whether the person is in the theatre. indexOf asks which seat"],"answer":3,"explain":"This section adds the position that contains does not provide.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is \"Java is fun. Java is useful.\".indexOf(\"Java\")?","options":["0","13","-1","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first Java starts at the first character.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is that sentence's lastIndexOf(\"Java\")?","options":["8","13","0","-1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The second Java starts later. lastIndexOf reports that later index.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the sentence's indexOf(\"fun\")?","options":["-1","13","8","0"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's sample prints 8 for fun.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is indexOf(\"Python\") in that sentence?","options":["0","4","13","-1"],"answer":3,"explain":"Python does not occur, so the result is the missing-text signal.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is \"catapult and cat\".indexOf(\"cat\")?","options":["0","13","-1","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first cat is the start of catapult. indexOf does not skip a match just because more letters follow.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call finds the dot in report.csv?","options":["endsWith('.')","indexOf('.')","lastIndexOf(\"csv\") as the dot's index","contains('.') stored in an int"],"answer":1,"explain":"indexOf returns the position of the first dot. contains would only say that a dot exists.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can indexOf('.') + 1 start an extension?","options":["Adding 1 makes a missing dot become 0","lastIndexOf cannot find a dot","The dot's index plus one is the first character after the dot","indexOf returns the extension text"],"answer":2,"explain":"If the dot is found, the next index is where csv begins.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The code treats indexOf(\"Python\") == 0 as \"not found\". What comparison matches a missing word?","options":["== 0","== length()","equals(0)","== -1"],"answer":3,"explain":"0 is a real position at the start. The not-found result is -1.","kind":"debug","code":"if (text.indexOf(\"Python\") == 0) {\n    System.out.println(\"missing\");\n}","fix":"if (text.indexOf(\"Python\") == -1) {\n    System.out.println(\"missing\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. What is pattern matching?","teach":"A pattern describes a shape many strings can follow, such as any three digits. Exact search looks for one fixed piece of text. Regex is the compact pattern language.","questions":[{"q":"How is a pattern different from exact text search?","options":["A pattern describes a rule that many different strings can follow","A pattern can match only one fixed word","Exact search uses \\\\d","A pattern ignores the String"],"answer":0,"explain":"The receptionist accepts any five-digit room number, not one specific number.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is regex?","options":["A way to compile a class","A compact pattern language used to find, validate, or replace text","A Java array","The same thing as contains"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regular expressions describe shapes such as three digits, written \\\\d{3} in the lesson's Java pattern.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which example is exact search rather than a pattern?","options":["Accept any five-digit room number","Match one digit","Find the word cat","Find any three digits"],"answer":2,"explain":"cat is specific text. Three digits is a shape that 204 and 123 both follow.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the lesson's \\\\d{3} describe?","options":["The exact text d{3}","Three spaces","The whole alphabet","Three digits in a row"],"answer":3,"explain":"\\\\d is one digit, and {3} says exactly three of them.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which text follows the rule \"any three digits\"?","options":["204","cat","ab","room with no digits"],"answer":0,"explain":"204 is three digits. The exact word cat does not follow that numeric shape.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which task needs a pattern rather than contains?","options":["Find the exact word dragon","Accept any five-digit room number","Check whether the title contains Java","Check whether a file starts with report"],"answer":1,"explain":"The room number is not one fixed String. The other tasks look for exact text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"\"A123B\" contains three digits. Which description matches the lesson?","options":["indexOf returns the digits as a String","equals(\"A123B\") is a pattern","A pattern such as \\\\d{3} can recognise them inside the text","contains(\"123\") is the only possible tool, and patterns cannot see digits"],"answer":2,"explain":"The section's pattern example includes A123B for three digits.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is exact comparison a poor room-number check?","options":["It accepts every String","It always returns an index","Digits cannot be compared","It would accept only one specific number, not every five-digit number"],"answer":3,"explain":"The form accepts a shape. Exact text would require you to list each room.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which tool from earlier sections answers \"does the exact word Java occur\"?","options":["contains(\"Java\")","A five-digit pattern","\\\\d{3}","replaceAll"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exact occurrence is the simple search. A digit pattern answers a different question.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What kind of answer does a pattern validation usually become?","options":["The file path","Whether the text fits the rule","The Java compiler version","A sorted array"],"answer":1,"explain":"The reception desk either accepts the value or it does not. Later methods turn that into a boolean or a match.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which string does the exact search for cat match in the lesson's contrast?","options":["204","A123B","cat","cats, when the comparison is the whole string"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says whole-string exact comparison matches cat and does not match cats.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The code uses contains(\\\\d{3}) to mean three digits. Why is that the wrong tool?","options":["\\\\d{3} is already a call to contains","Patterns cannot describe digits","contains returns an index","contains looks for those exact characters, not for the digit pattern. A regex method is needed"],"answer":3,"explain":"The characters backslash, d, brace are not the same thing as \"any three digits.\"","kind":"debug","code":"text.contains(\"\\\\d{3}\")","fix":"text.matches(\".*\\\\d{3}.*\")","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Regex building blocks","teach":"\\\\d is one digit, \\\\w is a word character, and \\\\s is whitespace. A dot is any character. Quantifiers say how many: * is zero or more, + is one or more, ? is zero or one, and {3} is exactly three.","questions":[{"q":"Which pattern matches one digit?","options":["\\\\d","\\\\s","\\\\w","."],"answer":0,"explain":"\\\\d is a digit. \\\\s is whitespace, \\\\w is a word character, and a dot is any one character.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \\\\w match in the lesson?","options":["Any character, including a space","A letter, a digit, or an underscore","Only whitespace","Only a digit"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson calls \\\\w a word character. A space is \\\\s, not \\\\w.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does + mean?","options":["Exactly three","Any character","One or more of the previous item","Zero or one"],"answer":2,"explain":"\\\\d+ matches 8 and 204. * would also allow none, and {3} means exactly three.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does {3} mean?","options":["Three or more","At most three","A literal brace","Exactly three of the previous item"],"answer":3,"explain":"\\\\d{3} is exactly three digits, such as 204.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which pattern matches the digit 7?","options":["\\\\d","\\\\s","[A-Z]","abc"],"answer":0,"explain":"7 is one digit. [A-Z] is an uppercase letter, and abc is the literal letters abc.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pattern matches one uppercase English letter?","options":["[aeiou]","[A-Z]","[0-9]","\\\\s"],"answer":1,"explain":"[A-Z] is A through Z. [0-9] is a digit, and [aeiou] is a lowercase vowel.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pattern matches the word color and the word colour?","options":["colour only, as a pattern that rejects color","\\\\d+","colou?r","colou*r, which also allows colouuur"],"answer":2,"explain":"? means the u is optional. * would allow many u characters, which is more than the lesson's colour example.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pattern matches one or more digits, such as 8 and 204?","options":["\\\\d?","\\\\s+","\\\\d{3} only, which rejects 8","\\\\d+"],"answer":3,"explain":"\\\\d{3} is exactly three digits, so it fits 204 but not 8. + allows one or more.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does ab* match, among the lesson's examples?","options":["a, ab, and abbb","Only ab","Only b","Any digit"],"answer":0,"explain":"* means zero or more b characters after an a.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pattern is a literal abc rather than a digit rule?","options":["[0-9]","abc","\\\\d","\\\\d{3}"],"answer":1,"explain":"Letters without a slash are themselves. \\\\d is the digit token.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A space in a string should be described by which token?","options":["\\\\w",". as the lesson's whitespace token","\\\\s","\\\\d"],"answer":2,"explain":"\\\\s is whitespace. A dot matches many characters, not specifically space.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The pattern was supposed to mean one digit, but it was written as d. What is missing?","options":["A + quantifier, because one digit needs +","Quotes around the letter inside the pattern only","{3}","The regex digit token \\\\d, not the literal letter d"],"answer":3,"explain":"A plain d matches the letter d. \\\\d matches a digit such as 7.","kind":"debug","code":"pattern = \"d\"","fix":"pattern = \"\\\\d\"","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Java regex methods","teach":"String.matches checks the entire String. Pattern and Matcher are how you find a match inside longer text. matcher.find locates the next match. A five-digit PIN is \\\\d{5} against the whole text.","questions":[{"q":"What does String.matches check?","options":["Whether the entire String matches the pattern","Whether a matching part occurs anywhere","Only the first character","The index of the match"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"48291\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") is true. A longer sentence containing those digits is not a whole-string match.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which type pair finds a match inside longer text?","options":["nextInt","Pattern and Matcher","equals and length","trim and substring only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pattern.compile builds the pattern. matcher.find looks inside the text.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does matcher.find do?","options":["It splits on a comma","It replaces nothing and returns the length","It locates the next matching piece inside the text","It checks that the entire String is the pattern"],"answer":2,"explain":"The order-number example uses find to locate 104 and 208 inside a sentence.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does \"4829\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") fail?","options":["matches searches for a part and should have succeeded","4 is not a digit","The pattern means at most five","The whole String is four digits, not five"],"answer":3,"explain":"{5} on \\\\d requires the entire text to be exactly five digits.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"48291\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") return?","options":["true","false","48291","5"],"answer":0,"explain":"The whole String is five digits.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"4829\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") return?","options":["4","false","true","4829"],"answer":1,"explain":"Four digits are not five. matches does not accept a shorter String.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"A8291\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") return?","options":["A","-1","false","true"],"answer":2,"explain":"A is not a digit, so the whole String is not five digits.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Order numbers sit inside a sentence. Which approach matches the lesson?","options":["Call matches(\"\\\\d+\") on the whole sentence and expect the numbers","Use contains(\"\\\\d+\")","Use endsWith(\"104\")","Compile \\\\d+ and call matcher.find"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sentence is not made only of digits, so whole-string matches(\"\\\\d+\") is false. find looks inside it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pattern is a five-digit PIN?","options":["\\\\d{5}","\\\\d+","\\\\w{5}","\\\\d?"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exactly five digits is {5}. + would allow a longer run, and \\\\w includes letters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is matches(\".csv\") the wrong file-name test?","options":["endsWith is a regex","matches would require the entire String to be .csv","matches cannot see a dot",".csv is not text"],"answer":1,"explain":"report.csv is more than the four characters .csv. endsWith(\".csv\") checks the ending.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does Pattern.compile(\"\\\\d+\") describe?","options":["Whitespace","The literal characters d+","One or more digits","Exactly one digit"],"answer":2,"explain":"The compiled pattern is reused by a Matcher on a particular message.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A sentence contains 104, and matches(\"\\\\d+\") is false. Why?","options":["104 is not a digit run","\\\\d+ means letters","Pattern cannot be compiled","matches demands that the entire sentence be digits. find looks for digits inside it"],"answer":3,"explain":"Whole-string matching is the wrong question for a number embedded in words.","kind":"debug","code":"boolean found = message.matches(\"\\\\d+\");","fix":"Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(\"\\\\d+\");\nMatcher matcher = pattern.matcher(message);\nboolean found = matcher.find();","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Interactive regex lab","teach":"The lab tries a pattern against sample text. \\\\d+ finds digit runs such as 104 and 208. A whole-text five-digit check is ^\\\\d{5}$. The page uses a browser demonstration and Java regex is similar for these examples.","questions":[{"q":"What does the lab let you do?","options":["Try a pattern against sample text and see the matches","Compile and run a Java file","Read the keyboard with Scanner","Sort the text"],"answer":0,"explain":"The results area shows what the pattern finds or rejects. It is a browser demonstration.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which starter pattern finds runs such as 104 and 208?","options":[".","\\\\d+","\\\\w+ as the digit pattern","^\\\\d{5}$ as a search for every number in a sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"\\\\d+ is one or more digits. The five-digit pattern is for a whole PIN, not for finding several numbers.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which pattern validates a whole five-digit PIN in the lab notes?","options":["\\\\s","abc","^\\\\d{5}$","\\\\d+"],"answer":2,"explain":"The anchors mean the whole text, which matches the lesson's whole-string PIN check.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the lab warn about JavaScript versus Java?","options":["They are the same in every regex feature","Java has no regex","The lab compiles Java","The demonstration is similar for these examples, but not identical in every detail"],"answer":3,"explain":"Learn the idea here, and use Java's Pattern, Matcher, and matches in Java source.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"The text is Order 104 is ready. Which pattern finds 104?","options":["\\\\d+","\\\\d{5}","[A-Z]","report"],"answer":0,"explain":"104 is a run of digits. It is not five digits, and it is not an uppercase letter.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which text matches ^\\\\d{5}$?","options":["Order 104","48291","4829","A8291"],"answer":1,"explain":"Exactly five digits, with nothing before or after, matches. The shorter, lettered, and sentence texts do not.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \\\\w+ find in ordinary text?","options":["Only the digit 1","A five-digit PIN and nothing else","Word-like sequences of letters, digits, and underscores","Only spaces"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lab's word pattern is \\\\w+.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can the same digits use \\\\d+ in one mode and ^\\\\d{5}$ in another?","options":["They mean the same pattern","{5} means five or more","^ means a digit","One searches for a run, and the other validates an entire five-digit value"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lab separates find-anywhere from match-the-whole-text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Order 104 and Order 208 are in the sample. What does \\\\d+ have to find?","options":["Both 104 and 208","Only 104","The word Order","A five-digit PIN"],"answer":0,"explain":"+ repeats the digit, so each run is a match. find can be applied again for the next run.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pattern is the literal word Order, not a digit rule?","options":["\\\\s+","Order","\\\\d+","\\\\d{3}"],"answer":1,"explain":"Letters without a digit token match themselves.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A replacement mode is offered in the lab. What does replacement do?","options":["It reads System.in","It changes \\\\d into \\\\w","It substitutes the matches with the replacement text","It compiles Java"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lab includes replacement text so you can see a pattern used to rewrite matches.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The PIN check uses \\\\d+ and accepts 482919. Why is that too loose?","options":["482919 contains a letter","\\\\d does not match 4","Anchors reject every digit","+ allows any number of digits. A five-digit PIN needs exactly {5} on the whole text"],"answer":3,"explain":"^\\\\d{5}$ rejects a sixth digit. \\\\d+ accepts it.","kind":"debug","code":"pin.matches(\"\\\\d+\")","fix":"pin.matches(\"\\\\d{5}\")","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Practice problems","teach":"Use contains for an exact word, indexOf for the first position, and matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") for a whole five-digit PIN. In a Java string literal, a digit token is written with escaped backslashes.","questions":[{"q":"How do you check that \"Java makes practice easier\" contains practice?","options":["contains(\"practice\")","matches(\"practice\"), which requires the whole sentence to be that one word","indexOf without comparing the result","\\\\d{5}"],"answer":0,"explain":"The practice solution prints true from contains. The sentence is longer than the word.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the first index of cat in \"catapult and cat\"?","options":["8","0","13","-1"],"answer":1,"explain":"indexOf returns the first match, at the start of catapult.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which call accepts 48291 and rejects 4829A?","options":["endsWith(\"5\")","indexOf(\"48291\") == -1","\"48291\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\")","contains(\"\\\\d{5}\")"],"answer":2,"explain":"The whole String must be five digits. 4829A includes a letter.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How is a digit token written in a Java string literal?","options":["\"d\"","\".\"","\"\\\\s\" for a digit","\"\\\\d\", so the regex sees \\d"],"answer":3,"explain":"The practice note says a Java String literal escapes the backslash.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"Java makes practice easier\".contains(\"practice\") return?","options":["true","false","8","practice"],"answer":0,"explain":"The word occurs, so contains is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"catapult and cat\".indexOf(\"cat\") return?","options":["3","0","12","-1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The earliest cat starts at index 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"48291\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") return?","options":["48291","5","true","false"],"answer":2,"explain":"Exactly five digits match the whole String.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"4829A\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") return?","options":["true","A","-1","false"],"answer":3,"explain":"A is not a digit, and the length is five only because of that letter.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does \"4829\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\") return?","options":["false","true","4829","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Four digits are not a five-digit PIN.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which method finds the position of the first cat, rather than only whether cat occurs?","options":["endsWith(\"cat\")","indexOf(\"cat\")","contains(\"cat\")","matches(\"cat\")"],"answer":1,"explain":"contains returns a boolean. indexOf returns 0 for this text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A checker should accept only 48291, not a sentence that mentions it. Which method fits?","options":["indexOf \u003e -1 as a whole-PIN test","startsWith(\"4\")","matches","contains"],"answer":2,"explain":"matches applies the five-digit rule to the entire text.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The PIN test is written matches(\"d{5}\"). What will a digit PIN do?","options":["Pass, because d means digit","Pass only when the PIN contains the letter d","Throw ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException","Fail, because d{5} means five letter d characters. The digit token is \\\\d"],"answer":3,"explain":"The backslash is part of the digit token. In Java source it is escaped.","kind":"debug","code":"\"48291\".matches(\"d{5}\")","fix":"\"48291\".matches(\"\\\\d{5}\")","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_sets_maps_unique_values_keys_lookup.html","code":"12","name":"Sets and maps","sections":[{"title":"1. Start with a problem, not a definition","teach":"A Set answers whether something is present and keeps it once. A Map looks up a value by a key. A plain list would count Arun three times and would make you scan for Maya's score.","questions":[{"q":"Arun signs up three times and the guest count should include him once. Which structure matches?","options":["A Set","A List that keeps every signup","An array whose length grows by itself","A switch"],"answer":0,"explain":"The guest-list job cares whether a name is present, not how many times the form was submitted.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"The teacher asks for Maya's score by name. Which structure matches?","options":["An int with no key","A Map from name to score","A Set of scores with no names","A List that must be scanned by eye, which is the problem the lesson wants to avoid"],"answer":1,"explain":"The registration notebook points a student identity at details. That lookup is a Map job.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What question does the lesson assign to a Set?","options":["How many notebooks are in stock for each product?","What is at index 2?","Is Arun on the list?","What score did Maya get?"],"answer":2,"explain":"Membership is the Set question. The score and the stock lookup are Map questions.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why can a plain list be the wrong guest list?","options":["It cannot store Strings","It sorts names automatically","It rejects every duplicate, which is the opposite of the lesson's warning","It keeps every signup, so a repeated name can be counted again"],"answer":3,"explain":"The opening problem is the repeated Arun. A Set is what rejects that duplicate.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Three signups are Arun, Maya, and Arun. How many guests should the Set report?","options":["2","3","1","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arun is one guest even though the form arrived twice. Maya is the other.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The shop asks how many notebooks are in stock. Which shape fits?","options":["One array index for every possible sentence","A Map from product name to a count","A Set of counts","A boolean"],"answer":1,"explain":"The product is the key and the stock is the value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which question is not a Set question in this opening?","options":["Has this email already been used?","Is this name present?","What score did Maya get?","Is Arun on the list?"],"answer":2,"explain":"A score looked up by a person is a Map. Presence is a Set.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does the lesson start with the registration app?","options":["Sets and Maps are the same","Every collection should be an array","The app cannot use Java","The right container depends on the question, not on a definition memorised first"],"answer":3,"explain":"Guest list and score book are different jobs, so they lead to different structures.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A list of signups is Arun, Arun, Arun. What does a Set's size become?","options":["1","3","0","2"],"answer":0,"explain":"All three entries are the same person, so uniqueness leaves one element.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need both \"is this student registered\" and \"what is their score\". What does the lesson's split suggest?","options":["A Map rejects duplicate values even when the keys differ","A Set can answer presence, and a Map can answer the score lookup","One int can do both","A Set stores the score under the name"],"answer":1,"explain":"The two school jobs stay different. A Map key is unique, while its values may repeat.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which storage would make the teacher scan every record for Maya?","options":["An index that is the name","get(\"Maya\")","A plain list of records with no key lookup","A Map whose key is the student"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson presents that scan as the problem a Map avoids.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The guest count uses a List and adds Arun on every signup. Why is the count wrong for this problem?","options":["List cannot store names","Set would count him three times","add always fails","The list keeps the repeated signup. A Set would count Arun once"],"answer":3,"explain":"Choose the structure from the question. This question wants uniqueness.","kind":"debug","code":"List\u003cString\u003e guests = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();\nguests.add(\"Arun\");\nguests.add(\"Arun\");","fix":"Set\u003cString\u003e guests = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\nguests.add(\"Arun\");\nguests.add(\"Arun\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Collections recap: array, list, Set and Map","teach":"An array is a fixed numbered sequence. A list is an ordered growing sequence that allows duplicates. A Set keeps unique items. A Map looks up a value by key.","questions":[{"q":"Which description matches an array in the recap?","options":["Fixed-size numbered seats, accessed by index","Unique items with no interest in duplicates","A dictionary from key to value","A growing list that allows duplicates"],"answer":0,"explain":"The table's array question is \"who is in seat 2?\"","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which description matches a list?","options":["Key-value lookup","Ordered items, duplicates allowed, and the sequence can grow","Fixed length chosen at creation","Unique names only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The recap's list example is a growing queue of tasks.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which description matches a Set?","options":["A score stored under a name","Duplicates kept in order","Unique items, such as a guest list","Indexed seats"],"answer":2,"explain":"Has this email already been used? is the Set question.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which description matches a Map?","options":["A fixed row of same-type values","A guest list with no payload","An ordered list of duplicate tasks","Look up a value by key, like a dictionary or labelled locker"],"answer":3,"explain":"What is product X's stock? is the Map question.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"You need who is in seat 2 of a fixed row. Which recap entry fits?","options":["Array","Set","Map","The guest-list Set"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seat 2 is an index. That is the array's strength.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need the next task, and the same task text may appear twice. Which entry fits?","options":["Array whose length cannot grow, if the recap's growing queue is the point","List","Set","Map from task to itself when duplicates must remain"],"answer":1,"explain":"The recap says a list keeps ordered items and allows duplicates.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need to know whether an email was already used. Which entry fits?","options":["List that keeps both copies as the answer","Map, if all you store is the address with no value","Set","Array index"],"answer":2,"explain":"Presence of a unique email is the Set row.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need product X's stock. Which entry fits?","options":["Set of stock numbers","Array of names with no values","List of every past stock update as the lookup","Map"],"answer":3,"explain":"The product is the key and the stock is the value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair can both be walked by index in the recap?","options":["Array and list","Set and Map","Map and array only, excluding lists","Set and list, because a Set has indexes in this recap"],"answer":0,"explain":"The recap treats array and list as sequences where position matters. Set and Map are not introduced as index sequences.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the quick analogy for a Map?","options":["A fixed length","A dictionary: look up a word to find its meaning","A row of boxes where only position matters","A list of distinct names with no payload"],"answer":1,"explain":"The key is the word and the value is the meaning.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does the recap say not every problem is a numbered sequence?","options":["Lists cannot grow","Indexes start at 1","Some problems care about uniqueness or about lookup by a label","Arrays cannot store numbers"],"answer":2,"explain":"Set and Map are the tools when position is not the question.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Email uniqueness is stored in a List, so the same address appears twice. Which recap structure matches the job?","options":["An array of fixed duplicates","A Map with no key","The list, because duplicates are the goal","A Set"],"answer":3,"explain":"The recap's Set row is exactly \"has this email already been used?\"","kind":"debug","code":"List\u003cString\u003e used = new ArrayList\u003c\u003e();\nused.add(email);\nused.add(email);","fix":"Set\u003cString\u003e used = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\nused.add(email);\nused.add(email);","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Meet Set: the guest list that rejects duplicates","teach":"A Set does not keep duplicates. HashSet is the common first implementation. add returns false when the value is already present. contains checks membership. size counts the unique elements. Do not rely on HashSet order.","questions":[{"q":"What does a Set refuse to keep?","options":["A duplicate of an element it already contains","Every String","The first element","Elements of the declared type"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adding Arun the second time leaves the size at the number of unique names.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does Set\u003cString\u003e say?","options":["The angle brackets are a comment","The Set will hold String values","The Set holds one String only","The Set is a Map"],"answer":1,"explain":"The type argument says what each element is. new HashSet\u003c\u003e() creates the object.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does guests.add return when Arun is already in the Set?","options":["The old Arun","2","false","true"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first add of a new name returns true. The duplicate add returns false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does contains(\"Maya\") answer?","options":["Maya's index","How many times Maya was added","The size","Whether Maya is an element"],"answer":3,"explain":"Membership is true or false. It is not an index.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After adding Arun, Maya, and Arun to a HashSet, what is size()?","options":["2","3","1","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"The second Arun is not a new element.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"An empty HashSet receives guests.add(\"Arun\") twice. What does the second call return?","options":["2","false","true","null"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arun is already present, so the Set rejects the duplicate and reports false.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does contains(\"Maya\") return after Arun and Maya were added?","options":["2","Maya","true","false"],"answer":2,"explain":"Maya was added once, so membership is true.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does contains(\"Kavi\") return when Kavi was never added?","options":["true","null","-1","false"],"answer":3,"explain":"Absence is false, not an exception and not -1, in the lesson's contains example.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which creation matches the sample?","options":["Set\u003cString\u003e guests = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();","Set\u003cString\u003e guests = new Set\u003c\u003e();","HashSet guests = \"Arun\";","List\u003cString\u003e guests = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":0,"explain":"The variable can be the Set interface, and the object is a HashSet.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why should you not print a HashSet and expect Arun then Maya?","options":["add always appends in alphabetical order","HashSet does not promise iteration order","size() sorts the names","contains sorts them"],"answer":1,"explain":"The later section says not to depend on HashSet order. Uniqueness is the promise.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Three distinct names are added once each. What is size()?","options":["0","6","3","1"],"answer":2,"explain":"No duplicates were rejected, so each add increases the size.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The second add is tested with == true, but the code treats a duplicate as success. What does a duplicate add return?","options":["true","the size","the name","false"],"answer":3,"explain":"Success at inserting a new element is true. Already present is false.","kind":"debug","code":"boolean addedAgain = guests.add(\"Arun\");\nif (addedAgain == true) {\n    System.out.println(\"new guest\");\n}","fix":"boolean addedAgain = guests.add(\"Arun\");\nif (addedAgain == false) {\n    System.out.println(\"already present\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Set lab: try to add the same name twice","teach":"The set lab imitates a HashSet in the browser. The first add of a name returns true. A second add of that same name returns false and the unique count stays the same. contains checks membership. Removing a name that is present decreases the size. The lab does not compile Java.","questions":[{"q":"What does the set lab actually run?","options":["A browser imitation of Set behaviour","A Java compiler on your computer","A program that edits a guest file on disk","A Map of scores"],"answer":0,"explain":"The page says the lab is a visual simulation. The Java sample beside it is the real version of the same idea.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the first guests.add(\"Arun\") return on an empty Set?","options":["Arun","true","false","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"A new element is accepted. The sample marks that first call true.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the second guests.add(\"Arun\") return?","options":["2","null","false","true"],"answer":2,"explain":"Arun is already present, so the Set rejects the duplicate and the method returns false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After only Arun has been added, twice, what is size()?","options":["2","0","true","1"],"answer":3,"explain":"The unique count is the number of different names. Two attempts to add Arun still leave one guest.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does contains(\"Arun\") return after Arun was added?","options":["true","false","1","the index 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"contains answers membership. It does not return an index.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"You add Arun, then Maya, then Kavi. What is the size?","options":["6","3","2","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three different names are three elements. Nothing was a duplicate.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You add Arun two times. Does the unique count become 2?","options":["Yes, because the lab is a List","It becomes 0","No. It stays 1","Yes. Each click adds another Arun"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lab challenge asks this directly. A Set records Arun once.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Maya and Kavi are in the Set, then Maya is removed. What is the size?","options":["2","0","3","1"],"answer":3,"explain":"Removing a name that is present decreases the size by one. Kavi remains.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Kavi was added, and you check whether Kavi is present. What is the result?","options":["true","false","null","Kavi's position"],"answer":0,"explain":"contains returns true for a name the Set is holding.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The sample sets added1, added2, found, and then prints the size after two adds of Arun. What are those four results?","options":["true, false, false, and size 2","true, false, true, and size 1","true, true, true, and size 2","false, false, false, and size 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first add succeeds, the second is rejected, Arun is still present, and the size is 1.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You remove a name that was never added. What happens to the size?","options":["It grows by 1","The Set turns into a List","It stays the same","It becomes 0"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only a present element can leave. A missing name is not an extra removal.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two adds of Arun are followed by a test that the size is 2. What is wrong?","options":["add always appends","size counts attempts, not elements","HashSet sorts before it counts","The second add does not create another element, so the size is 1"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lab's Java sample prints 1 after Arun is added twice.","kind":"debug","code":"guests.add(\"Arun\");\nguests.add(\"Arun\");\nif (guests.size() == 2) {\n    System.out.println(\"two Aruns\");\n}","fix":"guests.add(\"Arun\");\nguests.add(\"Arun\");\nif (guests.size() == 1) {\n    System.out.println(\"one Arun\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Meet Map: a label that leads to information","teach":"A Map stores key-value pairs. A key appears once. put on an existing key replaces the value and the size does not grow. Different keys may share a value. get returns the value, or null when the key is missing. containsKey checks the key. getOrDefault supplies a fallback. Do not unbox a missing get straight into an int.","questions":[{"q":"What does a Map connect?","options":["One key to one value","A list of duplicate keys","Only positions, with no labels","A Set of values and nothing else"],"answer":0,"explain":"The locker label is the key. What is inside is the value.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can two different keys store the same value?","options":["Only if the keys are numbers","Yes. Keys must be unique, but values may repeat","No. A repeated value deletes both keys","No. The second key is rejected"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arun and Maya can both have the score 90. The keys are still different.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does put do when the key is already present?","options":["It leaves the old value and ignores the new one","It clears the Map","It replaces that key's value","It creates a second entry with the same key"],"answer":2,"explain":"scores.put(\"Maya\", 96) replaces 92. There is still one Maya key.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does get(\"Kavi\") return on a normal HashMap when Kavi was never stored?","options":["0","false","an empty String","null"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson warns that null is not a score. Unboxing it into an int can throw NullPointerException.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does containsKey(\"Arun\") answer?","options":["Whether that key is in the Map","Arun's score","How many times Arun was put","The size"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is a yes-or-no check for the key, separate from reading the value.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"put Maya 92, put Arun 85, then put Maya 96. What does get(\"Maya\") return?","options":["188","96","92","85"],"answer":1,"explain":"The second Maya put replaces 92. get reads the current value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After those three put calls, what is size()?","options":["1","96","2","3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Maya and Arun are two keys. Replacing Maya's score does not add a key.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Maya is stored as 90 and Arun is stored as 90. What is size()?","options":["1","90","0","2"],"answer":3,"explain":"The values match, but the keys differ, so both entries stay.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration matches the score example?","options":["Map\u003cString, Integer\u003e scores = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();","Map\u003cString, Integer\u003e scores = new Set\u003c\u003e();","HashMap scores = \"Maya\";","List\u003cInteger\u003e scores = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":0,"explain":"The variable uses the Map interface. The object is a HashMap. The imports are java.util.Map and java.util.HashMap.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Kavi is absent. Which call gives 0 without throwing on unboxing?","options":["scores.size()","scores.getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0)","int score = scores.get(\"Kavi\");","scores.containsKey(\"Kavi\") used as the score"],"answer":1,"explain":"getOrDefault returns the fallback. Assigning get's null to an int is the case the lesson warns about.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does containsKey(\"Maya\") return after Maya was put?","options":["false","null","true","96"],"answer":2,"explain":"The key exists, so the check is true. The score itself comes from get.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The missing score is read with get and stored in an int. What goes wrong when Kavi is absent?","options":["get returns 0","the Map adds Kavi with value 0","containsKey replaces get","get returns null, and unboxing that null into an int can throw NullPointerException"],"answer":3,"explain":"Use getOrDefault or test containsKey before treating the result as an int.","kind":"debug","code":"int kaviScore = scores.get(\"Kavi\");","fix":"int kaviScore = scores.getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0);","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Map lab: create a tiny score book","teach":"The score book is a browser simulation of a HashMap. Saving a new name adds an entry. Saving an existing name replaces the score and the entry count stays the same. Looking up a missing name with the default returns 0 and does not add that name.","questions":[{"q":"What does the map lab actually do?","options":["It imitates Map behaviour in the browser","It writes scores.txt on your computer","It compiles the Java sample","It sorts the students before every save"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Java sample is shown for reading. The buttons are a simulation.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"You save Maya as 92 and later save Maya as 96. What happens to the entry count?","options":["The book is cleared","It stays the same, because put replaces the value","It becomes 2","Maya is removed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lab challenge asks whether the number of entries increased. It did not.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Kavi is not in the book. What does getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0) return?","options":["Kavi","the size","0","null"],"answer":2,"explain":"The fallback is used when the key is absent. The sample stores that result in kaviScore.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does that default lookup add Kavi to the book?","options":["Yes, with score 0","Yes, with score 96","It adds Kavi only when the book is empty","No. It only returns the fallback"],"answer":3,"explain":"getOrDefault reads. It does not put a new key.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A normal lookup of a name that was never saved, using get, gives what?","options":["null","0","false","an exception before get returns"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson separates get, which can be null, from the default lookup that returns 0.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Save Maya 92 and Arun 85, then save Maya 96. How many entries are stored?","options":["96","2","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two names, and Maya's score was replaced.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is Maya's score after that second save?","options":["85","188","96","92"],"answer":2,"explain":"The later put is the value get returns.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0), is Kavi a key?","options":["Yes, mapped to 0","Yes, mapped to 92","Only if Arun is absent","No"],"answer":3,"explain":"The call did not put Kavi.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Maya and Arun are saved, then Maya is removed. What remains?","options":["One entry, Arun","Both students","An empty book","Maya only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing a present student decreases the entry count. Arun's score stays.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Maya is saved as 85 and Arun is saved as 85. How many entries?","options":["0","2","1","85"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same score, different names. The keys are both kept.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which Java matches the lab sample's Kavi line?","options":["scores.add(\"Kavi\", 0);","kaviScore = scores.size();","int kaviScore = scores.getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0);","int kaviScore = scores.get(\"Kavi\");"],"answer":2,"explain":"The sample uses the fallback form so a missing name becomes 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The lab code prints get(\"Kavi\") and the comment expects 0. Kavi was never saved. What should the call be?","options":["put(\"Kavi\", 0) before every lookup","size(), which is 0 only when the book is empty","containsKey, which returns 0","getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0), because get returns null"],"answer":3,"explain":"The default lookup is the lesson's way to show 0 for a missing name.","kind":"debug","code":"System.out.println(scores.get(\"Kavi\")); // expected 0","fix":"System.out.println(scores.getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0));","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. How do we loop through a Set or Map?","teach":"An enhanced for loop visits each unique Set element once. A HashSet does not promise which element comes first. A Map is visited through entrySet. getKey reads the label and getValue reads the contents.","questions":[{"q":"What does one pass of for (String guest : guests) give you?","options":["One element from the Set","The size","Every element joined into one String","The index of the guest"],"answer":0,"explain":"The enhanced loop hands you the element. A HashSet does not give you an index.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can you rely on a HashSet printing Arun before Maya?","options":["Only when size is 2","No. HashSet does not promise iteration order","Yes. add order is the print order","Yes. It sorts names first"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says not to assume which guest prints first.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does scores.entrySet() give the loop?","options":["Only the size","A List of duplicate keys","Each key-value record","Only the scores"],"answer":2,"explain":"The analogy is opening every locker record, label and contents together.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does entry.getKey() read?","options":["The score","The size of the Map","Whether the key is missing","The label, such as the student name"],"answer":3,"explain":"getKey is the locker label. getValue is what is inside.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does entry.getValue() read?","options":["The value stored for that key","The next key","The insertion index","null whenever the key exists"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the score loop, getValue is the Integer score.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A HashSet holds Arun and Maya. How many names does the enhanced loop visit?","options":["4","2, in an order you must not assume","2, always Arun then Maya","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each unique element is visited once. The order is not part of the HashSet promise.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which loop matches the lesson's Map example?","options":["for (int i = 0; i \u003c scores.length; i++)","for (Integer score : scores.keys())","for (Map.Entry\u003cString, Integer\u003e entry : scores.entrySet())","for (String name : scores)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Map itself is not a list of names. entrySet provides the pairs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Inside that loop, which expression prints the lesson's sentence shape?","options":["entry.getValue() + \" scored \" + entry.size()","guest + \" scored \" + scores.size()","entry.getKey() only","entry.getKey() + \" scored \" + entry.getValue()"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sample prints the name, the word scored, and the score.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Three unique guests are in a HashSet. How many lines does the guest loop print?","options":["3","1","6","0, because HashSet cannot be looped"],"answer":0,"explain":"Uniqueness limits the visits. It does not stop the loop.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need every name and every score. Which call supplies both in one record?","options":["contains","entrySet","keySet alone, with no further get","size"],"answer":1,"explain":"keySet gives names. The lesson's record that holds both parts is the entry.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is a numeric index the wrong tool for this HashSet loop?","options":["contains returns the index","size is the last index","The lesson visits values with an enhanced for loop, and HashSet is not an indexed sequence","Indexes start at 1 for a Set"],"answer":2,"explain":"The recap treated arrays and lists as numbered seats. This Set loop does not.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The loop tries to walk the Map as if it were a Set of names. What should it walk?","options":["scores.length","a second HashSet of the same scores","the key as an int index","scores.entrySet(), then read getKey and getValue"],"answer":3,"explain":"A Map is a collection of pairs. The lesson's loop uses the entry set.","kind":"debug","code":"for (String name : scores) {\n    System.out.println(name);\n}","fix":"for (Map.Entry\u003cString, Integer\u003e entry : scores.entrySet()) {\n    System.out.println(entry.getKey() + \" scored \" + entry.getValue());\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"8. Three useful real-world patterns","teach":"A HashSet built from a list keeps each email once. A LinkedHashSet keeps the order those emails were first seen. Counting words uses a Map: read the old count with getOrDefault, add one, and put it back. A Set of visitor ids counts people, not repeat visits. The word example is tea three times, coffee once, and juice once.","questions":[{"q":"A list of emails contains duplicates and you need each address once. What does the lesson build?","options":["A HashSet from that list","A Map with no keys","An array that deletes nothing","A count stored in one int for every address"],"answer":0,"explain":"new HashSet\u003c\u003e(emails) drops the repeated address. The sample size is 2.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When must that email Set be a LinkedHashSet?","options":["When order must be ignored","When the first-seen order has to be kept","When any duplicate must be stored twice","When the values are numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"HashSet does not promise order. LinkedHashSet iterates in insertion order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is a word count a Map job?","options":["Words cannot be Strings","A Map rejects repeated values","Each word is a key and the number of appearances is the value","A Set stores the count as its size for one word even when other words exist"],"answer":2,"explain":"A Set can only say whether the word exists. The count lives in the value.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the counting loop do for a word it has not seen?","options":["It skips the word","It stores null","It removes every earlier word","getOrDefault returns 0, then the code stores 1"],"answer":3,"explain":"The steps are: read the current count, treat a missing word as zero, add one, and put the result back.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Pattern C adds user-17, user-21, and user-17 again to a HashSet. What does that Set represent?","options":["The distinct visitors","The number of page loads, including repeats","A Map from user to visit time","A list that still contains user-17 twice"],"answer":0,"explain":"The second user-17 does not create another element.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"emails are a@example.com, b@example.com, and a@example.com again. What is the HashSet size?","options":["0","2","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The repeated address is one element.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The words are tea, coffee, tea, juice, tea. What is the count for tea?","options":["5","2","3","1"],"answer":2,"explain":"tea is updated from 0 to 1, then 2, then 3. coffee and juice stay at 1.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is the HashSet size for those same words?","options":["5","1","4","3"],"answer":3,"explain":"tea, coffee, and juice are the distinct words. The Set does not remember that tea appeared three times.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line updates a word that was already counted?","options":["counts.put(word, oldCount + 1)","counts.add(word)","counts.put(word, oldCount)","counts.getOrDefault(word, 0) with no put"],"answer":0,"explain":"getOrDefault only reads. The put writes the increased count under the same key.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"user-17 is added twice and user-21 once. What is visitors.size()?","options":["17","2","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The repeated id is rejected. Two distinct visitors remain.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need the distinct emails in the order they first appeared. Which constructor's type fits?","options":["new TreeSet\u003c\u003e() when you need first-seen order specifically","new ArrayList\u003c\u003e(emails), which also drops duplicates","new LinkedHashSet\u003c\u003e(emails)","new HashSet\u003c\u003e(emails), because it promises insertion order"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson names LinkedHashSet for first-seen order. An ArrayList would keep the duplicate row.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The count loop stores oldCount without adding one. What does tea's count stay at after later teas?","options":["3, because put always adds one","0, because getOrDefault clears the Map","The size of the word array","The same number it already had, because the increased value is never stored"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's update is oldCount + 1, then put.","kind":"debug","code":"int oldCount = counts.getOrDefault(word, 0);\ncounts.put(word, oldCount);","fix":"int oldCount = counts.getOrDefault(word, 0);\ncounts.put(word, oldCount + 1);","debug":true}]},{"title":"9. Which implementation should I choose?","teach":"Set and Map name the operations. HashSet and HashMap are the ordinary choices when order does not matter. LinkedHashSet and LinkedHashMap keep insertion order. TreeSet and TreeMap keep sorted order. Set elements are unique. Map keys are unique and Map values may repeat. A Map is not a Set of values.","questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between Set and HashSet in this section?","options":["Set describes the operations. HashSet is a class that does the work","They are two names for a List","HashSet allows duplicate elements","Set keeps keys sorted"],"answer":0,"explain":"The same split applies to Map and HashMap.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Unique items, and order does not matter. Which class does the table suggest?","options":["ArrayList","HashSet","TreeSet","LinkedHashSet"],"answer":1,"explain":"HashSet is the general-purpose Set. Choose another class when order is part of the requirement.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Unique items that must iterate in insertion order. Which class?","options":["TreeSet","HashMap","LinkedHashSet","HashSet"],"answer":2,"explain":"LinkedHashSet keeps uniqueness and insertion order.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Key-to-value lookup, and key order does not matter. Which class?","options":["TreeMap","HashSet","LinkedHashSet","HashMap"],"answer":3,"explain":"HashMap is the ordinary lookup choice. TreeMap is for sorted keys.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which statement matches the last distinction?","options":["Map keys are unique, and values may repeat","Map values must be unique and keys may repeat","A Map is a Set of its values","HashMap prints keys in sorted order"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two students can share a score. Their names cannot share one key.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Product codes must stay sorted. Which Map fits?","options":["HashSet","TreeMap","HashMap","LinkedHashMap, which sorts keys"],"answer":1,"explain":"TreeMap keeps keys sorted. LinkedHashMap keeps insertion order.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Scores should come back in the order the names were first saved. Which Map fits?","options":["TreeMap","TreeSet","LinkedHashMap","HashMap"],"answer":2,"explain":"LinkedHashMap iterates in insertion order by default.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You need sorted unique names and no payload. Which Set fits?","options":["HashSet","LinkedHashSet","HashMap","TreeSet"],"answer":3,"explain":"TreeSet keeps elements sorted. A Map would be for key-value pairs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Day one, you only need uniqueness. What does the note tell you to start with?","options":["HashSet","TreeSet, so the order is decided early","ArrayList","A hand-written array"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says not to overthink performance. Start with HashSet or HashMap until ordering matters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Day one, you only need key-value lookup. What should you start with?","options":["LinkedHashSet","HashMap","TreeMap","HashSet"],"answer":1,"explain":"HashMap is the ordinary lookup implementation.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is a Map not a Set of values?","options":["Because keys may be duplicated","Because HashMap sorts values","A Map connects each key to a value. The values themselves may repeat","Because a Map cannot be looped"],"answer":2,"explain":"The section lists that distinction separately from uniqueness.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Guests are stored in a HashSet and the program expects the print order Arun then Maya. What should change?","options":["Call size() to sort the names","Switch to a List and keep both Arun entries","Use contains to print in order","Use a LinkedHashSet when that insertion order is required"],"answer":3,"explain":"Hash-based collections do not guarantee insertion order.","kind":"debug","code":"Set\u003cString\u003e guests = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();\nguests.add(\"Arun\");\nguests.add(\"Maya\");","fix":"Set\u003cString\u003e guests = new LinkedHashSet\u003c\u003e();\nguests.add(\"Arun\");\nguests.add(\"Maya\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"10. Practice problems","teach":"Choose the structure from the job. A username used once is a Set. red, blue, red has size 2. put replaces a Map value. A product code to a stock count is Map\u003cString, Integer\u003e. getOrDefault returns the fallback and does not add the key. Word counts need a Map. Insertion order for unique items is a LinkedHashSet. Two keys may share a value.","questions":[{"q":"Each username may be registered only once. Which structure fits?","options":["A Set of Strings, such as a HashSet","A List, because it keeps the second registration","A Map with no value","An array whose length grows when a duplicate is added"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Set rejects a username it already holds.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does put do when Maya is already a key?","options":["It rejects the new score and keeps 92","It updates Maya's value and does not add a second Maya key","It stores both scores","It deletes Maya"],"answer":1,"explain":"The practice problem's Map goes from 92 to 96 for the same key.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Word counts in a document need which structure?","options":["A Queue","An array that cannot hold Strings","A Map from the word to an integer count","A Set, because size is the count of one chosen word"],"answer":2,"explain":"The word is the key. The number of appearances is the value.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Unique items must be iterated in insertion order. Which implementation?","options":["HashSet","TreeSet","HashMap","LinkedHashSet"],"answer":3,"explain":"The practice answer names LinkedHashSet. TreeSet would sort instead.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can Arun and Maya both be stored with the score 90?","options":["Yes. Different keys may share a value","No. The second 90 deletes Arun","No. Values must be unique","Only if the Map is a TreeSet"],"answer":0,"explain":"Keys are unique. Values may repeat.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the size after adding red, blue, and red to a HashSet?","options":["0","2","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The second red is a duplicate.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A Map has Maya mapped to 92. After put(\"Maya\", 96), what does get(\"Maya\") return?","options":["188","null","96","92"],"answer":2,"explain":"The value is replaced. The key is not duplicated.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which declaration stores a product code and an integer stock count?","options":["Set\u003cString\u003e stock = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();","Map\u003cInteger, Integer\u003e stock = new HashSet\u003c\u003e();","List\u003cString\u003e stock = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();","Map\u003cString, Integer\u003e stock = new HashMap\u003c\u003e();"],"answer":3,"explain":"The practice answer uses Map and HashMap, with a String key and an Integer value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Kavi is not a key. What does scores.getOrDefault(\"Kavi\", 0) return?","options":["0","null","an exception","Maya's score"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fallback is returned. Kavi is not inserted by this call.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"After that getOrDefault call, what is true of Kavi?","options":["Kavi's value is 96","Kavi is still absent","Kavi is stored as 0","The Map is empty"],"answer":1,"explain":"Returning a fallback does not put the key.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"tea appears three times in the word array. A Set of those words cannot answer which question?","options":["Whether coffee is a different word from tea","How many distinct words there are","How many times tea appeared","Whether tea appeared at least once"],"answer":2,"explain":"Membership and distinct count are Set questions. The repetition count is the Map's value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Stock for notebook is saved with a second put, and the code then reads the size as 2. The Map started empty. What is the size?","options":["2, because each put adds a key","0, because put deletes","92","1, because the second put replaces the same key"],"answer":3,"explain":"One product code is one key, even when the quantity changes.","kind":"debug","code":"stock.put(\"notebook\", 20);\nstock.put(\"notebook\", 18);\nSystem.out.println(stock.size()); // comment expects 2","fix":"stock.put(\"notebook\", 20);\nstock.put(\"notebook\", 18);\nSystem.out.println(stock.size()); // 1\nSystem.out.println(stock.get(\"notebook\")); // 18","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_classes_objects_encapsulation_interactive.html","code":"14","name":"Classes and objects","sections":[{"title":"1. Why do we need classes?","teach":"Separate variables such as student1Name and student2Name become hard to organise. A class groups the data and the actions that belong together. The class is the cookie cutter. An object is one cookie made from it.","questions":[{"q":"Why does the lesson introduce a class for students?","options":["Related data and actions can be grouped instead of kept as a long list of separate variables","Java allows only one variable in a program","A class prints faster than a variable","Names cannot be stored in Strings"],"answer":0,"explain":"Maya's name and age, and Arun's name and age, would otherwise be four unrelated variables. Five hundred students would be worse.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In the cookie-cutter analogy, what is the class?","options":["The name printed on one cookie","The cutter, or the design, that describes each cookie","One particular cookie","The oven"],"answer":1,"explain":"The class describes the shape. It is not itself one student or one cookie.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In that same analogy, what is an object?","options":["A comment","The list of all variable names in the program","One actual cookie made from the cutter","The cutter"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each object is a particular instance made from the class.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What becomes messy with student1Name, student1Age, student2Name, and student2Age?","options":["Java refusing to store a String","The need to use a loop for two students","Constructors being required before any variable exists","Passing, organising, and maintaining a growing pile of separate variables"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's point is organisation, not a ban on variables.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What should travel together for one student?","options":["The student's data and the actions that use that data","Only the student's name","Every student's data in one String","The main method of every other class"],"answer":0,"explain":"A class is the place those related pieces live.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Maya's account needs a balance and a deposit action. Why does a class fit?","options":["A balance cannot be a field","The balance and the deposit behaviour belong to one account idea","A class can store the balance but cannot have a method","Deposit has to be a separate program"],"answer":1,"explain":"The data and the action are one concept.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How many separate variables does the lesson show for two students' names and ages?","options":["1","500","4","2"],"answer":2,"explain":"student1Name, student1Age, student2Name, and student2Age are four variables.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the lesson say about 500 students stored as separate variables?","options":["Java creates a class automatically at 500","An array is illegal for students","Each student must be a static int","They are difficult to organise, pass around, and maintain"],"answer":3,"explain":"The class exists so that growth does not mean hundreds of loose variables.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair matches the analogy?","options":["Class is the design. Object is one result made from that design","Class and object are two names for the same variable","The object is written once and the class is created with new","The design is the object"],"answer":0,"explain":"You can make many objects from one class.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A lamp needs a colour and a way to turn on. What is the lesson's reason to use a class?","options":["A class replaces the boolean type","The colour and the turn-on action belong together","A colour cannot be stored","turn-on must be written in main only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Grouping state and behaviour is the reason, even before the Lamp example is written out.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which problem is the class meant to solve in this section?","options":["Removing the need for types","Storing every student in one int","Keeping one thing's data and behaviour as one unit","Making println optional"],"answer":2,"explain":"The school-app story starts from that organisation problem.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The program adds student3 by declaring student3Name and student3Age beside the other loose variables. What is the design problem?","options":["A String cannot hold a name","int cannot hold an age","Java allows only two variables","Each new student adds more unrelated variables instead of creating another object from one class"],"answer":3,"explain":"The class is the cutter. Another student should be another object.","kind":"debug","code":"String student3Name = \"Kavi\";\nint student3Age = 14;","fix":"Student kavi = new Student(\"Kavi\", 14);","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Class vs object","teach":"The Dog class describes a name and bark. It does not by itself create a dog. new creates an object. firstDog named Pixel barks \"Pixel says woof!\" and secondDog named Chutney barks with Chutney's own name. The class is the house plan. Each object is a house.","questions":[{"q":"Does writing class Dog create a particular dog?","options":["No. The class only describes what a Dog object will have","Yes. The class is the first object","Yes, but only if bark is static","It creates one dog named Dog"],"answer":0,"explain":"new is the step that creates an object.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does new Dog() do?","options":["It compares two names","It creates a Dog object","It renames the class","It calls println by itself"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sample then assigns that object's name field.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In the house-plan analogy, what is the class?","options":["The paint colour of one house","The word new","The architectural plan","House A"],"answer":2,"explain":"House A and House B are the objects built from the plan.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does firstDog.bark() print when firstDog.name is Pixel?","options":["Chutney says woof!","Dog says woof!","name says woof!","Pixel says woof!"],"answer":3,"explain":"bark uses that object's own name field.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why can the two dogs bark different names?","options":["Each object has its own name field","The class stores one name shared by every dog","bark ignores the field","new copies the class name into the output"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pixel and Chutney are separate objects from one class.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How many Dog objects does the sample create?","options":["One class for each dog","2","1","0, until bark runs"],"answer":1,"explain":"firstDog and secondDog are two objects. There is one Dog class.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does secondDog.bark() print?","options":["secondDog","woof, with no name","Chutney says woof!","Pixel says woof!"],"answer":2,"explain":"secondDog.name was set to Chutney.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line creates the object rather than describing the class?","options":["class Dog {","String name;","void bark() {","Dog firstDog = new Dog();"],"answer":3,"explain":"class starts the description. new builds an object.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Both houses have a door, but different addresses. Which lesson point is that?","options":["Objects from one class share the design and keep their own field values","A class can have only one object","Field values are copied from the class name","Addresses are methods"],"answer":0,"explain":"The plan is shared. The particular values are not.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is printed if you call bark before setting a name, according to the shape of the method?","options":["The other dog's name","The current value of that object's name, followed by says woof!","The class name Dog","An empty program"],"answer":1,"explain":"The method reads the name field of the object you call it on. The sample sets the name before barking.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which statement is one object, not the class?","options":["void bark","The house plan","secondDog","class Dog"],"answer":2,"explain":"secondDog refers to an object created with new.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The program prints Chutney's bark but the name was assigned to firstDog. What does secondDog use?","options":["firstDog's name, because a class has one name","The word Dog","bark's return value","secondDog's own name field, which is still unset if only firstDog.name was assigned"],"answer":3,"explain":"Setting one object's field does not fill the other object's field.","kind":"debug","code":"Dog firstDog = new Dog();\nDog secondDog = new Dog();\nfirstDog.name = \"Chutney\";\nsecondDog.bark();","fix":"Dog firstDog = new Dog();\nDog secondDog = new Dog();\nsecondDog.name = \"Chutney\";\nsecondDog.bark();","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Fields and methods","teach":"A field stores state, such as a lamp's colour and isOn. A method is behaviour, such as turnOn and turnOff. Calculator.add returns the sum of its two parameters. A void method can change state without returning a value.","questions":[{"q":"What is a field?","options":["Data or state stored by an object","An action","A constructor's return type","Only a local name that disappears when the method ends"],"answer":0,"explain":"colour and isOn are fields. turnOn and turnOff are methods.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is a method?","options":["A second copy of a field","An action the object can perform","A stored colour","The class name"],"answer":1,"explain":"Behaviour belongs with the data it uses.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In the Lamp class, what is isOn?","options":["The class name","A value that turnOn cannot change","A field that stores whether the lamp is on","A method"],"answer":2,"explain":"turnOn sets that field to true. turnOff sets it to false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does Calculator.add return?","options":["Nothing, because it is void","The Calculator object","Only the first parameter","The sum of its two int parameters"],"answer":3,"explain":"return first + second gives the result to the caller.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does void turnOn() return a value?","options":["No. It performs an action or changes state","Yes, it returns true","Yes, it returns the colour","It returns the class"],"answer":0,"explain":"The practice problems state this directly. The caller does not receive a result.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A lamp starts off. turnOn runs. What is isOn?","options":["null","true","false","the colour"],"answer":1,"explain":"turnOn assigns true to the field.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"turnOn runs and then turnOff runs. What is isOn?","options":["both","the String off","false","true"],"answer":2,"explain":"turnOff assigns false. The later call is the current state.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"add(2, 5) uses the lesson's Calculator. What is the result?","options":["25","2","void","7"],"answer":3,"explain":"The method returns first + second.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which member is behaviour in Lamp?","options":["turnOff","colour","isOn","String"],"answer":0,"explain":"colour and isOn are state. turnOff is an action.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does the lesson put colour and turnOn in the same class?","options":["turnOn replaces the need for a class","The action belongs with the state it changes","A method cannot use a field","colour has to be static"],"answer":1,"explain":"A good class groups the data and the actions that belong together.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the caller receive from int sum = calc.add(10, 4)?","options":["void","10","14","calc"],"answer":2,"explain":"The returned int is stored in sum.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"add prints the sum and is declared void, but the caller writes int sum = calc.add(3, 4). What is missing?","options":["The parameters","new Calculator()","A field named sum","A return of the int sum. void does not give a value back"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's add returns first + second so the caller can use the result.","kind":"debug","code":"void add(int first, int second) {\n    System.out.println(first + second);\n}","fix":"int add(int first, int second) {\n    return first + second;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Constructors: give an object its starting values","teach":"A constructor runs when the object is created and stores the starting values. Its name matches the class exactly. It has no return type, not even void. new Student(\"Maya\", 14) creates Maya at age 14. void Student(...) is an ordinary method, not a constructor.","questions":[{"q":"When does the constructor run?","options":["When new creates the object","Only when a field is printed","After main finishes","Whenever a method is declared"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is the check-in step that initialises the new object.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which rule must the constructor name follow?","options":["It must start with a lowercase letter","It matches the class name exactly","It is always main","It must be turnOn"],"answer":1,"explain":"Student is the constructor inside class Student.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What return type does a constructor have?","options":["int","String","None. Not even void","void"],"answer":2,"explain":"A return type, including void, makes it an ordinary method.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does new Student(\"Maya\", 14) do?","options":["It compares Maya with 14","It declares a class named Maya","It calls a static method named new","It creates a Student and passes those values to the matching constructor"],"answer":3,"explain":"The constructor stores the name and the age.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the lesson prefer a constructor over assigning fields afterwards?","options":["It is easy to forget a later assignment, so the object receives what it needs at creation","Fields cannot be assigned","Constructors print the object","new is optional if a constructor exists"],"answer":0,"explain":"The hotel form records the guest at check-in, not after they have already entered.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After new Student(\"Arun\", 15), what are the stored name and age?","options":["Student and 15","Arun and 15","Maya and 14","Arun and 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"The arguments are handed to the constructor that assigns the fields.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header is a constructor for class Student?","options":["int Student(String name, int age)","student(String name, int age)","Student(String name, int age)","void Student(String name, int age)"],"answer":2,"explain":"No return type, and the name matches the class, including the capital S.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is void Student(String name)?","options":["The constructor, because the name matches","A field","A call to new","An ordinary method, not a constructor"],"answer":3,"explain":"void gives it a return type, so new Student(...) will not use it as the constructor.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two students are created: Maya age 14 and Arun age 15. How many constructors ran?","options":["2, one for each new","1, because there is one class","0, because the fields were parameters","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each new runs the constructor for that object.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which analogy matches the constructor?","options":["The class keyword","The hotel check-in form that records the new guest","The building plan with no guests","A method that can run long after check-in"],"answer":1,"explain":"The information is supplied as the object is created.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The parameters are name and age. Where do those values need to be stored?","options":["In a comment","Only in the caller's variables, never in the object","In the object's fields","In the class name"],"answer":2,"explain":"Otherwise the object forgets the values it was given.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"new Student(\"Maya\", 14) does not store the name, because the header is void Student. What should the header be?","options":["int Student(String name, int age)","void main(String name, int age)","String student(String name, int age)","Student(String name, int age), with no return type"],"answer":3,"explain":"Drop void. The name must match the class.","kind":"debug","code":"class Student {\n    String name;\n    int age;\n    void Student(String name, int age) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.age = age;\n    }\n}","fix":"class Student {\n    String name;\n    int age;\n    Student(String name, int age) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.age = age;\n    }\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. What does this mean?","teach":"Inside an instance method or constructor, this is the current object. In this.name = name, the right-hand name is the parameter and this.name is the field. Maya's this is Maya's object. Arun's this is Arun's object.","questions":[{"q":"What does this refer to?","options":["The current object, the one whose code is running","The class as a whole","The parameter only","The Java compiler"],"answer":0,"explain":"When Maya's object runs, this means Maya's object.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In this.name = name, what is this.name?","options":["A local variable of main","The field belonging to the current object","The parameter","The class name"],"answer":1,"explain":"The parameter is the incoming value. The field is where it is stored.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In this.name = name, what is the name on the right?","options":["A static count","The word this","The parameter","The field"],"answer":2,"explain":"Read it as: take the incoming name and store it in this object's name field.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why are the field and the parameter allowed the same name?","options":["Java forbids the parameter name","The names must differ by a number","this always means the parameter","this.name distinguishes the field from the parameter"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without this, the parameter would hide the field.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Two students say \"my name.\" What is the lesson's point?","options":["Each object means itself","Both mean the class","this means the first object ever created","Names are static"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arun's this is not Maya's object.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"new Student(\"Maya\") runs this.name = name. What is that object's name field?","options":["null, because this blocks the assignment","Maya","name","Student"],"answer":1,"explain":"The parameter value Maya is stored in the field.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Arun's constructor runs with the argument Arun. Whose field changes?","options":["Every Student","The class Student, and no object","The Arun object being created","The Maya object"],"answer":2,"explain":"this is that particular object.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which assignment stores the parameter in the field?","options":["name = name","this = name","name = this.name, when the goal is to copy the parameter into the field","this.name = name"],"answer":3,"explain":"The field is on the left. The incoming value is on the right.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"name = name, with no this, inside the constructor that has a parameter called name. What is stored in the field?","options":["The field is not updated, because both names refer to the parameter","The parameter is copied into the field","The class name is stored","Every object receives the parameter"],"answer":0,"explain":"The parameter shadows the field. this.name is how the lesson selects the field.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"set on Maya's object uses this.age = age with the argument 14. What is Maya's age?","options":["the word age","14","0","Arun's age"],"answer":1,"explain":"The assignment writes the parameter into that object's field.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which English matches this.name = name?","options":["Store the field back into the parameter and ignore the object","Create a new class called name","Store the incoming name in this object's name field","Replace the object with the parameter"],"answer":2,"explain":"Right to left in the lesson's plain English: incoming value, then the field.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The constructor parameter is name, and the body assigns name = name. Maya's field stays unset. What is the repair?","options":["Assign name = this","Remove the parameter","Make name static","Assign this.name = name"],"answer":3,"explain":"this selects the field of the object being initialised.","kind":"debug","code":"Student(String name) {\n    name = name;\n}","fix":"Student(String name) {\n    this.name = name;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Encapsulation: keep the rules close to the data","teach":"A public balance can be set to -5000 from outside. private hides the field. deposit rejects an amount that is not positive. withdraw returns false when the amount is not positive or is greater than the balance. getBalance reads the balance. A public setter that accepts any value does not protect the rule. The opening balance cannot be negative.","questions":[{"q":"Why is balance private?","options":["Code outside the class cannot change it directly and skip the rules","private makes the field static","private deletes the balance","A private field can be assigned from any class"],"answer":0,"explain":"account.balance = -5000 is the risky line the lesson blocks.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the ATM analogy?","options":["The balance is printed by the field name","Callers use the supported actions. They do not open the machine and spin the gears","Every caller may set the balance","private means the account has no methods"],"answer":1,"explain":"deposit, withdraw, and getBalance are the buttons.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does deposit do with an amount that is not positive?","options":["It returns false","It sets the balance to 0","It throws IllegalArgumentException","It adds the amount anyway"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson requires a positive deposit.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When does withdraw return false?","options":["When the amount is positive and affordable","When getBalance is called","Only when the account was constructed","When the amount is not positive, or when it is greater than the balance"],"answer":3,"explain":"A failed withdrawal leaves the balance unchanged and returns false.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is a setter that accepts any balance not enough?","options":["Callers can still store a value that breaks the account rules","Because setters cannot be public","Because getBalance is illegal","Because private fields cannot be read by methods in the same class"],"answer":0,"explain":"Encapsulation is the safe operations, not a getter and setter pair by themselves.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"The account starts at 1000. deposit(250) runs, then withdraw(100) succeeds. What is the balance?","options":["900","1150","1000","1250"],"answer":1,"explain":"1000 + 250 is 1250, and 100 can be withdrawn, leaving 1150.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The balance is 200. withdraw(250) runs. What does it return, and what is the balance?","options":["true, and the balance becomes 0","false, and the balance becomes 250","false, and the balance stays 200","true, and the balance becomes -50"],"answer":2,"explain":"250 is greater than 200, so the method refuses the change.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does getBalance provide?","options":["A way to set any balance","A constructor","Direct access to a public field","Controlled read access"],"answer":3,"explain":"Callers can see the balance without assigning the field.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"new BankAccount(-20) uses the lesson's constructor. What happens?","options":["IllegalArgumentException, because the opening balance cannot be negative","The balance becomes -20","The balance becomes 0","deposit is called automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"The check happens before the field is stored.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"deposit(0) uses the lesson's method. What happens?","options":["The account is deleted","IllegalArgumentException, because the deposit must be positive","The balance stays unchanged and the method returns false","0 is added"],"answer":1,"explain":"An amount that is not positive is rejected before balance += amount.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is the access the public design was trying to prevent?","options":["account.getBalance()","account.withdraw(10) when 10 is available","account.balance = -5000","account.deposit(20)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The field assignment bypasses deposit and withdraw.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"withdraw subtracts the amount before checking it. A request larger than the balance still changes the field. Where must the check go?","options":["After getBalance","Inside main, with the field left public","Only in the constructor","Before the subtraction, returning false when the amount is invalid or too large"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson validates first. The balance changes only when the request is allowed.","kind":"debug","code":"public boolean withdraw(double amount) {\n    balance -= amount;\n    if (amount \u003c= 0 || amount \u003e balance) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}","fix":"public boolean withdraw(double amount) {\n    if (amount \u003c= 0 || amount \u003e balance) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    balance -= amount;\n    return true;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Interactive bank account simulator","teach":"The simulator starts at 1000. deposit and withdraw are the only ways to change the private balance. deposit(250) then a successful withdraw(100) leaves 1150. A negative deposit and an overdraft are rejected. double is only a lesson choice here, not advice for real money.","questions":[{"q":"What does the simulator start with?","options":["A balance of 1000","A balance of 0","A public balance field","An empty class"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Java calls begin with new BankAccount(1000).","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which call is absent from the sample, on purpose?","options":["account.getBalance()","account.balance = ...","account.deposit(250)","account.withdraw(100)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The field is private. Callers use the public operations.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a successful withdraw(100) return after the deposit has made the balance large enough?","options":["100","1150","true","false"],"answer":2,"explain":"The method returns true when it actually subtracts.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What should you conclude from a negative deposit in the simulator?","options":["The balance becomes negative","The account resets to 0","private is removed","The account rejects it and keeps the previous balance"],"answer":3,"explain":"The deposit rule allows only a positive amount.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does this lesson use double for money?","options":["To keep the lesson focused on objects. It is not a recommendation for real financial systems","Because double is required for every field","Because int cannot be a parameter","Because getBalance returns a String"],"answer":0,"explain":"The page says real financial applications need a more careful representation.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Start at 1000, deposit 250, withdraw 100 successfully. What does getBalance print?","options":["900.0","1150.0","1000.0","1250.0"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sample comment shows 1150.0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Start at 1000 and withdraw 1000. What is the balance if the withdrawal succeeds?","options":["-1000","100","0","1000"],"answer":2,"explain":"The amount is equal to the balance, so it is allowed. The balance becomes 0.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Start at 1000 and withdraw 1001. What is the balance?","options":["-1","0","1","1000"],"answer":3,"explain":"The amount is greater than the balance, so withdraw returns false and changes nothing.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Start at 1000, deposit 250, then try to withdraw 1300. What is the balance?","options":["1250","1000","-50","1150"],"answer":0,"explain":"The deposit happens. The later withdrawal is larger than 1250, so it is refused.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which sequence matches the sample calls?","options":["withdraw(100) and then ignore getBalance","new BankAccount(1000), deposit(250), withdraw(100), getBalance()","balance = 1000, balance = 250, balance = 100","deposit before the account exists"],"answer":1,"explain":"The printed balance is the result of those method calls.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Reset puts the simulator back at the start. What balance is that?","options":["1150","250","1000","0"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's starting account is 1000, before the deposit and withdrawal.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The simulator call assigns the private field from main. What can main call instead to add 250?","options":["account.balance += 250","account.getBalance(250)","account.withdraw(-250)","account.deposit(250)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The private field is not assigned from outside. deposit is the operation that adds money.","kind":"debug","code":"account.balance = account.balance + 250;","fix":"account.deposit(250);","debug":true}]},{"title":"8. Instance members vs static","teach":"An instance field belongs to one object. A static field belongs to the class and is shared. Each Player has a name. playerCount is static and increases in the constructor. Two Player objects make Player.playerCount equal 2. Renaming one player does not rename the other.","questions":[{"q":"What does an instance field belong to?","options":["One object","The class, shared by every object","The Java runtime only","Every object at once, as a single copy"],"answer":0,"explain":"name is the example. Pixel and Chutney did not share one name.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a static field belong to?","options":["A local variable","The class, shared rather than copied into each object","Only the first object","The parameter list"],"answer":1,"explain":"The classroom sign is shared. Each desk is not.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the Player constructor do to playerCount?","options":["It resets the count to 0","It creates a separate count for that player","It increases the shared count","It copies the count into the name"],"answer":2,"explain":"playerCount++ runs for each new Player.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How does the sample read the shared count?","options":["p1.playerCount as a separate number from p2.playerCount","p1.name","new Player","Player.playerCount"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson prints the field on the class.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which data should stay an instance field?","options":["Each player's own name","The number of players created","A value that must be the same single copy for the class","The class name"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson uses instance fields for per-object data.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"new Player(\"Maya\") and new Player(\"Arun\") run. What is Player.playerCount?","options":["The name Maya","2","1","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each construction increases the same count.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Those two players exist. What is true of their names?","options":["Both names become 2","The names are static","Maya and Arun are separate instance values","Both names become Maya"],"answer":2,"explain":"name is not static.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"p1 is renamed from Maya to Nila. What is p2's name if p2 was Arun?","options":["Nila","2","Maya","Arun"],"answer":3,"explain":"Changing one object's instance field does not change the other object's field.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"One player is created. What is playerCount?","options":["1","0","2","The player's name"],"answer":0,"explain":"The constructor increments once.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which analogy is the static field?","options":["A local variable inside bark","One classroom room number shared by the students","Each student's own desk","A parameter named name"],"answer":1,"explain":"The desks are the instance data.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is playerCount++ in the constructor, not in main, in this sample?","options":["A constructor cannot change a static field","The count is an instance field","Creating a player is the moment the shared count should increase","main cannot read a static field"],"answer":2,"explain":"The sample puts the increment where every new Player passes.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"playerCount is declared without static, and the code expects one shared total. What does each object have instead?","options":["The class-level count","A copy of every other player's name","No fields","Its own playerCount"],"answer":3,"explain":"The shared total needs the static keyword from the lesson.","kind":"debug","code":"class Player {\n    String name;\n    int playerCount = 0;\n    Player(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n        playerCount++;\n    }\n}","fix":"class Player {\n        String name;\n        static int playerCount = 0;\n        Player(String name) {\n            this.name = name;\n            playerCount++;\n        }\n    }","debug":true}]},{"title":"9. A few habits that make classes easier to use","teach":"Class names use PascalCase, such as BankAccount. Methods and variables use camelCase, such as getBalance. Fields stay private by default. Rules live in the class. Constructors start objects in a valid state. One class has one job. A vending machine exposes buttons, not its motors.","questions":[{"q":"Which name style does the lesson use for a class?","options":["PascalCase, such as BankAccount","camelCase, such as bankAccount","A single lowercase word for every class","The name main"],"answer":0,"explain":"Method and variable names use camelCase.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which name style does the lesson use for a method?","options":["The class name repeated","camelCase, such as getBalance","PascalCase, such as GetBalance","UPPER_CASE"],"answer":1,"explain":"getBalance is the example.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the default advice for fields?","options":["Avoid methods","Store rules in comments only","Keep them private, and expose the operations callers need","Make every field public"],"answer":2,"explain":"Callers should not reach past the object's operations.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where should the negative-balance rule live?","options":["In every caller","In a comment that callers may ignore","In the variable name only","In the account class, inside deposit and withdraw"],"answer":3,"explain":"The class that owns the data owns the rule.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does \"one class, one job\" rule out?","options":["A Student class that also sends email, renders pages, and connects to every database","A Student class with a name and an age","A constructor","A private field"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson asks for the simplest focused class.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which constructor habit matches the lesson?","options":["Skip the constructor when a field has a rule","Use the constructor to check required information and start a valid object","Leave fields unset and hope the caller remembers","Give the constructor a void return type"],"answer":1,"explain":"An invalid opening state should be refused at creation.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair matches the naming examples?","options":["Class bankAccount, method GetBalance","Class BANKACCOUNT, method get_balance","Class BankAccount, method getBalance","Class getBalance, method BankAccount"],"answer":2,"explain":"PascalCase for the class. camelCase for the method.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the vending machine expose?","options":["Every internal gear as a public field","A public balance with no methods","One button that performs every job in the system","Buttons and payment, not the internal motors"],"answer":3,"explain":"Callers use the supported operations.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A balance must not become negative. Which design matches the habit?","options":["deposit and withdraw enforce that rule","A public field and a comment","A setter that accepts any number","A static name"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rule stays with the data.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why keep a class focused?","options":["Constructors cannot exist in a focused class","A class that does every job is harder to understand and change","Java allows only one method","Private fields require several responsibilities"],"answer":1,"explain":"Student data is a different job from sending email.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which field declaration matches the default habit?","options":["static double balance for one account's own money","balance with no class","private double balance","public double balance"],"answer":2,"explain":"Private is the starting choice. The methods reveal what callers may do.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"BankAccount also contains sendEmail and renderPage. Which habit does that break?","options":["PascalCase","Using a constructor","Keeping the balance private","One class should have one focused job"],"answer":3,"explain":"Email and page rendering are separate responsibilities from the account rules.","kind":"debug","code":"class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n    void sendEmail() { }\n    void renderPage() { }\n}","fix":"class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n    public double getBalance() { return balance; }\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"10. Practice problems","teach":"A class is the blueprint. An object is one instance. new Student(\"Maya\", 14) calls the matching constructor. A constructor has no return type. this.name is the current object's field. Balance stays private so rules cannot be skipped. void does not return a value. A static field is shared. A setter that accepts any balance is not encapsulation.","questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between a class and an object?","options":["The class describes the fields and methods. The object is one instance created from that description","They are the same thing","The object is the file and the class is the console","A class is created with new, and an object is created with class"],"answer":0,"explain":"The practice answer uses blueprint and instance.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does a constructor have no return type?","options":["Because fields cannot be assigned in it","It is the initialisation step, not an ordinary method","Because it always returns int","Because it is static"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its name matches the class. void would make a method.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does this.name mean in this.name = name?","options":["The class","A static count","The name field of the current object","The parameter"],"answer":2,"explain":"The bare name on the right is the parameter.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is a bank balance usually private?","options":["So getBalance cannot read it","So the field becomes static","So deposit is unnecessary","So callers cannot change it directly and bypass the rules"],"answer":3,"explain":"Public methods can validate deposits and withdrawals.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is one difference between an instance field and a static field?","options":["Each object has its own instance field. A static field is shared by the class","Static means private","An instance field is shared and a static field is copied","Neither can change"],"answer":0,"explain":"playerCount was the shared example. name was the per-object example.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does new Student(\"Maya\", 14) do?","options":["It calls main","It creates a Student and calls the matching constructor with Maya and 14","It declares a class named Maya","It compares the two arguments"],"answer":1,"explain":"The values are the starting name and age.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Does void turnOn() return a value?","options":["Yes, the colour","Yes, this","No","Yes, true"],"answer":2,"explain":"It can change state. The caller does not receive a result.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two Player objects are created and playerCount is static. What does the shared count report?","options":["0","1","A separate 1 inside each player","2"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each new player increases the same class-level count.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A public setBalance accepts any number, including -5000. Why is that not enough?","options":["Callers can still break the account rule","private blocks the setter from compiling","getBalance becomes illegal","The constructor cannot exist"],"answer":0,"explain":"Encapsulation needs the checks, not only a method shaped like a setter.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header constructs a Student?","options":["static Student()","Student(String name, int age)","void Student(String name, int age)","int Student(String name, int age)"],"answer":1,"explain":"No return type, and the name matches the class.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Maya and Arun are separate objects. Maya's name field changes to Nila. What is Arun's name?","options":["Student","2","Arun","Nila"],"answer":2,"explain":"The name field is instance data.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The method is written void Student(String name) and main expects new Student(\"Maya\") to store Maya. Why does that header fail the constructor rule?","options":["The parameter should be static","new cannot take a String","this is illegal in a constructor","void makes it an ordinary method. A constructor has no return type"],"answer":3,"explain":"Remove void and keep the class name.","kind":"debug","code":"void Student(String name) {\n    this.name = name;\n}","fix":"Student(String name) {\n    this.name = name;\n}","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_inheritance_polymorphism_abstraction_interactive.html","code":"15","name":"Inheritance and polymorphism","sections":[{"title":"1. Why do we need these three ideas?","teach":"Dogs, cats, birds, and fish share some features and specialise others. Inheritance reuses a parent: a Dog is an Animal. Polymorphism uses one common type while each actual object behaves in its own way. Abstraction keeps the shared idea without exposing every internal detail.","questions":[{"q":"What does the lesson mean by inheritance?","options":["A Dog is an Animal and can reuse Animal features","A Dog has an Animal stored as a number","Every class must be static","Inheritance copies console output"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vehicle family shares speed and a registration idea, then specialises how each vehicle behaves.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the lesson mean by polymorphism?","options":["Polymorphism replaces the class keyword","One common request can run different behaviour for each actual object","Every object must print the same text","A variable cannot refer to a child object"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Make every animal speak\" is the lesson's picture.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the lesson mean by abstraction here?","options":["Make every field public","Turn a class into a comment","Depend on the shared idea without needing every internal detail","Hide the program by deleting methods"],"answer":2,"explain":"The shared vehicle ideas matter. Each vehicle can still start and carry passengers differently.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which relationship is the inheritance example?","options":["A Dog has a collar, so Dog extends Collar","An Engine is a Car","A score is a Map","A Dog is an Animal"],"answer":3,"explain":"is-a is the relationship this lesson starts from.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why does the lesson introduce three ideas instead of one class per animal with no sharing?","options":["The animals have common features and also specialised behaviour","Java allows only one class","Fields cannot be shared through any mechanism","println works only in a parent class"],"answer":0,"explain":"The app would otherwise repeat the shared parts.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A car, a bus, and a motorcycle are all vehicles. Which idea names that shared kind?","options":["A file path","Inheritance from a vehicle parent, when the is-a relationship is genuine","A Set of speeds","Replacing every vehicle with one int"],"answer":1,"explain":"They share vehicle ideas and can still specialise.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The program should ask every animal to speak without a separate call style for each class. Which idea is that?","options":["Integer division","A static field","Polymorphism","Writing a new variable type for each call"],"answer":2,"explain":"One request, with the actual object supplying the behaviour.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The delivery screen needs \"deliver\" without showing every routing step. Which idea is that?","options":["Making deliver a public field","Copying the routing code into every button","A constructor with void","Abstraction"],"answer":3,"explain":"The caller depends on the operation, not on every internal step.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which sentence is not the lesson's meaning of inheritance?","options":["A Car is an Engine","A Dog is an Animal","A motorcycle is a vehicle","A child class can specialise a parent"],"answer":0,"explain":"A car has an engine. That is a later design warning, and it is not is-a.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What can differ after the shared vehicle ideas are inherited?","options":["Whether objects can exist","How each vehicle starts, stops, or carries passengers","The fact that a motorcycle is not a vehicle","The spelling of the class keyword"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shared ideas and specialised behaviour are the point of the section.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which trio matches the lesson's three names?","options":["try, catch, and finally","Field, method, and semicolon","Inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction","Set, Map, and Queue"],"answer":2,"explain":"This lesson builds on classes by adding those three ideas.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Dog is written as a complete copy of Animal plus bark, with no extends. What does the lesson use instead of the copy?","options":["class Dog implements Animal","class Animal extends Dog","A static copy of every method","class Dog extends Animal, so Dog reuses Animal and adds its own behaviour"],"answer":3,"explain":"The is-a relationship is declared with extends.","kind":"debug","code":"class Dog {\n    void eat() { System.out.println(\"This animal eats.\"); }\n    void bark() { System.out.println(\"Woof!\"); }\n}","fix":"class Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() { System.out.println(\"Woof!\"); }\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. Inheritance: build on an existing class","teach":"extends makes Dog a child of Animal. Dog inherits eat and can add bark. The parent is the superclass. The child is the subclass. new Dog() can call both eat and bark. The parent is a starter kit. The child keeps those accessible features and adds its own.","questions":[{"q":"Which keyword declares that Dog inherits from Animal?","options":["extends","implements","static","new"],"answer":0,"explain":"implements is for an interface. extends is the class relationship in this section.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"In class Dog extends Animal, which class is the parent?","options":["main","Animal","Dog","bark"],"answer":1,"explain":"Animal is the superclass. Dog is the subclass.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What can a Dog object call if only Animal declares eat and Dog declares bark?","options":["eat only after the method is copied by hand","eat only through a variable that is not a Dog","Both eat and bark","bark only"],"answer":2,"explain":"eat is inherited. bark is defined in Dog.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the starter-kit analogy?","options":["The child deletes the parent methods","The parent becomes the object","extends copies console output","The child receives the parent's accessible features and can add its own"],"answer":3,"explain":"Dog can eat because of Animal, and bark because Dog defines bark.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does inheritance give Dog every private detail of Animal automatically?","options":["The lesson's reuse is the accessible features, such as eat","Yes. private means shared with every child","Yes. Dog can assign Animal's private fields by name","extends makes every field public"],"answer":0,"explain":"The later design section says a private parent field is not directly accessible. This section's example reuses the accessible method.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does dog.eat() print in the sample?","options":["nothing, because eat was not copied into Dog","This animal eats.","Woof!","Dog"],"answer":1,"explain":"eat runs the inherited method.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does dog.bark() print?","options":["bark","Animal","Woof!","This animal eats."],"answer":2,"explain":"bark is Dog's own method.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"How many classes are in the sample relationship?","options":["One","Three, including bark","None until new","Two: Animal and Dog"],"answer":3,"explain":"One parent and one child.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is the inheritance declaration?","options":["class Dog extends Animal","class Dog implements Animal","Dog dog = new Dog();","void eat()"],"answer":0,"explain":"extends names the parent.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A Bicycle is a Vehicle and the shared model fits. What does the practice idea allow?","options":["Bicycle has no relationship to Vehicle","class Bicycle extends Vehicle","class Vehicle extends Bicycle, as the only legal direction","class Bicycle implements Vehicle when Vehicle is a class with fields"],"answer":1,"explain":"The later practice problem calls that a plausible is-a relationship.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does new Dog() give you?","options":["A compile error because Dog added a method","An object that cannot call eat","A Dog object that can use inherited eat and its own bark","The Animal class itself"],"answer":2,"explain":"The child object is the one the sample calls methods on.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Dog declares bark and the call dog.eat() is expected to compile, but Dog does not extend Animal and does not declare eat. What is missing?","options":["implements Animal","a void return type on the class","static on bark","extends Animal"],"answer":3,"explain":"The inherited method is available because of extends.","kind":"debug","code":"class Dog {\n    void bark() { System.out.println(\"Woof!\"); }\n}","fix":"class Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() { System.out.println(\"Woof!\"); }\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Method overriding vs method overloading","teach":"Overriding is a subclass replacing an inherited instance method with the same signature. @Override asks the compiler to catch a misspelled name or a signature that does not match. Overloading is the same name with different parameter lists, usually chosen at compile time. Overriding of an instance method is selected at runtime.","questions":[{"q":"What is overriding?","options":["A subclass supplying its own behaviour for an inherited method with a matching signature","The same name with a different parameter list","Making a field private","Calling main twice"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dog.speak replaces Animal.speak. Both have no parameters in the sample.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is overloading?","options":["extends","The same method name with different parameter lists","A child replacing an inherited method","A static field"],"answer":1,"explain":"Printer.print can take a String, an int, or a String and an int.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does @Override ask the compiler to check?","options":["That the class has no fields","That the method name is main","That the method really overrides an inherited method","That the method is static"],"answer":2,"explain":"A misspelled name or an incompatible signature is reported.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where does the lesson usually place overloading?","options":["Only between a parent and a child","Only on fields","Only in main","Often in the same class"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Printer methods sit together. Overriding needs the subclass relationship.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When is an overridden instance method selected?","options":["At runtime, from the actual object","At compile time, from the parameter list only","By the field names","By the order of the imports"],"answer":0,"explain":"The table says overloading is typically compile time, and overriding of an instance method is runtime.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Animal.speak prints Some animal sound. Dog overrides speak and prints Woof!. Which idea is Dog using?","options":["A constructor","Overriding","Overloading","A new field named speak"],"answer":1,"explain":"The signature matches and the behaviour is specialised.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"print(String) and print(int) are both in Printer. What is that?","options":["Inheritance of a field","A compile error, because the names match","Overloading","Overriding"],"answer":2,"explain":"The parameter lists differ. The compiler chooses from the arguments.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Dog declares speak(int times) while Animal declares speak(). What is that relationship?","options":["An override, because the name is speak","A constructor","A static field","Overloading by a different parameter list, not an override of speak()"],"answer":3,"explain":"An override must match the inherited signature. The int parameter makes a different list.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which annotation belongs above Dog's replacement of speak?","options":["@Override","@Static","@Param","@Extends"],"answer":0,"explain":"It documents the intent and lets the compiler verify it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair matches the memory trick?","options":["Overriding is selected by the parameter count","Overloading is the same name and different inputs. Overriding is a new implementation of the same inherited method","Both words mean a different parameter list","Both words mean a child class field"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson states that trick after the table.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Cat and Dog both answer \"what sound do you make?\" with their own sound. Which idea is the household analogy?","options":["A shared static String that both classes assign once","Integer arithmetic","Overriding","Overloading inside one class"],"answer":2,"explain":"The same kind of request gets specialised behaviour.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"@Override is placed above speek(), but Animal's method is speak(). What does the compiler catch?","options":["A missing semicolon in the class header","That overriding is illegal","That the method must be static","The name does not override anything, because it is misspelled"],"answer":3,"explain":"The annotation exists so this mistake is reported.","kind":"debug","code":"@Override\nvoid speek() {\n    System.out.println(\"Woof!\");\n}","fix":"@Override\nvoid speak() {\n    System.out.println(\"Woof!\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Polymorphism: one request, many behaviours","teach":"A parent variable can refer to a child object. Animal first = new Dog() and Animal second = new Cat() are both Animal variables. first.speak() prints Woof! and second.speak() prints Meow! when those classes override speak. The actual object decides. The variable type tells the compiler which call is allowed.","questions":[{"q":"What can an Animal variable refer to in this section?","options":["A Dog or a Cat object","Only an object created with new Animal(), never a child","A method","An int"],"answer":0,"explain":"The declared type is the parent. The created object is the child.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Animal first = new Dog(), and Dog overrides speak. What prints for first.speak()?","options":["Both messages","Woof!","Some animal sound, because the variable says Animal","Meow!"],"answer":1,"explain":"The actual object is a Dog, so Dog's overridden method runs.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the variable type decide?","options":["The object's fields are erased","The class that new creates, ignoring the constructor call","Which operation the compiler allows","The runtime body of an overridden instance method"],"answer":2,"explain":"The declared type is Animal. The object created by new is still a Dog.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the universal-remote analogy?","options":["Every device plays the same sound","The remote stores the child class's source code","A button works only when the variable and the object have different names","One kind of button runs the device you are actually pointing at"],"answer":3,"explain":"Power on a TV turns on the TV. Power on a speaker turns on the speaker.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does polymorphism in this example require the method to be overridden?","options":["The specialised result comes from the child's override","No. The parent body always runs","The child must overload the method with an extra int","The variable must be static"],"answer":0,"explain":"If Dog did not override speak, the inherited Animal body would run. The sample's Woof! depends on the override.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Animal second = new Cat(), and Cat overrides speak to print Meow!. What prints?","options":["Cat","Meow!","Woof!","Some animal sound"],"answer":1,"explain":"The variable says Animal. The object is a Cat.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair of lines matches the sample?","options":["Animal first = new Animal(); and the comment says Woof!","Cat second = new Dog();","Animal first = new Dog(); and Animal second = new Cat();","Dog first = new Animal();"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both variables use the parent type. new chooses the child.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"first and second are both Animal variables. Why can their speak calls differ?","options":["The variable names choose the method","println is overloaded","The calls run in alphabetical order","The objects are a Dog and a Cat"],"answer":3,"explain":"Runtime selection uses the actual object.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What is printed by the Dog version of speak in the sample?","options":["Woof!","Generic sound","Meow!","Some animal sound, which is Animal's version"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dog's override prints Woof!.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call is legal on an Animal variable when speak is declared on Animal?","options":["first = \"Dog\"","first.speak()","first.bark(), if bark exists only on Dog and the variable type is Animal","first.eat(15) when no such method exists"],"answer":1,"explain":"The compiler looks at the variable type. bark is not part of that Animal call in the sample.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two animals are asked to speak through Animal variables. How many method names does the caller use?","options":["None","One name per field","One, speak","Two different names"],"answer":2,"explain":"The request is common. The behaviour is not.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The variable is Animal, the object is new Dog(), and the programmer expects Animal's \"Some animal sound\" even though Dog overrides speak. What runs?","options":["Animal's speak, because the variable type wins for an override","Both, and Animal wins","Neither, because the types differ","Dog's speak"],"answer":3,"explain":"The override is chosen from the actual object.","kind":"debug","code":"Animal first = new Dog();\nfirst.speak(); // expectation written beside it: Some animal sound","fix":"Animal first = new Dog();\nfirst.speak(); // Woof!","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. Animal sound lab","teach":"The lab is a browser simulation, not a Java compiler. Dog prints Woof!, Cat prints Meow!, and Bird and Cow have their own sounds. One speak action is used for each animal. Make all speak uses that same kind of call for every animal.","questions":[{"q":"What does the animal lab actually run?","options":["A browser simulation of the idea","A Java compiler","A program that saves sounds to a file","A Set of animal names"],"answer":0,"explain":"The page says the playground is not a Java compiler.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What idea is the lab demonstrating?","options":["The lab sorts the animals","The same speak request can produce each object's own sound","Every animal must print the same text","speak is a field"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dog, Cat, Bird, and Cow are separate responses to one kind of call.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the equivalent Dog class print from speak?","options":["Generic sound, which is Animal's version","The class name","Woof!","Meow!"],"answer":2,"explain":"The equivalent Java shows Dog's override.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the equivalent Cat class print from speak?","options":["Woof!","Generic sound","Bird","Meow!"],"answer":3,"explain":"Cat specialises the same method.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does Make all speak do in the lesson's description?","options":["It uses the common speak action for each animal","It prints one shared sentence","It creates a new class","It calls bark on a Cat"],"answer":0,"explain":"The button is the polymorphism demonstration.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"You select Dog and press Speak. Which sound matches the equivalent Java?","options":["Moo","Woof!","Meow!","Generic sound"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dog's override is Woof!.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You select Cat and press Speak. Which sound matches the equivalent Java?","options":["I am a shape.","25","Meow!","Woof!"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cat's override is Meow!.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Animal's own speak, before an override, prints what in the equivalent Java?","options":["Woof!","Meow!","The animal's field count","Generic sound"],"answer":3,"explain":"The parent body is the generic message. The children replace it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which annotation does the equivalent Dog code put on speak?","options":["@Override","@Static","@Lab","@New"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lab's Java design marks the replacement.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Bird and Cow are in the lab as well as Dog and Cat. What does that add to the idea?","options":["Cow replaces the Animal class","More objects can answer the same speak request in their own way","The lab can only hold two classes","Bird must extend Cat"],"answer":1,"explain":"The common call is not limited to one child.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the lab not proof that your computer compiled Java?","options":["Because speak cannot be a method","Because extends is illegal","The page simulates the concept in the browser","Because Woof! is not a String"],"answer":2,"explain":"The equivalent Java is there to read. The buttons are the simulation.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The lab design intends Cat to say Meow!, but Cat does not override speak. What prints?","options":["Meow!","Woof!","A compile error because Cat extends Animal","Generic sound, from Animal"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without the override, the inherited method runs.","kind":"debug","code":"class Cat extends Animal {\n}","fix":"class Cat extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void speak() {\n        System.out.println(\"Meow!\");\n    }\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. Abstract classes: a useful base that is not complete by itself","teach":"Shape is abstract because a plain shape has no single area formula. It can still have a concrete describe method. Circle and Rectangle are concrete children. A circle uses \u03c0 times radius squared. A rectangle uses width times height. new Shape() is not allowed.","questions":[{"q":"Why is Shape abstract?","options":["The shared idea is useful, but a plain Shape has no sensible area formula","So that new Shape() becomes required","abstract means the class cannot have any methods","abstract replaces extends"],"answer":0,"explain":"The worksheet says every shape reports an area, and the formula depends on the shape.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can the program execute new Shape()?","options":["Only from main","No. An abstract class is not instantiated","Yes, and area returns 0","Yes, whenever the class has a constructor"],"answer":1,"explain":"Create a concrete child such as Circle.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What may an abstract class still contain?","options":["Only static fields","A required call to new Shape()","A concrete method, such as describe","No methods at all"],"answer":2,"explain":"describe prints I am a shape. area stays abstract.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What must a concrete Circle supply?","options":["A new class keyword on every call","Animal.speak","A public field named abstract","Its own area method"],"answer":3,"explain":"The child fills in the formula the parent left open.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which formula does the lesson give Circle?","options":["\u03c0 times radius squared","width times height","radius alone","\u03c0 times width"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rectangle formula is the other one.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which formula does the lesson give a rectangle?","options":["radius times height","width times height","\u03c0 times radius squared","width plus height"],"answer":1,"explain":"Circle and rectangle do not share one formula. That is why the parent method is abstract.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does describe print?","options":["Woof!","Shape cannot describe itself","I am a shape.","The area"],"answer":2,"explain":"The concrete method is inherited by the children.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which object can be created?","options":["A Shape","An abstract area method by itself","A class keyword","A Circle"],"answer":3,"explain":"Circle is concrete because it implements area.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Where does Circle store the number the formula needs?","options":["In its radius field, set from the constructor","In a static area shared by every shape","In the method name","In describe"],"answer":0,"explain":"Circle(double radius) stores this.radius.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does abstract double area(); declare?","options":["A constructor","A method the concrete child must implement, with no body in Shape","A method that returns 0","A field"],"answer":1,"explain":"The semicolon, not a body, marks the abstract method in the sample.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the parent still useful if it cannot be constructed?","options":["It replaces the child classes","It makes radius global","It holds the shared expectation and the shared concrete behaviour","It prints every area itself"],"answer":2,"explain":"Children share describe and must each supply area.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"main executes new Shape(). What is the repair?","options":["Remove abstract and leave area without a body","Call describe instead of constructing, while still writing new Shape()","Make Shape a method","Create a concrete subclass, such as a Circle with a radius"],"answer":3,"explain":"An abstract class is the worksheet, not one finished shape.","kind":"debug","code":"Shape shape = new Shape();","fix":"Shape shape = new Circle(3);","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Interfaces: agree on a capability","teach":"An interface is a contract. Payable requires pay. CardPayment and WalletPayment both implement Payable. They do not have to be the same kind of object or share one parent. implements is the keyword. A charging socket is the analogy: the connection is standard, and the device internals can differ.","questions":[{"q":"What is an interface in this lesson?","options":["A contract: operations a class promises to provide","A parent class with fields that every child inherits automatically","A concrete method body that cannot be replaced","Another name for extends"],"answer":0,"explain":"Payable does not say whether the payment is a card or a wallet.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which keyword means a class takes on Payable?","options":["new","implements","extends","static"],"answer":1,"explain":"extends is the class-inheritance keyword. implements is the interface keyword.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What do CardPayment and WalletPayment share?","options":["They share one balance field automatically","They are the same class","They both provide pay","They must extend the same concrete class"],"answer":2,"explain":"The capability is shared. The kind of object is not.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the socket analogy?","options":["One device that is a subclass of every other device","A socket that stores the payment amount","A class that cannot have methods","A standard connection, with different internals behind it"],"answer":3,"explain":"Card and wallet can both fit the contract.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When might an interface be a better fit than an abstract class?","options":["When unrelated classes should promise the same capability without a common parent or shared instance state","When the classes must share private fields","When new should create the interface","When there is only one implementation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The practice problem gives that reason. A parcel locker and a delivery might both be trackable.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which header matches the card class?","options":["class Payable extends CardPayment","class CardPayment implements Payable","class CardPayment extends Payable, when Payable is the interface in the sample","interface CardPayment implements Payable"],"answer":1,"explain":"The class implements the interface. It does not extend it in the sample.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What must WalletPayment provide?","options":["area()","a field named Payable","pay(double amount)","bark()"],"answer":2,"explain":"The contract is the pay operation.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does the card's pay print in the sample?","options":["Paid by wallet: and the amount","Woof!","I am a shape.","Paid by card: and the amount"],"answer":3,"explain":"The wallet class prints the wallet message instead.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Can a card payment and a wallet payment both implement Payable?","options":["Yes. They share the pay capability without being the same kind of object","No. An interface allows only one class","No. One must extend the other","Only if both extend Payment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sample has both classes implement the same contract.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair is the interface and which is the class?","options":["Payable is constructed with new Payable()","Payable is the interface. CardPayment is a class","Both are abstract classes","CardPayment is the interface"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sample creates the card and wallet classes, not the interface.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can the two payment classes print different messages?","options":["The interface stores one message","pay is overloaded by the amount's type only","Each class supplies its own pay body","implements merges the two classes"],"answer":2,"explain":"The contract fixes the operation. The classes fill in the behaviour.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"CardPayment is declared with extends Payable, and Payable is the interface. Which keyword belongs there?","options":["static","new","abstract on the card method only, with extends left in place","implements"],"answer":3,"explain":"A class implements an interface.","kind":"debug","code":"class CardPayment extends Payable {\n    public void pay(double amount) {\n        System.out.println(\"Paid by card: \" + amount);\n    }\n}","fix":"class CardPayment implements Payable {\n    public void pay(double amount) {\n        System.out.println(\"Paid by card: \" + amount);\n    }\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"8. One scenario, three ideas","teach":"BikeDelivery and VanDelivery extend Delivery and share data such as a delivery id. Each overrides estimateTime. BikeDelivery returns 25 and VanDelivery returns 40. Delivery job = new BikeDelivery() prints 25. The declared type makes the call legal. The actual object chooses the body. Trackable is an interface option for things that are not all deliveries.","questions":[{"q":"Which classes extend Delivery?","options":["BikeDelivery and VanDelivery","Trackable and Payable","Dog and Cat","Only BikeDelivery, because a van is unrelated"],"answer":0,"explain":"They share delivery data and specialise the estimate.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does polymorphism mean in this scenario?","options":["estimateTime is chosen by the parameter list","A Delivery reference calls estimateTime, and the actual object supplies the number","The reference type prints 0","Both classes return the same hard-coded number"],"answer":1,"explain":"job is a Delivery. The object is a BikeDelivery.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does abstraction mean for deliver?","options":["abstraction means the method is deleted","The caller must construct the routing class","The app uses a simple operation without knowing every routing step","deliver exposes every internal step as a public field"],"answer":2,"explain":"The shared operation is the part the app depends on.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the interface option in the story?","options":["extends between the locker and the van","A static map of routes","Delivery used as an interface in the sample","Trackable, which a delivery or a parcel locker could implement"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sample itself uses an abstract class for Delivery. Trackable is the extra capability.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the declared type Delivery tell the compiler?","options":["That estimateTime is available","That the object is a van","The number 40","That new is optional"],"answer":0,"explain":"The actual BikeDelivery object still decides the override.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Delivery job = new BikeDelivery(). What does job.estimateTime() return?","options":["The delivery id","25","40","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"BikeDelivery's override returns 25.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What does VanDelivery's estimateTime return?","options":["0","The word van","40","25"],"answer":2,"explain":"The two children specialise the same method.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line shows the polymorphic call?","options":["BikeDelivery job = new Delivery();","int time = 25; with no object","new Trackable()","Delivery job = new BikeDelivery(); job.estimateTime();"],"answer":3,"explain":"The variable is the parent type. new builds the bike delivery.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can a parcel locker be Trackable without extending Delivery?","options":["An interface is a capability, not a claim that the locker is a delivery","extends and implements are the same keyword","A locker is a kind of van","abstract classes cannot have children"],"answer":0,"explain":"The section separates the shared delivery parent from the trackable contract.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What kind of member is estimateTime in Delivery?","options":["A constructor","An abstract method that each concrete delivery implements","A static field","A private int that children assign by name"],"answer":1,"explain":"The parent requires the operation. Bike and van provide the bodies.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two jobs are Delivery variables, one bike and one van. How many method names does the app use for the estimate?","options":["None","One per field in the id","One, estimateTime","Two, one per class name"],"answer":2,"explain":"The common call is the polymorphism.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A van object is stored in a Delivery variable, and the comment expects 25. What does the override return?","options":["25","0","An exception, because the variable type is Delivery","40"],"answer":3,"explain":"The object is a VanDelivery, so its estimate runs.","kind":"debug","code":"Delivery job = new VanDelivery();\nSystem.out.println(job.estimateTime()); // comment expects 25","fix":"Delivery job = new VanDelivery();\nSystem.out.println(job.estimateTime()); // 40","debug":true}]},{"title":"9. Design rules and common mistakes","teach":"Use extends for a genuine is-a relationship. A Dog is an Animal. A Car is not an Engine; it has an Engine, so the engine is a field. Use @Override. Depend on an abstraction when several implementations should fit one parameter. Do not build a deep hierarchy for no reason. A private parent field is not directly accessible in the child.","questions":[{"q":"When does the lesson say to use inheritance?","options":["For a genuine is-a relationship","For every has-a relationship","Whenever two classes share a letter","For a Car and its Engine"],"answer":0,"explain":"A Labrador is a dog. A car is not an engine.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How should \"a car has an engine\" be modelled?","options":["implements Engine automatically","Give Car a field that refers to an Engine","class Car extends Engine","Make Engine an abstract method of Car"],"answer":1,"explain":"That relationship is composition.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does @Override add?","options":["It creates the parent","It changes the parameter list","The compiler checks that an inherited method is really being overridden","It makes the method static"],"answer":2,"explain":"A misspelled override is caught.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why accept an interface type as a parameter?","options":["The interface can be constructed","The parameter becomes a parent field","extends is then unnecessary for every class","The method can work with several implementations of that capability"],"answer":3,"explain":"Payable or Trackable can have more than one implementing class.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the lesson say about a deep hierarchy?","options":["It is harder to change, so use the simplest design that fits","It is required for every program","Each extra level makes fields public","Depth replaces composition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Do not force every difference into inheritance.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A Labrador is a dog. Which declaration fits the analogy?","options":["Labrador has a Dog field and no is-a","class Labrador extends Dog","class Dog extends Labrador, as the only reading of is-a","class Labrador extends Engine"],"answer":1,"explain":"The specific dog is a dog.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Can Dog read Animal's private secret field by name?","options":["Yes, if the field is static","Only from bark","No","Yes, because extends shares private members"],"answer":2,"explain":"Private members stay inside the declaring class. The child uses accessible behaviour instead.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which mistake is Car extends Engine?","options":["Forgetting @Override on a real override","Using an interface for a capability","Keeping one class focused","Using inheritance for has-a"],"answer":3,"explain":"The engine is part of the car, not a parent type of the car.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A method should accept any payment. Which parameter type matches the rule?","options":["Payable","CardPayment only, when wallets must also work","Engine","a private field of Payment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The interface lets card and wallet both be passed.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Two classes differ in a small way that is not is-a. What does the lesson prefer?","options":["Copying every method into both classes with no other choice","The simplest design, often composition or a separate field, rather than another subclass layer","A new abstract parent for every difference","Making both classes static"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deep hierarchies are the thing the section tells you not to force.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which keyword is the wrong one for class Dog and interface Payable?","options":["@Override on Dog's pay if Dog implemented Payable","public on pay","extends","implements"],"answer":2,"explain":"Dog would implement Payable if a dog could pay. A class extends a class.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"class Car extends Engine is used for a car that contains an engine. What is the repair?","options":["class Engine extends Car","Make Engine abstract and still extend it","Implement Car on the engine","Keep an Engine field in Car"],"answer":3,"explain":"has-a is a field. is-a would mean a car is a kind of engine.","kind":"debug","code":"class Car extends Engine {\n}","fix":"class Car {\n    Engine engine;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"10. Practice problems","teach":"Animal is the parent of Dog. A Bicycle can extend Vehicle when the is-a model fits. Overloading changes the parameter list. Overriding replaces an inherited method. Animal a = new Dog() runs Dog's speak. Abstract Shape is not constructed. An interface fits a shared capability without a shared parent. A car has an engine by composition. @Override asks the compiler to verify the override.","questions":[{"q":"In class Dog extends Animal, which is the parent?","options":["Animal","Dog","speak","extends"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dog is the child.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the difference between overloading and overriding?","options":["Both are constructors","Overloading uses different parameter lists. Overriding replaces an inherited method with a matching signature","They are the same rule","Overriding changes the parameter list and overloading does not"],"answer":1,"explain":"The practice answer splits them that way.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Can new Shape() run when Shape is abstract?","options":["Yes, if Shape has a constructor","Only when area is abstract","No. Create a concrete subclass such as Circle","Yes, always"],"answer":2,"explain":"The abstract class is incomplete.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When might an interface beat an abstract class?","options":["When they must share private fields","When only one class will ever exist","When new should create the interface","When classes must share a capability without a common parent or shared instance state"],"answer":3,"explain":"Card and wallet, or a locker and a delivery, can promise the same operations.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A Car has an Engine. Inheritance or composition?","options":["Composition","Inheritance","Both, by writing extends","Neither, because a car cannot have fields"],"answer":0,"explain":"The car is not a kind of engine.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Is \"a Bicycle is a Vehicle\" a plausible extends relationship?","options":["Yes, by writing class Vehicle extends Bicycle as the only form","Yes, when the shared vehicle model fits","No, because a bicycle has wheels","No, because only animals can extend"],"answer":1,"explain":"The practice answer allows Bicycle extends Vehicle.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Animal a = new Dog(), and Dog overrides speak. What runs?","options":["Both, with Animal last","A compile error because the variable says Animal","Dog's speak","Animal's speak"],"answer":2,"explain":"The actual object selects the overridden instance method.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why add @Override?","options":["To change the return type","To make the method private","To create a constructor","So the compiler can verify that the method overrides an inherited one"],"answer":3,"explain":"It also documents the intent.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which keyword declares class inheritance?","options":["extends","implements","package","new"],"answer":0,"explain":"implements declares an interface.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which keyword says a class fulfils an interface?","options":["abstract on the call","implements","extends","static"],"answer":1,"explain":"Payable is implemented, not extended, by CardPayment.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"print(String) and print(int) are which idea?","options":["Composition","An abstract class","Overloading","Overriding"],"answer":2,"explain":"Same name, different parameter lists.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"@Override sits above speak(int times), but the parent method is speak(). What is wrong?","options":["@Override is illegal on Dog","The child must delete speak()","int makes the method static","The parameter list does not match, so this is not an override"],"answer":3,"explain":"Match the inherited signature, or drop the annotation if a new overload was intended.","kind":"debug","code":"@Override\nvoid speak(int times) {\n    System.out.println(\"Woof!\");\n}","fix":"@Override\nvoid speak() {\n    System.out.println(\"Woof!\");\n}","debug":true}]}]},{"file":"java_exception_handling_file_processing_interactive.html","code":"17","name":"Exceptions and files","sections":[{"title":"By the end, you can handle problems without making your program panic.","teach":"Exception handling is a planned response when input or a file is not what the program expected. The labs on this page simulate those ideas in the browser. They do not compile Java and they do not touch the real file system. The code samples are real Java syntax.","questions":[{"q":"What do the interactive labs on this page actually do?","options":["They simulate the ideas in the browser and do not touch the real file system","They compile Java and edit files on your computer","They send each file to a database","They turn every exception into a String variable"],"answer":0,"explain":"The code samples are Java. The buttons are a simulation.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the lesson's picture of exception handling?","options":["Replace files with comments","Notice a problem, choose a safe response, and keep control of the program","Hide every problem with an empty catch","Stop the language from having errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"A bad input or a missing file should have a response.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the lesson's picture of file processing?","options":["Store the file inside a variable name","Skip paths and write to the console only","Find a file, read it, transform the data, and write the result carefully","Delete every file that fails to parse"],"answer":2,"explain":"The rest of the lesson builds those steps.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A user types banana where a number is required. What kind of situation is that?","options":["A syntax error in the class name","Proof that files cannot be read","A reason to remove try and catch","A predictable bad input that the program should be able to answer"],"answer":3,"explain":"The introduction uses that example before any catch is written.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does a simulated Read button open a file on your computer?","options":["No","Yes, in your home directory","Yes, but only .java files","Yes, whenever the sample contains Path"],"answer":0,"explain":"The page states that the labs do not access the real file system.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which sentence can you rely on while using the file labs?","options":["A simulated exception deletes the source file","The numbers you see are a simulation, not a change to your disk","The lab creates marks.txt beside the HTML file","The lab compiles the catch blocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Treat the samples as Java to read, and the buttons as a demonstration.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A network drive is unavailable. Which introduction idea applies?","options":["Java removes the need for a response","The file becomes a String automatically","The program needs a planned response instead of only crashing","The drive should be ignored with no message"],"answer":2,"explain":"The introduction lists that failure next to a moved file.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which pair is the page's two topics?","options":["Sorting and regex","Sets and maps only","Constructors and static fields","Exception handling and file processing"],"answer":3,"explain":"The opening chips name those two.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The sample code uses Java syntax. What is still true of the lab?","options":["Pressing the buttons does not compile that Java","The buttons run javac","The buttons require a JDK on the machine","The simulation writes bytecode"],"answer":0,"explain":"Learning the syntax here does not mean the page compiled it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does the introduction mention a moved file?","options":["Files cannot be represented by a path","Finding and reading a file can fail, so the program needs a response","Every path is relative to the source file","A moved file is a comment"],"answer":1,"explain":"The later sections show how a missing file is reported.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"What should you not expect this page's lab to change?","options":["The wording of a Java sample","Which button you clicked","Files on your computer","The ideas you practise"],"answer":2,"explain":"The simulation stays in the browser.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A comment says the lab saved marks.txt on disk. What did the lab do?","options":["It compiled Java and wrote the file","It appended to every text file","It turned the exception into a path","It only simulated the read. It did not touch the real file system"],"answer":3,"explain":"Keep that limit in mind for every lab on the page.","kind":"debug","code":"// This button writes marks.txt to disk","fix":"// This button simulates a read and does not touch the disk","debug":true}]},{"title":"1. Why do exceptions exist?","teach":"An exception is an alarm: the normal plan cannot continue. A catch is the planned response. It should handle the situation rather than hide every alarm. Some mistakes, such as integer division by zero, are represented by ArithmeticException. Other problems should be prevented with an ordinary check before they happen.","questions":[{"q":"What is the lesson's picture of an exception?","options":["An alarm that the normal plan cannot continue","A comment","A second main method","A file path"],"answer":0,"explain":"The food-delivery story is a missing address, a declined payment, or a closed restaurant.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the lesson's picture of a catch?","options":["A constructor","The planned response for that kind of failure","A way to hide every problem with no response","The place a program must start"],"answer":1,"explain":"The response should help, not swallow every alarm.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does 12 / seats throw when seats is 0?","options":["FileNotFoundException","OutOfMemoryError","ArithmeticException","IOException"],"answer":2,"explain":"The sample marks that integer division as ArithmeticException.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Should every possible mistake be left to an exception?","options":["Yes. if is replaced by catch","Yes. ArithmeticException is a compile error","No, because exceptions are comments","No. Some predictable mistakes should be prevented with an ordinary check"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson separates prevention from failure handling.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What can the program do after a failure is handled?","options":["Continue with the response you wrote","Only exit, because catch stops the runtime","Recompile itself","Ignore the type of the failure"],"answer":0,"explain":"The point of the plan is to keep control.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"The restaurant is closed. Which lesson response fits?","options":["Divide the order by zero","Explain the problem and offer a useful next step","Crash with no message","Hide the failure in an empty catch"],"answer":1,"explain":"The app in the story does not simply crash.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"seats is 0 and the code divides 12 by seats. Which exception matches the comment?","options":["IOException","A missing semicolon","ArithmeticException","NumberFormatException"],"answer":2,"explain":"The arithmetic is legal to write. The failure happens when it runs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A missing address is noticed before the order is sent. What does the lesson prefer when the check is ordinary?","options":["Wait for OutOfMemoryError","Catch FileNotFoundException","Throw a new class for every field","Prevent it with a normal check"],"answer":3,"explain":"Not every problem needs to become an exception first.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is an empty catch a poor emergency plan?","options":["It hides the alarm instead of handling it","It is the only legal catch","It prints the stack by itself","It closes every file"],"answer":0,"explain":"The carpet analogy is the warning.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which word is the signal, and which word is the response?","options":["The signal is a semicolon","The exception is the signal. The catch is the response","The catch is the signal. The exception is a path","Both words create files"],"answer":1,"explain":"You cannot choose a response until you know what failed.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"peoplePerCar = 12 / seats is described as legal code. When is it a problem?","options":["When seats is 12","When the class is named Main","When it runs and seats is 0","When the file is saved"],"answer":2,"explain":"The compiler accepts the division. The runtime reports the illegal arithmetic.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The catch is empty, so a zero divisor is swallowed. What should the catch do?","options":["Delete the try","Assign seats = 12 inside every program","Catch OutOfMemoryError for this arithmetic","Report that division by zero is not allowed"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sample message is the planned response.","kind":"debug","code":"try {\n    int peoplePerCar = 12 / seats;\n} catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n}","fix":"try {\n    int peoplePerCar = 12 / seats;\n} catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"You cannot divide by zero.\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"2. try and catch : attempt and recovery","teach":"Statements in try run in order. A matching exception skips the rest of that try and enters the matching catch. If nothing is thrown, the catch is skipped. After a handled exception, code after the try/catch still runs. 10 / 0 prints the divide-by-zero message and then Program continues.","questions":[{"q":"If the try finishes with no exception, what happens to its catch?","options":["The catch is skipped","The catch still runs","The program stops before every later statement","finally is required before the catch can be skipped"],"answer":0,"explain":"The catch is only the failure path.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"If a matching exception is thrown, what happens to the rest of that try?","options":["The method restarts at main","Those later statements are skipped","They run, then the catch runs","The catch is skipped too"],"answer":1,"explain":"Java looks for the matching catch next.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"After the matching catch finishes, what runs?","options":["Nothing, ever","The same catch a second time","The code after the try/catch","The skipped try statements"],"answer":2,"explain":"Handling the exception returns control to the following statements.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which type matches the 10 / 0 sample?","options":["IOException","NumberFormatException","FileNotFoundException","ArithmeticException"],"answer":3,"explain":"The message in that catch is You cannot divide by zero.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is try for?","options":["The code that might fail","The response","A method signature","A path"],"answer":0,"explain":"catch is the response for one kind of failure.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"10 / 0 is caught. What prints?","options":["The result, and the catch is skipped","You cannot divide by zero. then Program continues.","The result 0, then Program continues.","Only Program continues."],"answer":1,"explain":"The println of the result is skipped. The statement after the try/catch still runs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"parseInt(\"10\") succeeds, the try prints A, the catch would print B, and C prints after the try/catch. What appears?","options":["A then B","C only","A then C","B then C"],"answer":2,"explain":"No exception, so the catch is skipped.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"parseInt(\"cat\") throws, A is after the parse inside the try, B is the catch, and C is after the try/catch. What appears?","options":["A then C","A then B then C","C only","B then C"],"answer":3,"explain":"A is skipped. The catch handles the failure. C still runs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The catch parameter is ArithmeticException, and the failure is a missing file. What happens to that catch?","options":["It does not handle the file failure, because the types do not match","It handles every failure","It turns the file failure into 0","It skips the code after the try/catch even when nothing was caught"],"answer":0,"explain":"A catch handles the type it names, not every problem.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which order matches a successful try that prints Ready and is followed by Done?","options":["Ready, the catch message, and Done","Ready, then Done. The catch does not print","The catch message, then Done","Done only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Success skips the catch and continues.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why is the result println inside the 10 / 0 try not seen?","options":["The catch runs before the division","Program continues replaces every message","The exception happens first, so that later try statement is skipped","println cannot print an int"],"answer":2,"explain":"Order inside the try matters.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The divide-by-zero message is printed, but Program continues is inside the try after the division. Why might that line be missing?","options":["catch cannot print","10 / 0 equals 0","The line after the whole try/catch is the one that was skipped","It is skipped because it sits in the try after the exception"],"answer":3,"explain":"Put the continuation after the try/catch.","kind":"debug","code":"try {\n    int result = 10 / 0;\n    System.out.println(\"Program continues.\");\n} catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"You cannot divide by zero.\");\n}","fix":"try {\n    int result = 10 / 0;\n} catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"You cannot divide by zero.\");\n}\nSystem.out.println(\"Program continues.\");","debug":true}]},{"title":"3. Exception types: not every problem is the same","teach":"Throwable splits into Error and Exception. RuntimeException is unchecked. Other exceptions are often checked. IOException and FileNotFoundException are checked. ArithmeticException, NumberFormatException, and NullPointerException are unchecked. OutOfMemoryError is an Error. A checked exception must be caught or declared. Do not catch Exception just to hide failures.","questions":[{"q":"Which exception is checked?","options":["IOException","NullPointerException","ArithmeticException","OutOfMemoryError"],"answer":0,"explain":"A checked exception must be caught or declared with throws.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which exception is unchecked?","options":["A missing semicolon, which is a compile error","NullPointerException","IOException","FileNotFoundException"],"answer":1,"explain":"NullPointerException extends RuntimeException. The compiler does not force a catch.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is FileNotFoundException in the lesson's table?","options":["An unchecked arithmetic failure","A String","A checked exception: the file cannot be found or opened","An Error"],"answer":2,"explain":"It is listed beside IOException in the checked column.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is OutOfMemoryError?","options":["A checked exception","An unchecked NumberFormatException","A catch parameter that every program must use","An Error: a serious condition outside normal application recovery"],"answer":3,"explain":"Error is the other branch under Throwable.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the school-permission analogy?","options":["A checked exception is a slip you must submit or account for. An unchecked exception can still happen without that form","Every exception is an Error","The compiler ignores checked exceptions","A permission slip is a file path"],"answer":0,"explain":"Error is compared to a major building problem, not a routine classroom mistake.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"A string cannot be parsed as the requested number. Which row is that?","options":["OutOfMemoryError","NumberFormatException, unchecked","IOException, checked","FileNotFoundException, checked"],"answer":1,"explain":"parseInt(\"cat\") is this kind of failure.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Integer division by zero matches which row?","options":["FileNotFoundException, checked","Error","ArithmeticException, unchecked","IOException, checked"],"answer":2,"explain":"The table's arithmetic example is divide by zero.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Code uses a null reference as an object. Which row is that?","options":["IOException, checked","FileNotFoundException, checked","A compile error because null is illegal to write","NullPointerException, unchecked"],"answer":3,"explain":"The table describes that as using null as an object.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A read or write fails. Which row is that?","options":["IOException, checked","ArithmeticException, unchecked","NumberFormatException, unchecked","OutOfMemoryError"],"answer":0,"explain":"The compiler will require a catch or a throws for that checked exception.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does the lesson warn against catch (Exception e) everywhere?","options":["It converts checked exceptions into comments","It can hide failures you do not meaningfully handle","Exception is illegal syntax","It is required for ArithmeticException"],"answer":1,"explain":"Catch the types you can handle, and keep enough information to diagnose the rest.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which hierarchy order matches the lesson?","options":["Error extends NumberFormatException","RuntimeException is checked","Throwable, then Error and Exception. RuntimeException sits under Exception","Exception, then Throwable"],"answer":2,"explain":"You do not need every class. You need these branches.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"openFile throws IOException and the method neither catches it nor declares it. What is the compiler enforcing?","options":["IOException is an Error","A missing file is ArithmeticException","Unchecked exceptions must be declared","IOException is checked, so it must be caught or declared"],"answer":3,"explain":"The permission-slip rule is the check.","kind":"debug","code":"String text = Files.readString(path);","fix":"String text;\ntry {\n    text = Files.readString(path);\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Reading failed: \" + e.getMessage());\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"4. Control flow: what runs, and what gets skipped?","teach":"parseInt(\"oops\") skips the rest of its try. The trace prints A, B, D, and E. C is skipped. FileNotFoundException is more specific than IOException, so it must be caught first. The reverse order is rejected because the specific catch is already covered.","questions":[{"q":"Once an exception happens inside try, what happens to the later statements in that same try?","options":["They are skipped","They run after the catch","They choose the catch type","They always print C"],"answer":0,"explain":"Java then searches for a compatible handler.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the oops trace print?","options":["D only","A, B, D, and E","A, B, C, D, and E","C and E"],"answer":1,"explain":"C is the println after parseInt, so it does not run.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Why is C skipped?","options":["E throws","A is the exception","parseInt throws before that line","C is in the catch"],"answer":2,"explain":"B prints. The parse fails. D handles it. E is outside.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which catch must come first when you handle both FileNotFoundException and IOException?","options":["IOException","Exception, and then the specific type","ArithmeticException","FileNotFoundException"],"answer":3,"explain":"The specific type comes before the broader parent.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is wrong with catching IOException before FileNotFoundException?","options":["The specific catch is already covered, so that order is rejected","FileNotFoundException is not an exception","The broader type must be first","try cannot be used with files"],"answer":0,"explain":"FileNotFoundException is a kind of IOException.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"parseInt(\"cat\") throws, A would print after it inside the try, the catch prints B, and C prints after the try/catch. What appears?","options":["C then B","B then C","A then B then C","A then C"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same skipping rule as the oops trace. A is the skipped line.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The oops trace reaches E. What does that show?","options":["The program restarted","C printed as well","A handled exception does not cancel the code after the try/catch","E is inside the catch"],"answer":2,"explain":"E is the statement after the handler.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is B in the trace?","options":["A: before","C: value","E: after handler","B: start try"],"answer":3,"explain":"B is inside the try, before the parse.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line is D?","options":["D: invalid number","C: value","A: before","E: after handler"],"answer":0,"explain":"D is the catch for NumberFormatException.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A file is missing and the first catch is FileNotFoundException. Which handler can run for that failure?","options":["ArithmeticException","The FileNotFoundException catch","Only a later IOException catch, because the first one is too specific","The catch is skipped because the file is missing"],"answer":1,"explain":"The specific handler is reachable only when it stands before the broader one.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The broader IOException catch is still useful after the specific one. What does it handle?","options":["Every NumberFormatException","OutOfMemoryError","Other input/output problems that are not the missing-file case","The missing file, before the specific catch"],"answer":2,"explain":"The sample's second catch is the other I/O problem.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"IOException is caught before FileNotFoundException. What must change?","options":["Delete both catches","Catch Error first","Move E inside the try","Put FileNotFoundException first"],"answer":3,"explain":"The later specific catch would be unreachable.","kind":"debug","code":"try {\n    // read a file\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"I/O\");\n} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Missing\");\n}","fix":"try {\n    // read a file\n} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Missing\");\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"I/O\");\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"5. throw vs throws","teach":"throw raises an exception now. throws on a method signature warns that a checked exception may leave the method. age \u003c 0 throws IllegalArgumentException. loadText declares throws IOException when it lets the caller handle Files.readString. A custom exception can extend Exception, but a standard type is enough when it already describes the problem.","questions":[{"q":"What does throw do?","options":["It raises an exception at that moment","It declares a method","It catches an exception","It closes a file"],"answer":0,"explain":"The flag goes up where the invalid situation is detected.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does throws do?","options":["It is required on ArithmeticException","It declares that a checked exception may leave the method","It raises an exception on every call, even when the input is valid","It catches IOException"],"answer":1,"explain":"The warning is on the method's door, for the caller.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which keyword belongs on loadText when the caller will handle the checked I/O failure?","options":["catch IOException","extends IOException","throws IOException","throw IOException"],"answer":2,"explain":"The scenario in the lesson chooses throws.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does the age check throw?","options":["IOException","FileNotFoundException","OutOfMemoryError","IllegalArgumentException"],"answer":3,"explain":"The message is Age cannot be negative.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When should you invent a new exception class?","options":["When no standard type describes the domain problem. Otherwise start with a standard type","For every if","Instead of throws","For every null"],"answer":0,"explain":"InvalidOrderException is shown as a later option, not the first habit.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"age is -1 and the lesson's check runs. What happens?","options":["IOException is caught","throw new IllegalArgumentException with the age message","The method declares throws and continues","age is stored as 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"throw is inside the method, at the moment of the bad value.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"loadText returns Files.readString(path) and does not catch. What does the signature include?","options":["catch (IOException e)","throws ArithmeticException","throws IOException","throw IOException"],"answer":2,"explain":"readString's checked failure is passed to the caller.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which line actually raises an exception?","options":["void loadText(Path path) throws IOException","catch (IOException e)","extends Exception","throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Age cannot be negative\");"],"answer":3,"explain":"throws only declares. throw creates the object and raises it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A method catches IOException itself. What is then unnecessary for that handled failure?","options":["Declaring throws IOException for a failure that does not escape","The try","A message","The method name"],"answer":0,"explain":"throws is for the failure that leaves the method. A catch that handles it is the other choice.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"InvalidOrderException extends Exception and calls super(message). What is that?","options":["An unchecked RuntimeException in the sample","A custom checked exception, for when a standard type is not clear enough","An Error","A replacement for throw"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sample extends Exception and passes the message to the parent.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which English matches the analogy?","options":["Both words catch","Both words close a reader","throw raises the flag now. throws posts the warning on the method","throws raises the flag now. throw posts the warning"],"answer":2,"explain":"The words look similar and do different jobs.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The method writes throw IOException in the signature. What is the declaration keyword?","options":["catch","extends","import","throws"],"answer":3,"explain":"throw is a statement. throws belongs on the signature.","kind":"debug","code":"static String loadText(Path path) throw IOException {\n    return Files.readString(path);\n}","fix":"static String loadText(Path path) throws IOException {\n    return Files.readString(path);\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"6. finally and try-with-resources","teach":"finally normally runs whether the try succeeds or a handled exception occurs. An abrupt JVM exit can prevent it. try-with-resources closes an AutoCloseable, including a BufferedReader, when the statement finishes, including when an exception occurs. That is safer than remembering to close in finally.","questions":[{"q":"When does finally normally run?","options":["After success and after a handled exception","Only after success","Only when the JVM exits","Never, if a catch exists"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sample's cleanup step is that block.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"When might finally not run?","options":["If the resource implements AutoCloseable","If the JVM terminates abruptly","Whenever a catch exists","Whenever the try prints"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson states that limit.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does try-with-resources close?","options":["Only a List","The catch parameter","An AutoCloseable resource declared in the try parentheses","Every variable in the method"],"answer":2,"explain":"BufferedReader is the sample.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does that close still happen if reading throws?","options":["No. An exception skips the close","Only if finally is also written","Only when the file is empty","Yes. The resource is closed when the statement finishes, including when an exception occurs"],"answer":3,"explain":"That is the reason the lesson prefers it to a manual close.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the library-book analogy?","options":["Return the book even if the study session is interrupted","Keep the book when an exception occurs","finally deletes the book","A resource must stay open"],"answer":0,"explain":"The automatic return is try-with-resources.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"The try prints Do the work, the catch does not run, and finally prints Cleanup step. What appears?","options":["Do the work, and finally is skipped because nothing failed","Do the work, then Cleanup step","Cleanup step only","The catch message, then Cleanup step"],"answer":1,"explain":"Success still runs finally.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The try throws a handled IOException, the catch prints Handle the I/O problem, and finally prints Cleanup step. What appears?","options":["Do the work, and no cleanup","The catch only","The catch message, then Cleanup step","Cleanup step, then the catch"],"answer":2,"explain":"A handled exception does not skip finally.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which header matches the sample?","options":["try (reader.close())","finally (BufferedReader reader)","catch (BufferedReader reader)","try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(path, StandardCharsets.UTF_8))"],"answer":3,"explain":"The resource is created in the parentheses.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why does the lesson prefer this to closing in finally yourself?","options":["The close is automatic, including on failure","finally is illegal","A reader closes when the variable is named","AutoCloseable cannot be closed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Forgetting the manual close is the leak this avoids.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The loop condition in the sample is while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null). What does a null line mean there?","options":["finally should be skipped","There are no more lines","The file is missing","The reader must be constructed again"],"answer":1,"explain":"The read ends when readLine returns null.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which resources does the section say should be closed?","options":["A Comparator","A HashSet","Files, readers, network connections, and database connections","Every int"],"answer":2,"explain":"Those are the examples that motivate finally and try-with-resources.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The reader is closed only on the success path, after the loop, so a thrown IOException can skip the close. What structure closes it either way?","options":["A second unclosed reader","catch without a close","Removing AutoCloseable","try-with-resources"],"answer":3,"explain":"Declare the reader in the try parentheses.","kind":"debug","code":"BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(path, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\nString line;\nwhile ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {\n    System.out.println(line);\n}\nreader.close();","fix":"try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(path, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {\n    String line;\n    while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {\n        System.out.println(line);\n    }\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Could not read the file: \" + e.getMessage());\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"7. Files and paths: where is the data?","teach":"A file outlives one run. A path is the address, not the contents. data/marks.txt is resolved from the current working directory, not from the source file's folder. /home/student/data/marks.txt is an absolute path. A Windows Java string can look like C:\\\\school\\\\marks.txt. Path and Files live in java.nio.file.","questions":[{"q":"What is a path?","options":["The address Java uses to locate a file","The contents of the file","A catch block","An exception type"],"answer":0,"explain":"Path is the location. Files provides the operations.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Where is the relative path data/marks.txt resolved from?","options":["The path of the HTML lesson","The program's current working directory","The folder that contains the .java source, automatically","The user's home directory, always"],"answer":1,"explain":"A relative path is not automatically beside the source file.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is /home/student/data/marks.txt in the lesson?","options":["A Windows path","The file contents","An absolute path from the root of the file system","A relative path"],"answer":2,"explain":"It names the full location.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which package holds Path and Files?","options":["java.util","java.lang only, with no import","java.io.Scanner","java.nio.file"],"answer":3,"explain":"The modern file API in this lesson is java.nio.file.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does Files provide, as opposed to Path?","options":["Operations such as reading, writing, checking existence, and copying","Only the address","Exception types","The working directory itself"],"answer":0,"explain":"Path represents the location.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"The program's working directory is /home/student and the relative path is data/marks.txt. Which location is that?","options":["C:\\\\school\\\\marks.txt","/home/student/data/marks.txt","The directory of Main.java, whatever that is","/data/marks.txt only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The relative path hangs off the working directory.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"You move Main.java to another folder and do not change the working directory. What happens to data/marks.txt?","options":["It becomes absolute automatically","It throws OutOfMemoryError","It still resolves from the working directory","It follows the source file"],"answer":2,"explain":"The source file's new folder is not the rule.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which Java string is the lesson's Windows example?","options":["/home/student/data/marks.txt","data/marks.txt","school/marks.txt with no drive","C:\\\\school\\\\marks.txt"],"answer":3,"explain":"Windows paths look different. The Java source escapes each backslash.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which import matches Path?","options":["import java.nio.file.Path;","import java.util.Path;","import java.io.Path;","import java.nio.file.Scanner;"],"answer":0,"explain":"Files has its own import from the same package.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Path file = Path.of(\"notes.txt\") creates what?","options":["A checked exception","A path value","The text that is inside the file","An already-open reader"],"answer":1,"explain":"Building a path does not read the contents. Reading is a later Files operation.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Why can the same relative path open a different file for two runs?","options":["Path.of sorts directories","Files deletes the working directory","The two runs can have different working directories","Relative paths include the source folder in the string"],"answer":2,"explain":"The address is completed from where the program was started.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The comment says data/marks.txt is beside Main.java, but the program was started in another directory. Where does Java look?","options":["Always beside the source file","In the JDK folder","At an absolute path built from the class name","In the current working directory"],"answer":3,"explain":"Put the file in the working directory, or build an absolute path on purpose.","kind":"debug","code":"Path marks = Path.of(\"data/marks.txt\"); // assumed to mean \"beside Main.java\"","fix":"Path marks = Path.of(\"data/marks.txt\"); // resolved from the current working directory","debug":true}]},{"title":"8. Read and write files with Java","teach":"Files.readString reads a small file into one String and is Java 11+. Large files should be read line by line. writeString creates a file if needed and replaces existing content unless you pass CREATE and APPEND. APPEND does not add a newline unless that newline is in the text. Handle IOException. Choose UTF-8 explicitly.","questions":[{"q":"Which call reads a small text file into one String?","options":["Files.readString","Files.writeString","Path.of by itself","reader.close"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is the Java 11+ convenience method. Handle IOException.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the default effect of writeString on a file that already exists?","options":["It deletes the parent directory","It replaces the existing content","It appends","It refuses to write"],"answer":1,"explain":"The file is created if it does not exist.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How do you append instead?","options":["Use readString","Catch ArithmeticException","Pass StandardOpenOption.CREATE and StandardOpenOption.APPEND","Call writeString with no options, a second time"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's log example uses both options.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Does APPEND insert an extra newline for you?","options":["Yes, after every write","Yes, before every write","Only on Windows","No. A newline appears only if it is in the text you write"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sample text includes the newline character it wants.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How should a large file be processed?","options":["Line by line, with a buffered reader, rather than loading it all into one String","Always with readString","With Path.of only","By catching OutOfMemoryError and continuing"],"answer":0,"explain":"readString is the small-file tool.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"notes.txt contains Hello. What does readString return?","options":["null, because the file exists","The text Hello, as one String","A Path","The number of lines only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sample then prints that content.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"summary.txt already contains Done. writeString is called with Java practice completed and no APPEND option. What does the file then contain?","options":["Done","An empty file, because writeString cannot create text","Java practice completed","Done and then Java practice completed"],"answer":2,"explain":"The default is replacement. The sample's own text also includes a newline character in the source.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"log.txt contains Old. writeString appends New event with CREATE and APPEND, and the text has no newline. What is in the file?","options":["New event","Old, a newline, and New event, even though the text had none","Old only","OldNew event"],"answer":3,"explain":"Append adds the characters you pass. It does not invent a newline.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which charset does the lesson tell you to pass?","options":["StandardCharsets.UTF_8","The computer's default, with no argument, as the recommended choice","UTF-16 only","A path used as the charset"],"answer":0,"explain":"Explicit UTF-8 keeps the text stable across machines.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which call builds the path used by the read sample?","options":["throw notes.txt","Path.of(\"notes.txt\")","new FileReader()","Files.readString with no path"],"answer":1,"explain":"The path is the address. readString uses it.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Reading can fail. Which checked exception does the sample catch?","options":["NullPointerException","OutOfMemoryError","IOException","ArithmeticException"],"answer":2,"explain":"The message starts with Reading failed:.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A second writeString is meant to add a line to log.txt, but the options are omitted. What happens to the old text?","options":["It is kept, and the new text is appended","It is sorted","The call appends because writeString always appends","It is replaced"],"answer":3,"explain":"Add CREATE and APPEND for the log.","kind":"debug","code":"Files.writeString(Path.of(\"log.txt\"), \"New event\\n\", StandardCharsets.UTF_8);","fix":"Files.writeString(Path.of(\"log.txt\"), \"New event\\n\", StandardCharsets.UTF_8, StandardOpenOption.CREATE, StandardOpenOption.APPEND);","debug":true}]},{"title":"9. File safety lab: simulate a read, process, and write","teach":"The lab pretends to read marks, skips or reports text that is not a number, and can compute an average. The sample lines are 78, 91, hello, 64, and 85. It does not open a real file. A real solution needs a Path, a reader or readAllLines for a small file, an IOException handler, and a policy for invalid lines: stop, skip, or report.","questions":[{"q":"What does this lab touch?","options":["Nothing on disk. It simulates the lines in the browser","marks.txt in the working directory","Every .txt file","The Java compiler"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sample contents are part of the page.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which line in the sample is not a mark?","options":["85","hello","78","91"],"answer":1,"explain":"The number parser cannot treat hello as an int.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"How many of the sample lines are valid marks?","options":["3","1","4","5"],"answer":2,"explain":"78, 91, 64, and 85 are the numbers. hello is the invalid line.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What must a real solution decide about an invalid line?","options":["To delete the file always","To treat hello as 0 with no stated choice","To ignore IOException","A policy: stop, skip, or report it"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists those three policies.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What does a small-file Java solution need before the average?","options":["A Path, a reader or readAllLines, an IOException handler, and validation","Only println","A Set of lines","An unchecked divide by zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Text may not be a number, so each line is checked.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Using the sample, which values can be added?","options":["64 and 85 only","78, 91, 64, and 85","78, 91, hello, 64, and 85","hello only"],"answer":1,"explain":"hello is reported or skipped. It is not a mark.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"If the policy skips invalid lines, what happens to hello?","options":["It becomes 91","The file is deleted","It is not included in the numeric result, and the other lines can still be used","The program must crash"],"answer":2,"explain":"Skip is one of the three stated policies.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"If the policy stops, what is true of the lines after hello?","options":["They are averaged first","They replace hello with 0","They are written before the read","They are not processed"],"answer":3,"explain":"Stop means the failure ends the scan.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The clean example has no hello. What does the lab then lack?","options":["An invalid line to report","Any numbers","A Path in the real solution","The average idea"],"answer":0,"explain":"The clean button is the contrast to the mixed file.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"An empty simulated file has no marks. What should the average logic avoid?","options":["Reporting the empty result","Dividing by a count of 0","Reading a Path","Catching IOException"],"answer":1,"explain":"No valid lines means there is no average to divide out.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Which API does the lesson name for reading every line of a small file?","options":["Path.of as the parser","Collections.sort","Files.readAllLines, or a reader","Files.writeString as the read"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each line is then validated.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"Integer.parseInt is called on hello and there is no handler or policy. What does the lesson require before a number is trusted?","options":["A second parse of the same word","writeString","A HashSet of marks","Validation, plus a stop, skip, or report policy"],"answer":3,"explain":"hello is text. It is not a mark.","kind":"debug","code":"int mark = Integer.parseInt(line);","fix":"int mark;\ntry {\n    mark = Integer.parseInt(line);\n} catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Invalid line: \" + line);\n    continue;\n}","debug":true}]},{"title":"10. Practice problems","teach":"parseInt(\"cat\") prints B and then C. IOException is checked. NullPointerException is unchecked. throws declares a checked exception on the signature. FileNotFoundException is caught before IOException. A BufferedReader should be opened in try-with-resources. readString reads a small file into one String. APPEND is an open option, and it does not add a newline by itself.","questions":[{"q":"What prints? parseInt(\"cat\"), then A inside the try, B in the catch, C after the try/catch.","options":["B then C","A then C","A then B then C","C then A"],"answer":0,"explain":"The parse throws before A. The catch prints B. C is outside and runs.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Classify IOException and NullPointerException.","options":["IOException is unchecked and NullPointerException is checked","IOException is checked. NullPointerException is unchecked","Both are checked","Both are Errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"NullPointerException extends RuntimeException.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which keyword declares that a method may pass IOException to its caller?","options":["catch","finally","throws","throw"],"answer":2,"explain":"throw raises one exception. throws sits on the signature.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"FileNotFoundException and IOException both need handlers. Which comes first?","options":["IOException","Exception","Error","FileNotFoundException"],"answer":3,"explain":"The more specific type comes first, or the later catch is already covered.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"What is the recommended way to close a BufferedReader?","options":["try-with-resources","Close it only after a successful loop, on the next line","Leave it open","Catch OutOfMemoryError"],"answer":0,"explain":"The reader is declared in the try parentheses and closed when the statement finishes.","kind":"theory"},{"q":"Which call reads a small text file into one String on Java 11+?","options":["new ArrayList\u003c\u003e()","Files.readString(path, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)","Path.of(path)","Files.writeString"],"answer":1,"explain":"Handle the checked IOException around that read.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"writeString should add to log.txt instead of replacing it. What do you pass?","options":["StandardOpenOption.READ","A second Path only","StandardOpenOption.CREATE and StandardOpenOption.APPEND","No options"],"answer":2,"explain":"The default write replaces. The options change that.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The appended text is New event with no newline character. What does APPEND add?","options":["Those characters plus a newline","A blank line before them","Nothing, because APPEND replaces","Those characters only"],"answer":3,"explain":"The newline has to be in the text if you want one.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"data/marks.txt is relative. What completes that address?","options":["The current working directory","The folder of the source file, automatically","The catch block","UTF-8"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is not automatically beside the .java file.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"10 / 0 is caught as ArithmeticException and Program continues is after the try/catch. What prints?","options":["Only the catch, and the later line is skipped","You cannot divide by zero. then Program continues.","Program continues. then the result","Only the result"],"answer":1,"explain":"The handled failure does not skip the following statement.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"A method calls a method on a null reference. Which type matches the lesson's table?","options":["FileNotFoundException","OutOfMemoryError","NullPointerException","IOException"],"answer":2,"explain":"The table's unchecked null row is using a null reference as an object.","kind":"practice"},{"q":"The second catch is FileNotFoundException and the first is IOException. Why does this fail to compile?","options":["FileNotFoundException is unchecked and cannot be caught","A file catch must be last","throws belongs between the two catches","The specific exception is already covered by the earlier catch"],"answer":3,"explain":"Swap them so the missing-file handler comes first.","kind":"debug","code":"try {\n    Files.readString(path);\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"I/O\");\n} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Missing\");\n}","fix":"try {\n    Files.readString(path);\n} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Missing\");\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"I/O\");\n}","debug":true}]}]}]};
